window.LEARNEW_MENU = [
  { label: 'Learnings', all: 'learnings.html', items: [
    { title: 'RAG', tag: 'LLMs', blurb: 'Retrieval-Augmented Generation: give a language model the right documents at question time so its answers are grounded in your data.', desc: 'Retrieval, augmentation, generation',
      href: 'topic.html?g=Learnings&t=rag',
      sections: [
      { id: "what-is-rag", title: "What is RAG?", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> RAG lets an LLM look things up in your own data before it answers.</p><div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation) is a technique that allows an LLM (Large Language Model) to retrieve relevant information from external data sources and use that information to generate accurate, context-aware answers.</div><p>In simple words:</p><pre>RAG = Search your own data + Give relevant information to an LLM + Generate an answer</pre><h3>The three parts of the name</h3><ul><li><b>Retrieval</b>: search your own data and find the relevant information</li><li><b>Augmented</b>: give that information to the LLM along with the question</li><li><b>Generation</b>: the LLM writes an answer from it</li></ul>" },
      { id: "why-rag", title: "Why do we need RAG?", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> an LLM does not know your private data, and RAG gives it the right pieces of it.</p><p>Imagine you have a company with 1,000 PDF documents containing:</p><ul><li>Company policies</li><li>Employee information</li><li>Technical documentation</li><li>Product details</li><li>Financial reports</li></ul><p>You ask ChatGPT:</p><pre>\"What is our company's leave policy?\"</pre><p>A normal LLM might not know your company's internal leave policy, because that information wasn't part of its training data.</p><div class=\"callout\"><b>The fix</b>RAG solves this problem by retrieving the relevant information from your documents and providing it to the LLM.</div>" },
      { id: "data-sources", title: "Data sources and data collection", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> A data source is any place your RAG app collects knowledge from, and this phase is about getting that data in.</p><p>The first step in learning RAG is understanding how data enters a RAG system. Before learning embeddings, vector databases or retrieval, you should understand how to collect data from different sources and convert it into a format an AI application can process.</p><h3>What is a data source?</h3><p>A data source is any location or system from which we collect information.</p><p>In a RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation) application, data sources provide the knowledge that the AI model uses to answer user questions.</p><p>For example, imagine building a company knowledge assistant. You might have:</p><ul><li>Company policies in PDF files.</li><li>Employee information in Excel files.</li><li>Technical documentation in Markdown files.</li><li>Customer information in a database.</li><li>Product information available through APIs.</li><li>Knowledge articles on a website.</li></ul><p>All these locations are data sources.</p><h3>Why do we need data sources in RAG?</h3><p>An LLM has knowledge acquired during its training, but it does not automatically know your private documents, internal company information, or newly updated business data.</p><p>RAG solves this by retrieving relevant information from external knowledge sources and providing it to the LLM as context.</p><p>Example user question: \"What is our company's annual leave policy?\"</p><p>RAG workflow:</p><ol><li>Collect company policy documents.</li><li>Extract text from the documents.</li><li>Prepare the extracted content.</li><li>Convert the content into chunks.</li><li>Generate embeddings.</li><li>Store embeddings in a vector database.</li><li>Search for relevant information when a user asks a question.</li><li>Pass the retrieved information to the LLM.</li><li>Generate an answer.</li></ol><h3>Common data sources</h3><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Data source</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>PDF</td><td>Company policies</td></tr><tr><td>DOCX</td><td>Business documents</td></tr><tr><td>TXT</td><td>Plain text files</td></tr><tr><td>Markdown</td><td>Technical documentation</td></tr><tr><td>CSV</td><td>Customer records</td></tr><tr><td>Excel</td><td>Sales reports</td></tr><tr><td>JSON</td><td>Application data</td></tr><tr><td>HTML</td><td>Web pages</td></tr><tr><td>Database</td><td>PostgreSQL, MySQL</td></tr><tr><td>API</td><td>External services</td></tr><tr><td>Website</td><td>Documentation portals</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Real-world example</h3><p>Consider building a RAG application for an e-commerce company. The company has:</p><ul><li>Product descriptions in CSV.</li><li>Product manuals in PDF.</li><li>Customer FAQs in DOCX.</li><li>Order information in PostgreSQL.</li><li>Product details through an external API.</li></ul><p>Your RAG application must collect information from these sources and prepare it for retrieval.</p><h3>Important concepts</h3><ul><li><b>Data ingestion</b>: Bringing data into a RAG system.</li><li><b>Data extraction</b>: Extracting useful information from a source.</li><li><b>Data loading</b>: Loading extracted information into the next processing stage.</li><li><b>Data pipeline</b>: A sequence of operations that processes data.</li><li><b>Data connector</b>: A component that connects to a data source.</li><li><b>Data synchronization</b>: Keeping collected data updated when the original source changes.</li></ul>" },
      { id: "structured", title: "Structured vs unstructured data", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Different kinds of data need different extraction methods, so first know what kind you have.</p><p>Before extracting information, understand the types of data you are working with.</p><h3>Structured data</h3><p>Structured data is information organized according to a predefined schema, usually in rows and columns.</p><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>ID</th><th>Name</th><th>Department</th><th>Salary</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>101</td><td>Ravi</td><td>Engineering</td><td>50000</td></tr><tr><td>102</td><td>Priya</td><td>HR</td><td>45000</td></tr><tr><td>103</td><td>Kumar</td><td>Finance</td><td>55000</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Characteristics:</p><ul><li>Organized format.</li><li>Predefined columns.</li><li>Consistent data types.</li><li>Easy to filter and query.</li><li>Usually stored in relational databases or spreadsheets.</li></ul><p>Examples: SQL databases, CSV files, Excel spreadsheets, tabular datasets.</p><h3>Unstructured data</h3><p>Unstructured data does not follow a fixed tabular schema.</p><p>Example: \"Employees are eligible for 20 days of annual leave. Employees must obtain approval from their respective managers before taking leave.\"</p><p>Characteristics:</p><ul><li>No fixed row-and-column structure.</li><li>Content can have different formats.</li><li>Requires extraction and processing.</li><li>Often contains natural language.</li><li>May include images, tables and layouts.</li></ul><p>Examples: PDF documents, Word documents, text files, images, audio, videos, emails.</p><h3>Semi-structured data</h3><p>Semi-structured data does not follow a rigid relational schema, but it contains organizational elements such as keys, tags, or attributes.</p><pre>{\n  \"employee_id\": 101,\n  \"name\": \"Ravi\",\n  \"department\": \"Engineering\"\n}</pre><p>Examples: JSON, XML, HTML, JSONL.</p><h3>Comparison</h3><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Structured</th><th>Semi-structured</th><th>Unstructured</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Schema</td><td>Fixed</td><td>Flexible</td><td>No fixed schema</td></tr><tr><td>Organization</td><td>Rows and columns</td><td>Tags and keys</td><td>Free-form</td></tr><tr><td>Processing</td><td>Easier</td><td>Moderate</td><td>Often complex</td></tr><tr><td>Example</td><td>SQL</td><td>JSON</td><td>PDF</td></tr><tr><td>RAG extraction</td><td>Record extraction</td><td>Parsing</td><td>Text extraction</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Why is this important in RAG?</h3><p>Different data types require different extraction methods. For example:</p><ul><li>PDF → PDF parser.</li><li>CSV → Pandas.</li><li>JSON → Python JSON module.</li><li>HTML → BeautifulSoup.</li><li>Database → SQL queries.</li></ul><p>You cannot expect one extraction method to work perfectly for every source.</p>" },
      { id: "documents", title: "Document-based data", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Documents are the most common RAG data source, and metadata is what makes their content traceable.</p><p>Documents are among the most common data sources in RAG applications.</p><h3>What is document-based data?</h3><p>Document-based data refers to information stored in files such as PDF, DOCX, TXT and Markdown.</p><p>Examples: research papers, company documentation, product manuals, employee handbooks, technical documentation, legal documents.</p><h3>How does RAG process documents?</h3><p>A typical document ingestion workflow:</p><ol><li>Identify the document source.</li><li>Locate the document.</li><li>Read the file.</li><li>Extract text.</li><li>Extract useful metadata.</li><li>Clean extracted content.</li><li>Convert it into a common document structure.</li><li>Pass it to the chunking stage.</li></ol><h3>What is metadata?</h3><p>Metadata is additional information describing a document.</p><pre>{\n  \"source\": \"employee_policy.pdf\",\n  \"file_type\": \"pdf\",\n  \"page\": 3,\n  \"department\": \"HR\",\n  \"document_type\": \"policy\"\n}</pre><p>Why metadata matters:</p><ul><li>Identify the original document.</li><li>Filter retrieval results.</li><li>Track document versions.</li><li>Identify page numbers.</li><li>Support source citations.</li><li>Manage access permissions.</li></ul><h3>Important considerations</h3><p>When processing documents, consider:</p><ul><li>Duplicate documents.</li><li>Empty documents.</li><li>Corrupted files.</li><li>Unsupported formats.</li><li>Password-protected files.</li><li>Document updates.</li><li>File size limitations.</li><li>Encoding issues.</li></ul>" },
      { id: "pdf", title: "PDF data extraction", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> PDFs come in three kinds (text, scanned, mixed), and only scanned ones need OCR.</p><h3>What is a PDF?</h3><p>PDF stands for Portable Document Format. It is commonly used to store documents while maintaining their formatting and layout across different devices.</p><p>In RAG, we extract the textual information from PDF files so that it can be processed and retrieved.</p><h3>Types of PDF documents</h3><h4><b>Type 1 – Text-based PDF</b></h4><p>Contains actual selectable text. Examples: digital reports, research papers, generated documents. Text can usually be extracted directly using PDF parsing libraries.</p><h4><b>Type 2 – Scanned PDF</b></h4><p>Contains scanned images of physical documents. The content is visually available but may not exist as machine-readable text. OCR (Optical Character Recognition) is required to recognize the text. Examples: scanned invoices, old books, photographed documents.</p><h4><b>Type 3 – Mixed PDF</b></h4><p>Contains both selectable text and scanned images. A combination of PDF extraction and OCR may be required.</p><h3>Popular Python libraries</h3><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Library</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>PyMuPDF</td><td>PDF text and layout extraction</td></tr><tr><td>pypdf</td><td>Reading and extracting PDF content</td></tr><tr><td>pdfplumber</td><td>Text, tables and layout extraction</td></tr><tr><td>pytesseract</td><td>OCR</td></tr><tr><td>pdf2image</td><td>Convert PDF pages into images</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Install packages</h3><pre>uv add pymupdf pypdf pdfplumber</pre><p>For OCR:</p><pre>uv add pytesseract pdf2image</pre><div class=\"callout\"><b>Note</b>Tesseract OCR and Poppler may also need to be installed separately on your operating system.</div><h3>Extract text using PyMuPDF</h3><pre>import pymupdf\n\ndoc = pymupdf.open(\"sample.pdf\")\n\nfor page_number, page in enumerate(doc, start=1):\n    text = page.get_text()\n\n    print(f\"Page: {page_number}\")\n    print(text)</pre><p>Explanation:</p><ul><li><code>pymupdf.open()</code> opens the PDF.</li><li><code>doc</code> represents the PDF document.</li><li><code>doc</code> contains multiple pages.</li><li><code>page.get_text()</code> extracts the text from a page.</li><li><code>enumerate()</code> helps identify page numbers.</li></ul><h3>Extracting PDF metadata</h3><pre>import pymupdf\n\ndoc = pymupdf.open(\"sample.pdf\")\n\nmetadata = doc.metadata\n\nprint(metadata)</pre><p>Metadata may include: title, author, subject, creator, creation date, modification date.</p><h3>Image preprocessing for OCR</h3><p>Before applying OCR, images may need preprocessing. Common techniques:</p><ul><li>Grayscale conversion.</li><li>Noise removal.</li><li>Image resizing.</li><li>Thresholding.</li><li>Deskewing.</li><li>Contrast enhancement.</li><li>Rotation correction.</li></ul><h3>Important PDF extraction challenges</h3><ol><li>Incorrect reading order.</li><li>Multi-column layouts.</li><li>Tables losing their structure.</li><li>Headers and footers appearing repeatedly.</li><li>Scanned documents requiring OCR.</li><li>Images containing important information.</li><li>Special characters and mathematical equations.</li></ol><h3>Practical exercise</h3><p>Create a folder containing five PDF documents. Build a Python script that:</p><ol><li>Reads all PDFs.</li><li>Extracts text from every page.</li><li>Captures the filename.</li><li>Captures the page number.</li><li>Stores extracted content in a JSON file.</li></ol><p>Expected output:</p><pre>[\n  {\n    \"source\": \"company_policy.pdf\",\n    \"page\": 1,\n    \"content\": \"Company policy introduction...\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"source\": \"company_policy.pdf\",\n    \"page\": 2,\n    \"content\": \"Employee leave policy...\"\n  }\n]</pre>" },
      { id: "docx", title: "Word document extraction (DOCX)", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Pull paragraphs, headings and tables out of Word files with python-docx.</p><h3>What is DOCX?</h3><p>DOCX is the standard Microsoft Word document format. It is commonly used for reports, business documents, company policies, research papers, proposals and technical documentation.</p><h3>Why extract DOCX?</h3><p>In RAG, we need the textual content of Word documents rather than the original visual document. We extract:</p><ul><li>Paragraphs.</li><li>Headings.</li><li>Tables.</li><li>Lists.</li><li>Document metadata.</li></ul><h3>Popular Python library</h3><p>python-docx. Install:</p><pre>uv add python-docx</pre><h3>Extract paragraphs</h3><pre>from docx import Document\n\ndoc = Document(\"sample.docx\")\n\nfor paragraph in doc.paragraphs:\n    print(paragraph.text)</pre><h3>Extract tables</h3><pre>from docx import Document\n\ndoc = Document(\"sample.docx\")\n\nfor table in doc.tables:\n    for row in table.rows:\n        values = [cell.text for cell in row.cells]\n        print(values)</pre><h3>Important considerations</h3><ul><li>Preserve headings.</li><li>Extract tables separately.</li><li>Maintain paragraph order.</li><li>Avoid empty paragraphs.</li><li>Capture source metadata.</li><li>Handle images separately if they contain relevant information.</li></ul><h3>Practical exercise</h3><p>Create a DOCX file containing three headings, five paragraphs and one table. Write a Python program to extract all the information and save it as JSON.</p>" },
      { id: "text-files", title: "Text files (TXT and Markdown)", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Plain text is the easiest source to read, but Markdown headings are worth keeping.</p><h3>What is a TXT file?</h3><p>TXT is a plain-text file that contains readable characters without complex document formatting.</p><p>Example, company.txt: \"RAG stands for Retrieval-Augmented Generation. It combines information retrieval with language generation.\"</p><h3>What is Markdown?</h3><p>Markdown is a lightweight markup language used to format text.</p><pre># RAG Introduction\n\n## What is RAG?\n\nRAG stands for Retrieval-Augmented Generation.\n\n### Benefits\n\n- External knowledge\n- Updated information\n- Source references</pre><p>Markdown is widely used in GitHub documentation, technical documentation, README files, knowledge bases and developer notes.</p><h3>Reading TXT files</h3><pre>with open(\"sample.txt\", \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as file:\n    content = file.read()\n\nprint(content)</pre><h3>Reading Markdown files</h3><pre>with open(\"sample.md\", \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as file:\n    content = file.read()\n\nprint(content)</pre><p>Both can initially be processed as text. However, Markdown headings and lists contain useful structural information that you may want to preserve.</p><h3>Important considerations</h3><ul><li>Use the correct encoding.</li><li>Remove unnecessary whitespace.</li><li>Preserve meaningful headings.</li><li>Preserve paragraph boundaries.</li><li>Handle empty files.</li><li>Avoid unnecessarily removing punctuation.</li></ul><h3>Practical exercise</h3><p>Create ten Markdown documents containing different technical topics. Write a script that:</p><ol><li>Reads every Markdown file.</li><li>Extracts the content.</li><li>Captures the filename.</li><li>Stores the content and metadata in JSON.</li></ol>" },
      { id: "csv-excel", title: "CSV and Excel data", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Turn each spreadsheet row into readable text, but don't embed everything.</p><h3>What is CSV?</h3><p>CSV stands for Comma-Separated Values. It stores structured information in rows and columns.</p><pre>id,name,department\n101,Ravi,Engineering\n102,Priya,HR\n103,Kumar,Finance</pre><h3>What is Excel?</h3><p>Excel is a spreadsheet format used to store structured information across worksheets. Common formats: XLSX, XLS.</p><h3>Why are CSV and Excel important in RAG?</h3><p>Businesses often store information in spreadsheets: product catalogs, customer records, financial reports, inventory information, employee information, sales reports. RAG systems can retrieve relevant information from these sources.</p><h3>Install Pandas</h3><pre>uv add pandas openpyxl</pre><h3>Read CSV using Pandas</h3><pre>import pandas as pd\n\ndf = pd.read_csv(\"products.csv\")\n\nprint(df)</pre><h3>Read Excel using Pandas</h3><pre>import pandas as pd\n\ndf = pd.read_excel(\"products.xlsx\")\n\nprint(df)</pre><h3>Convert rows into text</h3><p>Consider this CSV:</p><pre>product,price,category\nLaptop,50000,Electronics\nPhone,20000,Electronics</pre><p>Convert each row into a readable text representation:</p><pre>import pandas as pd\n\ndf = pd.read_csv(\"products.csv\")\n\ndocuments = []\n\nfor _, row in df.iterrows():\n    content = (\n        f\"Product: {row['product']}. \"\n        f\"Price: {row['price']}. \"\n        f\"Category: {row['category']}.\"\n    )\n\n    documents.append(content)\n\nprint(documents)</pre><p>Output:</p><pre>[\n    \"Product: Laptop. Price: 50000. Category: Electronics.\",\n    \"Product: Phone. Price: 20000. Category: Electronics.\"\n]</pre><h3>Important considerations</h3><ul><li>Missing values.</li><li>Duplicate rows.</li><li>Incorrect column names.</li><li>Different data types.</li><li>Large spreadsheets.</li><li>Multiple worksheets.</li><li>Relationships between tables.</li></ul><div class=\"callout\"><b>Important</b>Not every structured dataset should be converted into text and embedded. Exact-value questions, filtering, calculations and frequently changing records may be better handled using SQL or structured queries.</div><h3>Practical exercise</h3><p>Create a CSV containing 100 product records. Extract each row and convert it into a document with metadata:</p><pre>{\n  \"content\": \"Product: Laptop. Price: 50000.\",\n  \"metadata\": {\n    \"product\": \"Laptop\",\n    \"category\": \"Electronics\"\n  }\n}</pre>" },
      { id: "json", title: "JSON and JSONL data", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> JSON is one object, JSONL is one object per line, and both become text for RAG.</p><h3>What is JSON?</h3><p>JSON stands for JavaScript Object Notation. It is a lightweight data format used to exchange information between applications.</p><pre>{\n  \"name\": \"Ravi\",\n  \"role\": \"AI Engineer\",\n  \"skills\": [\"Python\", \"RAG\", \"LLM\"]\n}</pre><p>JSON is commonly used in APIs, application configurations, database exports, application logs and AI datasets.</p><h3>What is JSONL?</h3><p>JSONL stands for JSON Lines. Each line represents an independent JSON object.</p><pre>{\"id\": 1, \"question\": \"What is RAG?\"}\n{\"id\": 2, \"question\": \"What is an embedding?\"}\n{\"id\": 3, \"question\": \"What is retrieval?\"}</pre><h3>Difference between JSON and JSONL</h3><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>JSON</th><th>JSONL</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Structure</td><td>Usually one JSON value</td><td>One JSON value per line</td></tr><tr><td>Reading</td><td>Load complete JSON</td><td>Read line by line</td></tr><tr><td>Large datasets</td><td>Can require more memory</td><td>Easier to stream</td></tr><tr><td>Common use</td><td>APIs and configuration</td><td>Datasets and logs</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Read JSON using Python</h3><pre>import json\n\nwith open(\"data.json\", \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as file:\n    data = json.load(file)\n\nprint(data)</pre><h3>Read JSONL using Python</h3><pre>import json\n\ndata = []\n\nwith open(\"data.jsonl\", \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as file:\n    for line in file:\n        record = json.loads(line)\n        data.append(record)\n\nprint(data)</pre><h3>Convert JSON into text</h3><pre>record = {\n    \"question\": \"What is RAG?\",\n    \"answer\": \"Retrieval-Augmented Generation\"\n}\n\ncontent = (\n    f\"Question: {record['question']}. \"\n    f\"Answer: {record['answer']}.\"\n)\n\nprint(content)</pre><h3>Practical exercise</h3><p>Create a JSON file containing 50 FAQ records. Extract the question, answer, category and ID. Convert each FAQ into a document with metadata.</p>" },
      { id: "html", title: "HTML and web page extraction", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Fetch a page, parse the HTML, and keep only the useful text.</p><h3>What is HTML?</h3><p>HTML stands for HyperText Markup Language. It is used to structure content on web pages.</p><pre>&lt;html&gt;\n&lt;body&gt;\n    &lt;h1&gt;Introduction to RAG&lt;/h1&gt;\n    &lt;p&gt;RAG combines retrieval and generation.&lt;/p&gt;\n&lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;</pre><h3>Why extract HTML?</h3><p>Many knowledge sources are available on websites: product documentation, technical blogs, knowledge bases, help center articles, developer documentation. A RAG system can collect useful text from these pages.</p><h3>Popular Python libraries</h3><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Library</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>BeautifulSoup</td><td>Parse HTML</td></tr><tr><td>Requests</td><td>Fetch web pages</td></tr><tr><td>lxml</td><td>Parse HTML and XML</td></tr><tr><td>Trafilatura</td><td>Extract main article content</td></tr><tr><td>selectolax</td><td>Fast HTML parsing</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Install packages</h3><pre>uv add beautifulsoup4 requests lxml trafilatura</pre><h3>Fetch HTML using Requests</h3><pre>import requests\n\nurl = \"https://example.com\"\n\nresponse = requests.get(url, timeout=20)\n\nprint(response.text)</pre><p>For production, also validate the response status, handle errors, and respect website access policies.</p><h3>Extract text using BeautifulSoup</h3><pre>import requests\nfrom bs4 import BeautifulSoup\n\nurl = \"https://example.com\"\n\nresponse = requests.get(url, timeout=20)\n\nsoup = BeautifulSoup(response.text, \"html.parser\")\n\ntext = soup.get_text(separator=\" \", strip=True)\n\nprint(text)</pre><h3>Extract specific elements</h3><pre>for heading in soup.find_all(\"h1\"):\n    print(heading.get_text(strip=True))\n\nfor paragraph in soup.find_all(\"p\"):\n    print(paragraph.get_text(strip=True))</pre><h3>Important considerations</h3><ul><li>Remove navigation menus.</li><li>Remove unnecessary advertisements.</li><li>Exclude footer content where appropriate.</li><li>Preserve headings.</li><li>Identify duplicate content.</li><li>Handle dynamic web pages.</li><li>Respect robots.txt, website terms and access restrictions.</li></ul><h3>Practical exercise</h3><p>Extract text from five publicly accessible documentation pages. Save the URL, page title, main content and extraction timestamp.</p>" },
      { id: "crawling", title: "Website crawling", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Crawling finds and visits many pages; scraping pulls the content out of them.</p><h3>What is web crawling?</h3><p>Web crawling is the process of automatically discovering and visiting multiple web pages. Unlike extracting one page, crawling collects information from several related pages.</p><p>Suppose a website contains /docs/introduction, /docs/installation, /docs/python, /docs/rag and /docs/retrieval. A crawler visits these pages and collects their content.</p><h3>Crawling vs scraping</h3><h4><b>Web crawling:</b></h4><ul><li>Discovers URLs.</li><li>Follows links.</li><li>Visits multiple pages.</li><li>Collects page locations.</li></ul><h4><b>Web scraping:</b></h4><ul><li>Extracts specific information.</li><li>Extracts headings, paragraphs and tables.</li><li>Processes page content.</li></ul><p>They are often used together.</p><h3>Popular crawling tools</h3><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Scrapy</td><td>Full-featured crawling framework</td></tr><tr><td>Crawl4AI</td><td>Crawling and extraction for AI workflows</td></tr><tr><td>Playwright</td><td>Browser automation</td></tr><tr><td>Selenium</td><td>Browser automation</td></tr><tr><td>Requests + BeautifulSoup</td><td>Simple crawling and extraction</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Basic crawling logic</h3><ol><li>Start with a seed URL.</li><li>Fetch the web page.</li><li>Extract its content.</li><li>Find links on the page.</li><li>Filter allowed links.</li><li>Add new URLs to a queue.</li><li>Visit discovered pages.</li><li>Stop when the crawl limit is reached.</li></ol><h3>Important concepts</h3><ul><li><b>Seed URL</b>: Starting URL.</li><li><b>URL queue</b>: List of URLs waiting to be visited.</li><li><b>Visited URLs</b>: Pages already processed.</li><li><b>Domain restriction</b>: Restrict crawling to a particular website.</li><li><b>Depth limit</b>: Maximum link-following depth.</li><li><b>Rate limiting</b>: Control the request frequency.</li><li><b>Duplicate detection</b>: Avoid processing the same content repeatedly.</li></ul><h3>Practical exercise</h3><p>Build a simple crawler that:</p><ul><li>Starts from a documentation website.</li><li>Visits up to ten pages.</li><li>Extracts headings and paragraphs.</li><li>Stores page URLs.</li><li>Saves extracted content in JSON.</li></ul>" },
      { id: "database", title: "Database data extraction", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Embed only the records that need meaning-based search; answer counts and filters with SQL.</p><h3>What is a database?</h3><p>A database is an organized system for storing and managing information. Examples: PostgreSQL, MySQL, SQLite, MongoDB, Microsoft SQL Server.</p><h3>Why extract database data for RAG?</h3><p>Businesses frequently store important information in databases. An e-commerce application may have product tables, customer tables, order tables, inventory tables and review tables. A RAG application may need to retrieve knowledge from selected database records.</p><h3>SQL database example</h3><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>id</th><th>product</th><th>category</th><th>price</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Laptop</td><td>Electronics</td><td>50000</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Mobile</td><td>Electronics</td><td>20000</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>SQL query:</p><pre>SELECT * FROM products;</pre><h3>Connect to SQLite using Python</h3><pre>import sqlite3\n\nconnection = sqlite3.connect(\"company.db\")\n\ncursor = connection.cursor()\n\ncursor.execute(\"SELECT * FROM employees\")\n\nrecords = cursor.fetchall()\n\nfor record in records:\n    print(record)\n\nconnection.close()</pre><h3>PostgreSQL connection</h3><p>Install:</p><pre>uv add psycopg</pre><p>Example:</p><pre>import psycopg\n\nconnection = psycopg.connect(\n    \"dbname=mydb user=myuser password=mypass host=localhost\"\n)\n\nwith connection.cursor() as cursor:\n    cursor.execute(\"SELECT * FROM products\")\n    records = cursor.fetchall()\n\n    for record in records:\n        print(record)\n\nconnection.close()</pre><div class=\"callout\"><b>Note</b>Use environment variables or a secret manager for credentials rather than hardcoding passwords.</div><h3>SQL vs NoSQL</h3><p><b>SQL databases:</b> PostgreSQL, MySQL, SQLite. Data is generally stored in tables with defined schemas.</p><p><b>NoSQL databases:</b> MongoDB, Redis, Cassandra. Data can be stored in document, key-value, wide-column or other models.</p><h3>Important considerations</h3><ul><li>Database authentication.</li><li>Connection pooling.</li><li>Query optimization.</li><li>Pagination.</li><li>Incremental extraction.</li><li>Data privacy.</li><li>Access permissions.</li><li>Database updates.</li><li>Large dataset handling.</li></ul><h3>Important RAG design decision</h3><p>Not every database record needs to be embedded.</p><p>Question: \"How many orders were placed yesterday?\" This is better answered using a database query than semantic vector retrieval.</p><p>Question: \"What are the most common complaints customers have about our products?\" This may benefit from retrieving and analyzing customer review text.</p>" },
      { id: "api", title: "API-based data collection", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Call an API with GET, check the response, and parse the JSON into documents.</p><h3>What is an API?</h3><p>API stands for Application Programming Interface. An API allows two software applications to communicate and exchange information.</p><p>For example, an application requests product information from an external service, and the API returns JSON data containing product details.</p><h3>Why use APIs in RAG?</h3><p>APIs allow RAG applications to collect information from external or internal services: product information, weather information, customer support tickets, CRM data, internal company services, external knowledge platforms.</p><h3>HTTP methods</h3><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>GET</td><td>Retrieve information</td></tr><tr><td>POST</td><td>Submit information</td></tr><tr><td>PUT</td><td>Replace information</td></tr><tr><td>PATCH</td><td>Partially update information</td></tr><tr><td>DELETE</td><td>Delete information</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>For data collection, GET is commonly used.</p><h3>API response example</h3><pre>{\n  \"id\": 101,\n  \"product\": \"Laptop\",\n  \"price\": 50000,\n  \"description\": \"High-performance laptop\"\n}</pre><h3>Collect data using Requests</h3><pre>import requests\n\nurl = \"https://api.example.com/products\"\n\nresponse = requests.get(url, timeout=20)\n\nresponse.raise_for_status()\n\ndata = response.json()\n\nprint(data)</pre><h3>Important API concepts</h3><h4><b>Authentication:</b></h4><ul><li>API keys.</li><li>Bearer tokens.</li><li>OAuth 2.0.</li></ul><p><b>Pagination:</b> APIs may return limited records per request.</p><ul><li>Page 1: Records 1–100.</li><li>Page 2: Records 101–200.</li><li>Page 3: Records 201–300.</li></ul><p><b>Rate limiting:</b> An API may limit the number of requests within a specific time.</p><p><b>Error handling:</b> Handle these status codes:</p><ul><li>400 Bad Request.</li><li>401 Unauthorized.</li><li>403 Forbidden.</li><li>404 Not Found.</li><li>429 Too Many Requests.</li><li>500 Internal Server Error.</li></ul><h3>Practical exercise</h3><p>Use a public API to collect information. Your program should:</p><ol><li>Send a GET request.</li><li>Validate the response.</li><li>Parse JSON.</li><li>Extract required fields.</li><li>Store the information in JSON.</li><li>Handle API errors.</li></ol>" },
      { id: "multi-source", title: "Multiple data source integration", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Convert every source into one common document structure so the later steps work the same way.</p><h3>What is multiple data source integration?</h3><p>It is the process of collecting information from different sources and bringing it into a common processing pipeline.</p><p>For example, build a company knowledge assistant using PDF policies, DOCX documents, Markdown documentation, CSV product information, PostgreSQL records, website content and external APIs. All sources need to be processed and converted into a consistent structure.</p><h3>Why is a common format important?</h3><p>Imagine extracting information from five different sources:</p><ul><li>PDF returns page text.</li><li>CSV returns rows.</li><li>JSON returns objects.</li><li>HTML returns web page content.</li><li>Database returns records.</li></ul><p>If every source produces a different output format, downstream processing becomes difficult. A common document structure makes it easier to implement chunking, embedding and retrieval.</p><h3>Common document structure</h3><p>A useful starting format:</p><pre>document = {\n    \"id\": \"doc_001\",\n    \"content\": \"Introduction to Retrieval-Augmented Generation\",\n    \"metadata\": {\n        \"source\": \"rag_intro.pdf\",\n        \"source_type\": \"pdf\",\n        \"page\": 1,\n        \"created_at\": \"2026-11-21\"\n    }\n}</pre><h3>Example architecture</h3><p>Each source has its own extraction component. All extracted content is converted into a common document structure.</p><pre>                 DATA SOURCES\n                      |\n        +-------------+-------------+\n        |             |             |\n       PDF           DOCX          CSV\n        |             |             |\n     Extractor     Extractor     Extractor\n        |             |             |\n        +-------------+-------------+\n                      |\n                COMMON FORMAT\n                      |\n                DATA VALIDATION\n                      |\n                DATA CLEANING\n                      |\n              DOCUMENT COLLECTION\n                      |\n               NEXT PHASE:\n                  CHUNKING</pre><h3>Important concepts</h3><ul><li>Modular architecture.</li><li>Reusable extraction functions.</li><li>Common data models.</li><li>Metadata standardization.</li><li>Error handling.</li><li>Logging.</li><li>Duplicate detection.</li><li>Incremental ingestion.</li><li>Data versioning.</li><li>Source tracking.</li></ul><h3>Practical exercise</h3><p>Build a mini data ingestion application. Input: 3 PDF files, 2 DOCX files, 2 CSV files, 2 JSON files, 1 Markdown file. Requirements:</p><ol><li>Identify file types.</li><li>Select the appropriate extractor.</li><li>Extract the content.</li><li>Capture metadata.</li><li>Convert everything into a common structure.</li><li>Store the results in JSONL.</li><li>Handle unsupported and corrupted files.</li></ol>" },
      { id: "project", title: "Final project: multi-format data ingestion system", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Build one Python app that turns many file formats into a single document collection.</p><h3>Project objective</h3><p>Build a Python application that accepts multiple file formats and extracts their contents into one unified document collection.</p><h3>Suggested project structure</h3><pre>rag-data-ingestion/\n│\n├── data/\n│   ├── pdf/\n│   ├── docx/\n│   ├── csv/\n│   ├── json/\n│   ├── markdown/\n│   └── txt/\n│\n├── src/\n│   ├── extractors/\n│   │   ├── pdf_extractor.py\n│   │   ├── docx_extractor.py\n│   │   ├── csv_extractor.py\n│   │   ├── json_extractor.py\n│   │   ├── markdown_extractor.py\n│   │   └── txt_extractor.py\n│   │\n│   ├── ingestion/\n│   │   ├── file_detector.py\n│   │   ├── document_loader.py\n│   │   └── metadata_extractor.py\n│   │\n│   ├── utils/\n│   │   └── validators.py\n│   │\n│   └── main.py\n│\n├── output/\n│   └── documents.jsonl\n│\n├── pyproject.toml\n└── README.md</pre><h3>Expected output</h3><p>Each extracted document should follow this structure:</p><pre>{\n    \"id\": \"doc_001\",\n    \"content\": \"Extracted document content...\",\n    \"metadata\": {\n        \"source\": \"employee_policy.pdf\",\n        \"source_type\": \"pdf\",\n        \"page\": 1\n    }\n}</pre><h3>Project completion criteria</h3><ul><li>Support all six file formats.</li><li>Extract text successfully.</li><li>Preserve source information.</li><li>Extract relevant metadata.</li><li>Handle errors.</li><li>Store results in JSONL.</li><li>Avoid duplicate processing.</li><li>Use separate, reusable extraction modules.</li></ul>" },
      { id: "phase-1-recap", title: "What you should understand before moving on", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> A checklist of what Phase 01 should have taught you.</p><p>By the end of this phase, you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain what data sources are in RAG.</li><li>Differentiate structured, semi-structured and unstructured data.</li><li>Extract text from PDF, DOCX and TXT.</li><li>Read CSV and Excel files using Pandas.</li><li>Process JSON and JSONL.</li><li>Extract information from HTML.</li><li>Understand web crawling.</li><li>Retrieve information from databases.</li><li>Collect data using APIs.</li><li>Integrate multiple data sources.</li><li>Convert extracted information into a common document structure.</li></ul><p>Next phase: Phase 02 – Data Cleaning, Preprocessing &amp; Document Preparation. This will cover text normalization, removing unwanted content, handling encoding, duplicate detection, metadata enrichment, document validation and preparing clean documents for chunking.</p>" },
      { id: "p3-cleaning", title: "Text cleaning", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Remove noise from extracted text without destroying its meaning.</p><h3>What is text cleaning?</h3><p>Text cleaning is the process of removing unwanted or irrelevant content from extracted text while preserving its actual meaning.</p><p>The purpose is to make extracted content more consistent and suitable for downstream processing.</p><p>Original extracted text: \"   RAG is a technique !!!   used to improve AI responses.     \"</p><p>After cleaning: \"RAG is a technique used to improve AI responses.\"</p><h3>Why do we need text cleaning?</h3><p>Extracted content may contain:</p><ul><li>Extra whitespace.</li><li>Repeated characters.</li><li>Unnecessary symbols.</li><li>Empty lines.</li><li>HTML tags.</li><li>Navigation elements.</li><li>Extraction artifacts.</li><li>Repeated headers and footers.</li></ul><p>These elements can create noise in the retrieval pipeline.</p><h3>Common text cleaning operations</h3><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Operation</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Whitespace removal</td><td>Remove unnecessary spaces</td></tr><tr><td>Newline handling</td><td>Normalize line breaks</td></tr><tr><td>HTML tag removal</td><td>Remove markup</td></tr><tr><td>Repeated text removal</td><td>Remove duplicated content</td></tr><tr><td>Empty line removal</td><td>Remove unnecessary blank lines</td></tr><tr><td>Artifact removal</td><td>Remove extraction errors</td></tr><tr><td>Formatting cleanup</td><td>Normalize inconsistent text</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Basic text cleaning using Python</h3><pre>import re\n\ntext = \"   RAG   is a technique !!!   used in AI.   \"\n\n# Remove extra whitespace\ntext = re.sub(r\"\\s+\", \" \", text)\n\n# Remove spaces at the beginning and end\ntext = text.strip()\n\nprint(text)</pre><p>Output:</p><pre>RAG is a technique !!! used in AI.</pre><p>Notice that we did not remove all punctuation because punctuation can carry meaning.</p><h3>Build a reusable cleaning function</h3><pre>import re\n\ndef clean_text(text: str) -&gt; str:\n\n    if not isinstance(text, str):\n        return \"\"\n\n    # Normalize whitespace\n    text = re.sub(r\"\\s+\", \" \", text)\n\n    # Remove leading and trailing whitespace\n    text = text.strip()\n\n    return text</pre><h3>Important considerations</h3><p>Text cleaning should not destroy useful information.</p><p>Original: \"Revenue increased from 10% to 25%.\"</p><p>Incorrect cleaning: \"Revenue increased from 10 to 25\"</p><p>The percentage symbol is important. Similarly, mathematical operators, currencies, dates and punctuation may carry essential meaning.</p><h3>Practical exercise</h3><p>Create a Python function that:</p><ol><li>Removes unnecessary whitespace.</li><li>Removes empty lines.</li><li>Removes unwanted extraction artifacts.</li><li>Preserves meaningful punctuation.</li><li>Returns clean text.</li></ol><p>Test it using five different extracted documents.</p>" },
      { id: "p3-characters", title: "Remove unnecessary characters", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Strip control characters and decorative separators, but only when you know they are noise.</p><h3>What are unnecessary characters?</h3><p>During document extraction, some characters may appear that do not contribute to the actual content:</p><ul><li>Repeated separators.</li><li>Decorative symbols.</li><li>Unexpected control characters.</li><li>Unwanted formatting artifacts.</li><li>Certain non-printable characters.</li></ul><p>Original: \"RAG ### combines retrieval *** and generation.\"</p><p>Cleaned: \"RAG combines retrieval and generation.\"</p><p>However, not every special character is unnecessary.</p><h3>Common unwanted characters</h3><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Character</th><th>Possible issue</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>\\t</td><td>Unwanted tab</td></tr><tr><td>\\x00</td><td>Null character</td></tr><tr><td>\\r</td><td>Carriage return</td></tr><tr><td>Repeated underscores</td><td>Decorative separator</td></tr><tr><td>Repeated hyphens</td><td>Page separator</td></tr><tr><td>Unexpected control characters</td><td>Extraction artifacts</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Remove control characters</h3><pre>import re\n\ntext = \"RAG\\x00 is a retrieval\\x01 system.\"\n\ntext = re.sub(\n    r\"[\\x00-\\x08\\x0B\\x0C\\x0E-\\x1F\\x7F]\",\n    \"\",\n    text\n)\n\nprint(text)</pre><p>Output:</p><pre>RAG is a retrieval system.</pre><h3>Remove repeated decorative separators</h3><pre>import re\n\ntext = \"\"\"\nIntroduction to RAG\n-------------------\nRAG combines retrieval and generation.\n===================\n\"\"\"\n\ntext = re.sub(r\"[-_=]{3,}\", \"\", text)\n\nprint(text)</pre><div class=\"callout\"><b>Important</b>Only remove separators when you know they are decorative. A hyphen or underscore can be meaningful in technical content, identifiers and mathematical expressions.</div><h3>Remove unnecessary tabs and spaces</h3><pre>import re\n\ntext = \"RAG\\t\\tis    useful.\"\n\ntext = re.sub(r\"[ \\t]+\", \" \", text)\n\nprint(text)</pre><p>Output:</p><pre>RAG is useful.</pre><h3>Practical exercise</h3><p>Create a text cleaning function that handles null characters, control characters, repeated separators, extra tabs and excessive whitespace. Test it with extracted PDF and HTML content.</p>" },
      { id: "p3-headers", title: "Remove headers and footers", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Repeated page headers and footers add noise, but some of them belong in metadata.</p><h3>What are headers and footers?</h3><p>Headers and footers are repeated elements appearing at the top and bottom of document pages.</p><ul><li>Header: \"COMPANY CONFIDENTIAL – INTERNAL USE ONLY\"</li><li>Main content: \"Employees are eligible for annual leave.\"</li><li>Footer: \"Page 01 | Copyright 2026\"</li></ul><p>These repeated elements may appear on every page.</p><h3>Why remove headers and footers?</h3><p>Imagine a 100-page PDF. If the same header and footer appear on every page, they introduce repeated content into the RAG knowledge base.</p><p>Problems:</p><ul><li>Unnecessary duplication.</li><li>More irrelevant text.</li><li>Increased embedding and storage usage.</li><li>Potentially less useful retrieval results.</li></ul><p>However, headers and footers are not always noise. These may be important and should be preserved separately as metadata:</p><ul><li>Document title.</li><li>Classification labels.</li><li>Version information.</li><li>Copyright notices.</li><li>Legal disclaimers.</li></ul><h3>Basic header and footer removal</h3><p>For known repeated phrases:</p><pre>def remove_headers_footers(text):\n\n    unwanted = [\n        \"COMPANY CONFIDENTIAL\",\n        \"Copyright 2026\"\n    ]\n\n    for phrase in unwanted:\n        text = text.replace(phrase, \"\")\n\n    return text</pre><h3>Remove page number patterns</h3><pre>import re\n\ntext = \"\"\"\nEmployee Policy\n\nEmployees are eligible for annual leave.\n\nPage 1\n\"\"\"\n\ntext = re.sub(\n    r\"(?im)^\\s*Page\\s+\\d+\\s*$\",\n    \"\",\n    text\n)\n\nprint(text)</pre><h3>Detect repeated headers and footers</h3><p>For large PDF collections, hardcoding phrases is not scalable. A better approach:</p><ol><li>Extract text page by page.</li><li>Identify text blocks near the top and bottom.</li><li>Normalize the extracted text.</li><li>Count repeated occurrences across pages.</li><li>Identify likely headers and footers.</li><li>Remove or separately store them.</li></ol><p>PDF layout tools such as PyMuPDF can provide block coordinates that help identify text positions.</p><h3>Practical exercise</h3><p>Take a PDF with at least 10 pages. Build a program that:</p><ul><li>Extracts text page by page.</li><li>Identifies repeated first and last lines.</li><li>Counts repeated phrases.</li><li>Removes confirmed decorative headers and footers.</li><li>Preserves meaningful document metadata.</li></ul>" },
      { id: "p3-duplicates", title: "Remove duplicate content", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Use exact hashing first, then normalized and near-duplicate checks, and keep the source.</p><h3>What is duplicate content?</h3><p>Duplicate content means the same or substantially identical information appears multiple times.</p><p>Duplicates may exist:</p><ul><li>Inside one document.</li><li>Across different documents.</li><li>Across different pages.</li><li>Across different ingestion runs.</li><li>Across different versions of a document.</li></ul><p>Document A: \"RAG combines retrieval and generation.\"</p><p>Document B: \"RAG combines retrieval and generation.\"</p><p>Both contain identical content.</p><h3>Why remove duplicate content?</h3><p>Duplicate information can cause:</p><ul><li>Redundant retrieval results.</li><li>Repeated answers.</li><li>Unnecessary embedding costs.</li><li>Increased vector storage.</li><li>Reduced diversity of retrieved context.</li></ul><p>However, two identical sentences from different sources may have different provenance or authority. Do not automatically discard source information.</p><h3>Types of duplicate detection</h3><h4><b>1. Exact duplicate detection</b></h4><p>Two pieces of text are exactly identical.</p><h4><b>2. Normalized duplicate detection</b></h4><p>Text is identical after normalizing whitespace, case or other harmless formatting differences.</p><h4><b>3. Near-duplicate detection</b></h4><p>Two texts are very similar but not exactly identical. For example: \"RAG combines retrieval and generation.\" and \"RAG combines information retrieval and text generation.\"</p><h3>Exact duplicate detection using Python</h3><pre>documents = [\n    \"RAG combines retrieval and generation.\",\n    \"Embeddings represent text as vectors.\",\n    \"RAG combines retrieval and generation.\"\n]\n\nunique_documents = list(set(documents))\n\nprint(unique_documents)</pre><div class=\"callout\"><b>Note</b>A set does not preserve the original ordering. For production, use an order-preserving approach.</div><pre>unique_documents = list(dict.fromkeys(documents))</pre><h3>Duplicate detection using hashing</h3><p>Hashing creates a fixed-length identifier from content.</p><pre>import hashlib\n\ndef generate_hash(text):\n\n    return hashlib.sha256(\n        text.encode(\"utf-8\")\n    ).hexdigest()\n\ntext1 = \"RAG combines retrieval and generation.\"\ntext2 = \"RAG combines retrieval and generation.\"\n\nprint(generate_hash(text1) == generate_hash(text2))</pre><p>Output:</p><pre>True</pre><h3>Normalized duplicate detection</h3><pre>import re\nimport hashlib\n\ndef normalize_for_deduplication(text):\n\n    text = text.casefold()\n    text = re.sub(r\"\\s+\", \" \", text)\n\n    return text.strip()\n\ndef generate_hash(text):\n\n    normalized = normalize_for_deduplication(text)\n\n    return hashlib.sha256(\n        normalized.encode(\"utf-8\")\n    ).hexdigest()</pre><h3>Near-duplicate detection</h3><p>Common approaches:</p><ul><li>Levenshtein distance.</li><li>Jaccard similarity.</li><li>MinHash.</li><li>SimHash.</li><li>Cosine similarity using embeddings.</li></ul><h4><b>Jaccard similarity:</b></h4><p>Measures the similarity between two sets.</p><pre>J(A, B) = |A ∩ B| / |A ∪ B|</pre><p>For example: A = {RAG, retrieval, generation} and B = {RAG, retrieval, embeddings}. The intersection has 2 items and the union has 4, so the Jaccard similarity is 2/4 = 0.5.</p><h3>Important considerations</h3><ul><li>Do not remove meaningful repeated sentences.</li><li>Preserve original source information.</li><li>Avoid comparing entire large documents using expensive pairwise operations.</li><li>Use exact hashing before expensive similarity checks.</li><li>Define duplicate detection scope clearly.</li><li>Maintain document version history.</li></ul><h3>Practical exercise</h3><p>Create 100 text records containing 50 unique records, 30 exact duplicates and 20 slightly modified records. Implement exact and near-duplicate detection and compare the results.</p>" },
      { id: "p3-empty", title: "Handle empty documents", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Check for empty content before chunking, and route image-only pages to OCR.</p><h3>What is an empty document?</h3><p>An empty document is a document that contains no useful extractable content.</p><pre>\"      \"\n\n\"\\n\\n\\n\"\n\n\"\"</pre><p>A document can also appear empty when:</p><ul><li>PDF text extraction fails.</li><li>It contains only scanned images.</li><li>It is corrupted.</li><li>The parser does not support its format.</li></ul><h3>Why is empty document handling important?</h3><p>Empty documents should not continue blindly into chunking and embedding. Otherwise, they may cause:</p><ul><li>Empty embedding inputs.</li><li>Processing errors.</li><li>Wasted computation.</li><li>Incorrect ingestion statistics.</li><li>Invalid retrieval records.</li></ul><h3>Basic empty document validation</h3><pre>def is_empty_document(text):\n\n    if not text:\n        return True\n\n    if not text.strip():\n        return True\n\n    return False</pre><h3>Identify empty PDF pages</h3><pre>import pymupdf\n\ndoc = pymupdf.open(\"sample.pdf\")\n\nfor page_number, page in enumerate(doc, start=1):\n\n    text = page.get_text().strip()\n\n    if not text:\n        print(f\"Empty or non-text page: {page_number}\")</pre><div class=\"callout\"><b>Important</b>An empty extraction result does not prove that the page is blank. It may contain scanned images. Such pages should be checked for images and potentially routed to OCR.</div><h3>Handling empty documents</h3><p>Possible actions:</p><ul><li>Skip the document.</li><li>Send it for OCR.</li><li>Mark it as failed.</li><li>Store an empty-content status.</li><li>Request a different source file.</li></ul><h3>Practical exercise</h3><p>Create a folder containing a normal TXT file, an empty TXT file, a blank PDF, a scanned PDF and a corrupted PDF. Build a validation program that assigns the correct processing status to each file.</p>" },
      { id: "p3-special", title: "Handle special characters", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Special characters are often meaningful, so normalize Unicode instead of deleting symbols.</p><h3>What are special characters?</h3><p>Special characters are characters other than ordinary alphabetic letters and digits.</p><ul><li>! @ # $ % ^ &amp; *</li><li>+ - = / \\</li><li>₹ € £ ¥</li><li>© ® ™</li><li>Emojis.</li><li>Mathematical symbols.</li><li>Unicode characters.</li></ul><h3>Why handle special characters?</h3><p>Extracted documents may contain:</p><ul><li>Incorrect symbols.</li><li>Encoding artifacts.</li><li>Unsupported characters.</li><li>Unexpected replacement characters.</li><li>Unwanted decorative symbols.</li></ul><p>These may affect readability and downstream processing. However, special characters are often essential:</p><ul><li>\"Price: ₹50,000\"</li><li>\"Temperature: 37°C\"</li><li>\"Accuracy: 95%\"</li><li>\"API: /v1/users\"</li></ul><p>Removing these symbols would change the meaning.</p><h3>Unicode</h3><p>Unicode is a standard that assigns code points to characters from different writing systems. It supports English, Telugu, Hindi, Chinese, Arabic, mathematical symbols and emojis. Python strings support Unicode.</p><pre>text = \"RAG గురించి తెలుసుకుందాం\"\n\nprint(text)</pre><h3>Unicode normalization</h3><p>Some visually identical characters can have different internal Unicode representations. Python provides the <code>unicodedata</code> module.</p><pre>import unicodedata\n\ntext = \"café\"\n\nnormalized = unicodedata.normalize(\"NFC\", text)\n\nprint(normalized)</pre><p>Common Unicode normalization forms:</p><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Form</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>NFC</td><td>Canonical composition</td></tr><tr><td>NFD</td><td>Canonical decomposition</td></tr><tr><td>NFKC</td><td>Compatibility composition</td></tr><tr><td>NFKD</td><td>Compatibility decomposition</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>NFC is often a sensible starting point for general text normalization. NFKC can change compatibility characters and should be applied only when appropriate for the content.</p><h3>Handle replacement characters</h3><p>A common extraction artifact is:</p><pre>RAG � combines retrieval.</pre><p>The replacement character <code>�</code> can indicate that decoding or extraction failed.</p><pre>text = text.replace(\"�\", \"\")</pre><p>However, removing it does not recover the missing original character. For important documents, investigate the source encoding or extraction process.</p><h3>Practical exercise</h3><p>Create sample documents containing Telugu text, Hindi text, currency symbols, mathematical symbols, emojis and Unicode characters. Apply normalization and verify that the original meaning remains intact.</p>" },
      { id: "p3-normalize", title: "Normalize text", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Standardize how text is represented while keeping its meaning, and keep casing unless you need to ignore it.</p><h3>What is text normalization?</h3><p>Text normalization is the process of converting text into a consistent representation. The objective is to reduce unnecessary variations while preserving meaning.</p><p>Original: \"  Retrieval-Augmented   Generation \"</p><p>Normalized: \"Retrieval-Augmented Generation\"</p><h3>Why is normalization important in RAG?</h3><p>The same information may appear in different textual representations: \"RAG SYSTEM\", \"Rag System\", \"rag system\". Depending on the application, these may need to be treated consistently for deduplication, matching or search.</p><p>Normalization can improve:</p><ul><li>Text consistency.</li><li>Duplicate detection.</li><li>Search preprocessing.</li><li>Data quality.</li><li>Comparison operations.</li></ul><h3>Common normalization techniques</h3><h4><b>1. Whitespace normalization</b></h4><p>Convert repeated whitespace into a single space.</p><h4><b>2. Unicode normalization</b></h4><p>Standardize Unicode representations.</p><h4><b>3. Case normalization</b></h4><p>Convert text to lowercase or casefold where appropriate.</p><h4><b>4. Line break normalization</b></h4><p>Standardize Windows, Unix and other line endings.</p><h4><b>5. Encoding normalization</b></h4><p>Ensure text is handled using a consistent encoding such as UTF-8.</p><h4><b>6. Punctuation normalization</b></h4><p>Standardize certain punctuation variants when safe.</p><h3>Python text normalization</h3><pre>import re\nimport unicodedata\n\ndef normalize_text(text):\n\n    # Unicode normalization\n    text = unicodedata.normalize(\"NFC\", text)\n\n    # Normalize line endings\n    text = text.replace(\"\\r\\n\", \"\\n\")\n    text = text.replace(\"\\r\", \"\\n\")\n\n    # Normalize whitespace\n    text = re.sub(r\"[ \\t]+\", \" \", text)\n\n    # Remove excessive blank lines\n    text = re.sub(r\"\\n{3,}\", \"\\n\\n\", text)\n\n    # Trim surrounding whitespace\n    text = text.strip()\n\n    return text</pre><h3>Lowercase vs case preservation</h3><pre>text = \"Retrieval Augmented Generation\"\n\nnormalized = text.casefold()</pre><p>But converting all text to lowercase may be undesirable:</p><ul><li><code>OpenAI</code> is a brand name.</li><li><code>HTTP</code> is an acronym.</li><li><code>Python</code> is a programming language.</li><li><code>US</code> and <code>us</code> can have different meanings.</li></ul><p>For general RAG content, preserve original casing unless a specific downstream operation requires case-insensitive normalization.</p><h3>Cleaning vs normalization</h3><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Cleaning</th><th>Normalization</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Removes unwanted content</td><td>Standardizes representation</td></tr><tr><td>Removes extraction artifacts</td><td>Standardizes whitespace</td></tr><tr><td>Removes unwanted separators</td><td>Standardizes Unicode</td></tr><tr><td>Removes repeated headers</td><td>May standardize case</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Practical exercise</h3><p>Build a reusable normalization module. Test it using PDF text, HTML text, Markdown, Telugu text and technical documentation. Compare original and normalized outputs.</p>" },
      { id: "p3-formatting", title: "Preserve document formatting", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Headings, lists, tables and code carry structure, so keep them instead of flattening to plain text.</p><h3>What is document formatting?</h3><p>Document formatting describes how content is visually and logically organized:</p><ul><li>Headings.</li><li>Subheadings.</li><li>Paragraphs.</li><li>Bullet points.</li><li>Numbered lists.</li><li>Tables.</li><li>Code blocks.</li><li>Mathematical expressions.</li></ul><h3>Why preserve formatting?</h3><p>Consider this original content:</p><pre># RAG Architecture\n\n## Components\n\n1. Retriever\n2. Vector Database\n3. Language Model</pre><p>If all formatting is removed: \"RAG Architecture Components Retriever Vector Database Language Model\". The relationships between the title, heading and list items become unclear. Preserving structure helps maintain contextual relationships.</p><h3>Formatting elements to preserve</h3><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>How to preserve</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Heading</td><td>Markdown heading</td></tr><tr><td>Paragraph</td><td>Paragraph boundaries</td></tr><tr><td>Bullet list</td><td>Markdown list</td></tr><tr><td>Numbered list</td><td>Ordered list</td></tr><tr><td>Table</td><td>Markdown or structured table</td></tr><tr><td>Code</td><td>Code block</td></tr><tr><td>Equation</td><td>Mathematical representation</td></tr><tr><td>Image</td><td>Image reference and caption</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Preserve formatting using Markdown</h3><pre># RAG Architecture\n\n## Components\n\n- Retriever\n- Vector Database\n- Language Model</pre><p>Store it without flattening the structure. Markdown is useful because it is readable, lightweight and supports document hierarchy.</p><h3>Why not remove all newlines?</h3><p>Newlines may indicate:</p><ul><li>Paragraph boundaries.</li><li>Heading boundaries.</li><li>Table rows.</li><li>Code formatting.</li><li>List items.</li></ul><p>Removing all newlines may damage the original meaning.</p><h3>Practical exercise</h3><p>Take a Markdown document and a formatted PDF. Extract the content while preserving headings, subheadings, lists, paragraphs, tables and code blocks. Compare the extracted structure with the original.</p>" },
      { id: "p3-enrichment", title: "Metadata enrichment", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Add useful descriptive fields to each document so retrieval can filter and cite it.</p><h3>What is metadata enrichment?</h3><p>Metadata enrichment is the process of adding useful descriptive information to an existing document or its metadata.</p><p>During document loading, we may only capture basic information:</p><pre>{\n  \"source\": \"policy.pdf\",\n  \"page\": 1\n}</pre><p>After enrichment, we may have:</p><pre>{\n  \"source\": \"policy.pdf\",\n  \"page\": 1,\n  \"document_type\": \"policy\",\n  \"department\": \"HR\",\n  \"language\": \"en\",\n  \"content_category\": \"employee_benefits\",\n  \"ingestion_date\": \"2026-11-22\"\n}</pre><h3>Why is metadata enrichment important?</h3><p>Metadata helps the retrieval system understand and filter documents. If a user asks \"Find the latest HR policies,\" the application may filter using:</p><pre>{\n    \"department\": \"HR\",\n    \"document_type\": \"policy\",\n    \"status\": \"active\"\n}</pre><h3>Types of metadata enrichment</h3><h4><b>1. Source enrichment</b></h4><ul><li>Source name.</li><li>File type.</li><li>URL.</li><li>Database table.</li></ul><h4><b>2. Content enrichment</b></h4><ul><li>Document title.</li><li>Topic.</li><li>Category.</li><li>Language.</li><li>Keywords.</li></ul><h4><b>3. Business enrichment</b></h4><ul><li>Department.</li><li>Product.</li><li>Customer category.</li><li>Business unit.</li></ul><h4><b>4. Technical enrichment</b></h4><ul><li>Document ID.</li><li>Version.</li><li>Content hash.</li><li>Processing timestamp.</li><li>Extraction method.</li></ul><h4><b>5. Access enrichment</b></h4><ul><li>Owner.</li><li>Access level.</li><li>Tenant ID.</li><li>Permissions.</li></ul><h3>Automatic metadata enrichment</h3><p>Metadata can be obtained from:</p><ul><li>File properties.</li><li>Document content.</li><li>Source systems.</li><li>Rules and lookup tables.</li><li>Classification models.</li><li>LLM-based extraction.</li></ul><p>A document containing \"Employee Leave Policy\" could be classified as:</p><pre>{\n  \"document_type\": \"policy\",\n  \"department\": \"HR\",\n  \"topic\": \"leave\"\n}</pre><p>However, automatically generated metadata should be validated. An LLM may infer an incorrect department or category.</p><h3>Metadata enrichment example</h3><pre>from datetime import datetime, timezone\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\ndef enrich_metadata(file_path, document_id):\n\n    path = Path(file_path)\n\n    return {\n        \"document_id\": document_id,\n        \"source\": path.name,\n        \"file_type\": path.suffix.lower(),\n        \"file_size\": path.stat().st_size,\n        \"ingestion_timestamp\": datetime.now(\n            timezone.utc\n        ).isoformat()\n    }</pre><h3>Important considerations</h3><ul><li>Use consistent field names.</li><li>Avoid unnecessary metadata.</li><li>Validate automatically generated fields.</li><li>Preserve original source information.</li><li>Do not store sensitive information unnecessarily.</li><li>Keep access-control metadata accurate.</li><li>Distinguish source metadata from inferred metadata.</li></ul><h3>Practical exercise</h3><p>Create a metadata enrichment module that adds document ID, filename, file type, file size, language, document category, ingestion timestamp and content hash.</p>" },
      { id: "p3-validation", title: "Document validation", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Check every processed document against rules before it moves on, and use Pydantic for schemas.</p><h3>What is document validation?</h3><p>Document validation is the process of checking whether a processed document satisfies the requirements of the ingestion pipeline. A document may be extracted successfully but still be unsuitable for further processing.</p><pre>{\n  \"document_id\": \"\",\n  \"content\": \" \",\n  \"metadata\": {}\n}</pre><p>This document has several problems:</p><ul><li>Missing document ID.</li><li>Empty content.</li><li>Missing metadata.</li></ul><h3>Why is validation important?</h3><p>Validation helps prevent poor-quality documents from entering the next stages. It helps identify:</p><ul><li>Missing content.</li><li>Missing identifiers.</li><li>Invalid metadata.</li><li>Incorrect data types.</li><li>Duplicate records.</li><li>Invalid source references.</li></ul><h3>Common validation rules</h3><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Validation</th><th>Expected condition</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Document ID</td><td>Must exist</td></tr><tr><td>Content</td><td>Must be non-empty</td></tr><tr><td>Metadata</td><td>Must be a valid dictionary</td></tr><tr><td>Source</td><td>Must be available</td></tr><tr><td>File type</td><td>Must be supported</td></tr><tr><td>Content type</td><td>Must be string</td></tr><tr><td>Page number</td><td>Must be valid when applicable</td></tr><tr><td>Encoding</td><td>Must be valid Unicode text</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Basic validation function</h3><pre>def validate_document(document):\n\n    errors = []\n\n    if not document.get(\"document_id\"):\n        errors.append(\"Missing document ID\")\n\n    content = document.get(\"content\")\n\n    if not isinstance(content, str) or not content.strip():\n        errors.append(\"Empty or invalid content\")\n\n    metadata = document.get(\"metadata\")\n\n    if not isinstance(metadata, dict):\n        errors.append(\"Invalid metadata\")\n\n    elif not metadata.get(\"source\"):\n        errors.append(\"Missing source\")\n\n    return {\n        \"is_valid\": len(errors) == 0,\n        \"errors\": errors\n    }</pre><h3>Validation vs cleaning</h3><p>These are different operations. Cleaning attempts to correct or remove unwanted content. Validation checks whether the resulting content satisfies predefined rules.</p><p>For example, cleaning removes extra whitespace, and validation checks whether the resulting document is empty.</p><h3>Schema validation</h3><p>For larger systems, use Pydantic to define a document schema. Install:</p><pre>uv add pydantic</pre><p>Example:</p><pre>from pydantic import BaseModel, Field\n\nclass Document(BaseModel):\n\n    document_id: str = Field(min_length=1)\n\n    content: str = Field(min_length=1)\n\n    metadata: dict</pre><p>Create a document:</p><pre>document = Document(\n    document_id=\"doc_001\",\n    content=\"Introduction to RAG\",\n    metadata={\n        \"source\": \"rag.pdf\",\n        \"page\": 1\n    }\n)</pre><p>Pydantic validates the data against the defined model. Additional custom validators can enforce rules such as non-whitespace content and valid page numbers.</p><h3>Practical exercise</h3><p>Create a validation module that checks document ID, content, metadata, source, page number, file type and duplicate hash. Generate a validation report for 50 documents.</p>" },
      { id: "p3-quality", title: "Data quality checks", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Measure completeness, accuracy, consistency, uniqueness, validity and timeliness with simple rates.</p><h3>What is data quality?</h3><p>Data quality refers to how suitable, accurate, complete, consistent and reliable data is for its intended purpose. In RAG, poor-quality input data can lead to poor retrieval and incorrect answers.</p><p>Original: \"Revenue increased by 25%.\"</p><p>Incorrect extracted version: \"Revenue increased by 2S%.\"</p><p>Even if the document is successfully processed, the extracted content contains an error.</p><h3>Dimensions of data quality</h3><h4><b>1. Completeness</b></h4><p>Is the required content available? Missing paragraphs, pages or tables.</p><h4><b>2. Accuracy</b></h4><p>Does extracted content match the original document? Incorrect OCR characters, numbers or dates.</p><h4><b>3. Consistency</b></h4><p>Is information represented in a consistent format? Different date formats, inconsistent metadata fields, different currency representations.</p><h4><b>4. Uniqueness</b></h4><p>Are unnecessary duplicate documents present?</p><h4><b>5. Validity</b></h4><p>Does the document follow the expected schema and rules?</p><h4><b>6. Timeliness</b></h4><p>Is the information current? Old product prices, expired company policies, outdated technical documentation.</p><h3>Data quality metrics</h3><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Metric</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Completeness rate</td><td>Percentage of expected content available</td></tr><tr><td>Duplicate rate</td><td>Percentage of duplicate records</td></tr><tr><td>Validation pass rate</td><td>Percentage of valid documents</td></tr><tr><td>Extraction success rate</td><td>Percentage of successfully processed files</td></tr><tr><td>OCR accuracy</td><td>Accuracy of recognized text</td></tr><tr><td>Processing failure rate</td><td>Percentage of failed documents</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Suppose we process 100 documents: 90 successfully extracted, 5 empty, 3 corrupted, 2 unsupported.</p><pre>Extraction success rate = 90 / 100 × 100 = 90%</pre><h3>Data quality report</h3><p>A simple report:</p><pre>{\n  \"total_documents\": 100,\n  \"successful_documents\": 90,\n  \"failed_documents\": 5,\n  \"empty_documents\": 5,\n  \"duplicate_documents\": 8,\n  \"validation_pass_rate\": 90.0,\n  \"extraction_success_rate\": 90.0\n}</pre><div class=\"callout\"><b>Note</b>These metrics can overlap. For example, a duplicate document may also be successfully extracted. Define the denominator and counting rules clearly for each metric.</div><h3>Why is data quality important for RAG?</h3><p>The RAG pipeline depends on the quality of the information supplied to it. Poor input quality can cause:</p><ul><li>Incorrect embeddings.</li><li>Irrelevant retrieval.</li><li>Missing context.</li><li>Incorrect answers.</li><li>Poor source citations.</li><li>Increased processing costs.</li></ul><h3>Practical exercise</h3><p>Create a dataset containing 100 documents with different quality issues: missing content, duplicate records, incorrect metadata, extra whitespace, invalid characters and outdated documents. Build a data quality report and calculate the quality metrics.</p>" },
      { id: "p3-project", title: "Final project: cleaning and preprocessing pipeline", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Build a reusable app that turns extracted documents into clean, validated documents ready for chunking.</p><h3>Project objective</h3><p>Build a reusable Python application that takes extracted documents from the previous phase and prepares clean, validated documents for the chunking stage.</p><h3>Input</h3><p>Your application receives standardized document objects:</p><pre>{\n  \"document_id\": \"doc_001\",\n  \"content\": \"   RAG   combines retrieval and generation.   \",\n  \"metadata\": {\n    \"source\": \"rag_intro.pdf\",\n    \"page_number\": 1,\n    \"source_type\": \"pdf\"\n  }\n}</pre><h3>Processing steps</h3><ol><li>Load extracted documents.</li><li>Validate input structure.</li><li>Remove unnecessary characters.</li><li>Normalize whitespace.</li><li>Remove unwanted headers and footers.</li><li>Detect duplicate content.</li><li>Handle empty documents.</li><li>Normalize Unicode.</li><li>Preserve meaningful formatting.</li><li>Enrich metadata.</li><li>Validate processed documents.</li><li>Generate a quality report.</li><li>Save clean documents.</li></ol><h3>Suggested project structure</h3><pre>rag-preprocessing/\n│\n├── data/\n│   ├── raw/\n│   └── processed/\n│\n├── src/\n│   ├── cleaning/\n│   │   ├── text_cleaner.py\n│   │   ├── character_cleaner.py\n│   │   ├── header_footer.py\n│   │   └── normalizer.py\n│   │\n│   ├── deduplication/\n│   │   ├── exact_duplicates.py\n│   │   └── near_duplicates.py\n│   │\n│   ├── metadata/\n│   │   └── metadata_enrichment.py\n│   │\n│   ├── validation/\n│   │   ├── document_validator.py\n│   │   └── quality_checker.py\n│   │\n│   ├── models/\n│   │   └── document.py\n│   │\n│   └── main.py\n│\n├── reports/\n│   └── quality_report.json\n│\n├── output/\n│   ├── clean_documents.jsonl\n│   └── rejected_documents.jsonl\n│\n├── pyproject.toml\n└── README.md</pre><h3>Expected output</h3><pre>{\n  \"document_id\": \"doc_001\",\n  \"content\": \"RAG combines retrieval and generation.\",\n  \"metadata\": {\n    \"source\": \"rag_intro.pdf\",\n    \"page_number\": 1,\n    \"source_type\": \"pdf\",\n    \"content_hash\": \"generated_hash\",\n    \"processing_status\": \"completed\"\n  }\n}</pre><h3>Project completion criteria</h3><ul><li>Clean extracted text.</li><li>Preserve important information.</li><li>Detect exact duplicates.</li><li>Identify empty documents.</li><li>Normalize Unicode.</li><li>Preserve document structure.</li><li>Enrich metadata.</li><li>Validate document objects.</li><li>Generate data quality reports.</li><li>Separate successful and rejected documents.</li></ul>" },
      { id: "p3-revision", title: "Revision checklist", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Sixteen questions to answer before moving on to chunking.</p><p>Before moving to the next phase, make sure you can explain:</p><ol><li>What is text cleaning?</li><li>Why should unnecessary characters be removed?</li><li>How do you identify repeated headers and footers?</li><li>What is exact duplicate detection?</li><li>What is near-duplicate detection?</li><li>What is hashing?</li><li>How do you handle empty documents?</li><li>What is Unicode?</li><li>What is Unicode normalization?</li><li>What is the difference between cleaning and normalization?</li><li>Why should document formatting be preserved?</li><li>What is metadata enrichment?</li><li>What is document validation?</li><li>What is schema validation?</li><li>What are the dimensions of data quality?</li><li>How do you calculate extraction success and duplicate rates?</li></ol>" },
      { id: "p3-next", title: "Next: document chunking", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> After cleaning, documents are divided into smaller pieces called chunks.</p><p>After collecting, extracting and cleaning the data, the next step is to divide documents into smaller pieces called chunks.</p><p>Topics to learn:</p><ol><li>What is document chunking?</li><li>Why is chunking required in RAG?</li><li>Document-level vs chunk-level processing</li><li>Fixed-size chunking</li><li>Character-based chunking</li><li>Token-based chunking</li><li>Sentence-based chunking</li><li>Paragraph-based chunking</li><li>Recursive character text splitting</li><li>Semantic chunking</li><li>Structure-aware chunking</li><li>Chunk size selection</li><li>Chunk overlap</li><li>Metadata inheritance</li><li>Parent-child chunking</li><li>Chunk quality evaluation</li></ol><div class=\"callout\"><b>Important</b>Do not aggressively clean your documents. The objective is not to make every document look like plain text. The objective is to remove noise while preserving the original meaning, relationships, technical details and document structure.</div>" },
      { id: "p4-original", title: "Original document storage", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Keep the uploaded file untouched, separate from its extracted text, so you can always reprocess it.</p><h3>What is original document storage?</h3><p>Original document storage is the process of storing the actual files collected from different data sources without modifying their original content.</p><p>Examples: PDF documents, DOCX files, TXT files, Markdown files, Excel spreadsheets, images, scanned documents.</p><p>Suppose a company uploads an employee handbook, employee_handbook.pdf. The original PDF should be preserved even after extracting and cleaning its text.</p><p>Why? Because the original file may be required later for:</p><ul><li>Reprocessing.</li><li>Document verification.</li><li>OCR improvements.</li><li>Auditing.</li><li>Version comparison.</li><li>Re-extraction using a different parser.</li></ul><h3>Why should we store original documents?</h3><p>Imagine you extracted a PDF using PyMuPDF, but later discovered that some tables were missing. If you only stored the extracted text, you might need to ask the user to upload the original PDF again. If the original file is preserved, you can process it again using another parser.</p><p>Benefits:</p><ul><li>Preserve original information.</li><li>Support reprocessing.</li><li>Maintain document history.</li><li>Support auditing.</li><li>Recover from extraction failures.</li><li>Compare different extraction methods.</li></ul><h3>Original document vs extracted document</h3><p><b>Original document:</b> the actual file uploaded by the user, for example employee_policy.pdf.</p><p><b>Extracted document:</b> the text and information extracted from the original file.</p><pre>{\n  \"document_id\": \"doc_001\",\n  \"content\": \"Employees are eligible for annual leave.\",\n  \"metadata\": {\n    \"source\": \"employee_policy.pdf\",\n    \"page_number\": 1\n  }\n}</pre><h3>Recommended storage approach</h3><p>Store the original document separately from its extracted content.</p><pre>RAG STORAGE\n│\n├── original_documents/\n│   └── employee_policy.pdf\n│\n├── extracted_documents/\n│   └── employee_policy.jsonl\n│\n└── metadata/\n    └── employee_policy_metadata.json</pre><h3>Important considerations</h3><ul><li>Never overwrite original documents accidentally.</li><li>Maintain file checksums.</li><li>Track upload timestamps.</li><li>Track document ownership.</li><li>Restrict access to sensitive documents.</li><li>Maintain backup policies.</li><li>Consider encryption at rest.</li><li>Validate files before storing them.</li></ul><h3>Practical exercise</h3><p>Create a Python application that:</p><ol><li>Accepts a PDF file.</li><li>Stores the original file.</li><li>Generates a document ID.</li><li>Calculates its SHA-256 checksum.</li><li>Stores basic file metadata.</li><li>Creates a record linking the original file to its document ID.</li></ol>" },
      { id: "p4-local", title: "Local file storage", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Local folders, JSONL and SQLite are enough for learning and small prototypes.</p><h3>What is local file storage?</h3><p>Local file storage means saving documents directly on the computer or server where your application runs.</p><pre>rag-project/\n│\n├── storage/\n│   ├── originals/\n│   │   ├── policy.pdf\n│   │   └── handbook.docx\n│   │\n│   ├── extracted/\n│   │   ├── policy.jsonl\n│   │   └── handbook.jsonl\n│   │\n│   └── metadata/\n│       ├── policy.json\n│       └── handbook.json</pre><h3>Why use local storage?</h3><p>Local storage is useful when:</p><ul><li>Learning RAG.</li><li>Building prototypes.</li><li>Developing locally.</li><li>Testing document loaders.</li><li>Processing small datasets.</li><li>Running offline applications.</li></ul><p>Advantages:</p><ul><li>Simple implementation.</li><li>No cloud storage costs.</li><li>Easy debugging.</li><li>Direct filesystem access.</li><li>No external storage dependency.</li></ul><p>Limitations:</p><ul><li>Limited by disk capacity.</li><li>Difficult to share across multiple servers.</li><li>Requires backup management.</li><li>Server failures can cause data loss.</li><li>Scaling requires additional infrastructure.</li><li>File permissions must be managed carefully.</li></ul><h3>Save files using Python</h3><pre>from pathlib import Path\nimport shutil\n\nsource = Path(\"sample.pdf\")\n\ndestination = Path(\"storage/originals\")\n\ndestination.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n\nshutil.copy2(\n    source,\n    destination / source.name\n)</pre><p>Explanation:</p><ul><li><code>Path()</code> creates a filesystem path.</li><li><code>mkdir()</code> creates the directory.</li><li><code>parents=True</code> creates missing parent folders.</li><li><code>copy2()</code> copies the original file and preserves available file metadata.</li></ul><h3>Read and write extracted content</h3><pre>import json\n\ndocument = {\n    \"document_id\": \"doc_001\",\n    \"content\": \"Introduction to RAG\",\n    \"metadata\": {\n        \"source\": \"sample.pdf\"\n    }\n}\n\nwith open(\"document.json\", \"w\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as file:\n    json.dump(document, file, ensure_ascii=False, indent=4)</pre><h3>JSON vs JSONL storage</h3><h4><b>JSON</b></h4><p>Stores one complete JSON structure. Useful for individual document records, configuration and metadata.</p><h4><b>JSONL</b></h4><p>Stores multiple JSON records, one per line. Useful for large document collections, streaming, incremental processing and batch ingestion.</p><pre>{\"document_id\":\"doc_001\",\"content\":\"RAG introduction\"}\n{\"document_id\":\"doc_002\",\"content\":\"Embedding introduction\"}\n{\"document_id\":\"doc_003\",\"content\":\"Retrieval introduction\"}</pre><h3>When should you use local storage?</h3><p>For your initial RAG learning projects, local storage is a good starting point. You can use:</p><ul><li>Local folders for original files.</li><li>JSONL for extracted documents.</li><li>SQLite for document metadata.</li></ul><p>This combination is enough to build a small but functional ingestion system.</p><h3>Practical exercise</h3><p>Create a local document storage manager with functions to:</p><ol><li>Save original documents.</li><li>Read stored documents.</li><li>Save extracted content.</li><li>Retrieve document information.</li><li>List all stored documents.</li><li>Calculate file checksums.</li></ol>" },
      { id: "p4-cloud", title: "Cloud storage (AWS S3, Azure Blob)", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Object storage in the cloud gives every server shared, durable access to your files.</p><h3>What is cloud storage?</h3><p>Cloud storage allows applications to store files on remote infrastructure managed by a cloud provider. Instead of storing documents on your local machine or application server, you store them in cloud storage services.</p><p>Popular services: AWS S3, Azure Blob Storage, Google Cloud Storage.</p><h3>Why do we need cloud storage?</h3><p>Imagine building a RAG application for 500 companies. Each company uploads thousands of documents, and your application servers may be running on multiple machines. If documents are stored only on one server, other servers may not be able to access them.</p><p>Cloud object storage provides a centralized storage layer accessible to authorized application components.</p><p>Advantages:</p><ul><li>High durability.</li><li>Scalable storage capacity.</li><li>Access from multiple servers.</li><li>Lifecycle policies.</li><li>Versioning support.</li><li>Encryption options.</li><li>Backup and replication features.</li></ul><h3>What is AWS S3?</h3><p>AWS S3 stands for Amazon Simple Storage Service. It is an object storage service used to store files and other unstructured data.</p><h4><b>Bucket</b></h4><p>A container for storing objects. Example: rag-production-documents</p><h4><b>Object</b></h4><p>An individual stored file. Example: employee_policy.pdf</p><h4><b>Object key</b></h4><p>The unique name or path used to identify an object inside a bucket. Example: tenant_001/documents/employee_policy.pdf</p><h4><b>Metadata</b></h4><p>Additional information associated with an object.</p><h4><b>Version ID</b></h4><p>An identifier for a particular version of an object when bucket versioning is enabled.</p><h3>Example S3 architecture</h3><pre>AWS S3 BUCKET\n│\n├── tenant_001/\n│   ├── originals/\n│   │   ├── policy.pdf\n│   │   └── handbook.docx\n│   │\n│   └── processed/\n│       ├── policy.jsonl\n│       └── handbook.jsonl\n│\n├── tenant_002/\n│   ├── originals/\n│   └── processed/\n│\n└── shared/\n    └── public_documents/</pre><p>This is an example of organizing objects using key prefixes. S3 itself is a flat object store, not a traditional filesystem with actual directories.</p><h3>Upload a file to AWS S3</h3><p>Install:</p><pre>uv add boto3</pre><pre>import boto3\n\ns3 = boto3.client(\"s3\")\n\ns3.upload_file(\n    \"sample.pdf\",\n    \"rag-production-documents\",\n    \"tenant_001/originals/sample.pdf\"\n)</pre><h3>Download a file from S3</h3><pre>s3.download_file(\n    \"rag-production-documents\",\n    \"tenant_001/originals/sample.pdf\",\n    \"downloaded_sample.pdf\"\n)</pre><h3>List objects</h3><pre>response = s3.list_objects_v2(\n    Bucket=\"rag-production-documents\",\n    Prefix=\"tenant_001/\"\n)\n\nfor obj in response.get(\"Contents\", []):\n    print(obj[\"Key\"])</pre><p>For large collections, use pagination instead of relying on a single response.</p><h3>What is Azure Blob Storage?</h3><p>Azure Blob Storage is Microsoft's cloud object storage service. It is designed to store large amounts of unstructured data.</p><ul><li><b>Storage account</b>: the main Azure storage resource.</li><li><b>Container</b>: a logical container for blobs.</li><li><b>Blob</b>: an individual stored object.</li></ul><pre>Storage Account\n│\n└── rag-documents\n    │\n    ├── tenant_001/\n    │   ├── policy.pdf\n    │   └── handbook.docx\n    │\n    └── tenant_002/\n        └── report.pdf</pre><h3>Upload to Azure Blob Storage</h3><p>Install:</p><pre>uv add azure-storage-blob</pre><pre>from azure.storage.blob import BlobServiceClient\n\nconnection_string = \"YOUR_CONNECTION_STRING\"\n\nservice = BlobServiceClient.from_connection_string(\n    connection_string\n)\n\ncontainer = service.get_container_client(\n    \"rag-documents\"\n)\n\nblob = container.get_blob_client(\n    \"tenant_001/policy.pdf\"\n)\n\nwith open(\"policy.pdf\", \"rb\") as file:\n    blob.upload_blob(\n        file,\n        overwrite=True\n    )</pre><div class=\"callout\"><b>Note</b>Use secure credentials such as IAM roles, managed identities or a secrets manager rather than hardcoding production credentials.</div><h3>AWS S3 vs Azure Blob Storage</h3><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>AWS S3</th><th>Azure Blob</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Provider</td><td>AWS</td><td>Microsoft Azure</td></tr><tr><td>Storage type</td><td>Object storage</td><td>Object storage</td></tr><tr><td>Container</td><td>Bucket</td><td>Container</td></tr><tr><td>Stored item</td><td>Object</td><td>Blob</td></tr><tr><td>Access management</td><td>IAM policies</td><td>Azure RBAC</td></tr><tr><td>Versioning</td><td>Supported</td><td>Supported</td></tr><tr><td>Lifecycle policies</td><td>Supported</td><td>Supported</td></tr><tr><td>Encryption</td><td>Supported</td><td>Supported</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Important cloud storage concepts</h3><ul><li>IAM and access policies.</li><li>Encryption at rest.</li><li>Encryption in transit.</li><li>Bucket versioning.</li><li>Object lifecycle.</li><li>Replication.</li><li>Retention policies.</li><li>Signed URLs.</li><li>Multipart uploads.</li><li>Storage classes.</li><li>Backup and recovery.</li></ul><h3>Practical exercise</h3><p>Create an AWS S3 bucket and build a Python application that:</p><ol><li>Uploads PDF files.</li><li>Lists uploaded documents.</li><li>Downloads selected files.</li><li>Organizes documents using tenant prefixes.</li><li>Retrieves object metadata.</li><li>Handles missing files and upload failures.</li></ol>" },
      { id: "p4-extracted", title: "Extracted text storage", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Store extracted text on its own so you never parse the same file twice.</p><h3>What is extracted text storage?</h3><p>Extracted text storage means storing the content retrieved from original documents after loading, parsing and preprocessing.</p><p>Original file: employee_policy.pdf. Extracted content: \"Employees are eligible for 20 days of annual leave.\" This extracted content should be stored independently from the original file.</p><h3>Why store extracted text?</h3><p>Extracting the same document repeatedly can consume time and processing resources. By storing extracted content, we can:</p><ul><li>Avoid repeated parsing.</li><li>Resume interrupted pipelines.</li><li>Perform chunking later.</li><li>Generate embeddings later.</li><li>Reprocess only when required.</li><li>Compare extraction results.</li></ul><h3>Storage options</h3><ul><li><b>JSON</b>: suitable for individual documents.</li><li><b>JSONL</b>: suitable for document collections and incremental processing.</li><li><b>SQL database</b>: suitable for structured document records and relationships.</li><li><b>NoSQL database</b>: suitable for flexible document-oriented records.</li><li><b>Object storage</b>: suitable for large extracted files and datasets.</li></ul><h3>Example extracted document</h3><pre>{\n  \"document_id\": \"doc_001\",\n  \"content\": \"Employees are eligible for annual leave.\",\n  \"metadata\": {\n    \"source\": \"employee_policy.pdf\",\n    \"page_number\": 1,\n    \"source_type\": \"pdf\"\n  }\n}</pre><h3>Store multiple documents in JSONL</h3><pre>import json\n\ndocuments = [\n    {\n        \"document_id\": \"doc_001\",\n        \"content\": \"Introduction to RAG\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"document_id\": \"doc_002\",\n        \"content\": \"Introduction to embeddings\"\n    }\n]\n\nwith open(\n    \"documents.jsonl\",\n    \"w\",\n    encoding=\"utf-8\"\n) as file:\n\n    for document in documents:\n        file.write(\n            json.dumps(document, ensure_ascii=False) + \"\\n\"\n        )</pre><h3>Extracted text vs chunked text</h3><p>These are different storage stages.</p><p><b>Extracted text:</b> the complete extracted content of a document or page.</p><p><b>Chunked text:</b> smaller pieces created from extracted content.</p><p>Complete extracted document: \"RAG combines retrieval and generation. Embeddings represent text as vectors. A retriever finds relevant information.\"</p><p>After chunking:</p><pre>[\n  {\n    \"chunk_id\": \"chunk_001\",\n    \"content\": \"RAG combines retrieval and generation.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"chunk_id\": \"chunk_002\",\n    \"content\": \"Embeddings represent text as vectors.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"chunk_id\": \"chunk_003\",\n    \"content\": \"A retriever finds relevant information.\"\n  }\n]</pre><p>Store these as separate logical processing artifacts.</p><h3>Practical exercise</h3><p>Build an extracted-text storage module that:</p><ul><li>Saves extracted text.</li><li>Retrieves documents by ID.</li><li>Lists documents.</li><li>Stores page-level information.</li><li>Exports records to JSONL.</li></ul>" },
      { id: "p4-metadata", title: "Document metadata storage", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Keep document facts in a database so you can query them without opening any files.</p><h3>What is document metadata storage?</h3><p>Document metadata storage means maintaining information that describes documents separately from their main content.</p><pre>{\n  \"document_id\": \"doc_001\",\n  \"filename\": \"employee_policy.pdf\",\n  \"file_type\": \"pdf\",\n  \"file_size\": 102400,\n  \"uploaded_by\": \"user_001\",\n  \"tenant_id\": \"tenant_001\",\n  \"status\": \"processed\"\n}</pre><h3>Why store metadata separately?</h3><p>Suppose you have 100,000 documents and you want to find:</p><ul><li>All PDFs.</li><li>Documents uploaded by a specific user.</li><li>Documents belonging to one tenant.</li><li>Documents that failed processing.</li><li>Documents uploaded during the last month.</li></ul><p>It is more efficient to query structured metadata than to open every original document.</p><h3>Metadata storage options</h3><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Storage</th><th>Suitable for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>JSON files</td><td>Small projects</td></tr><tr><td>SQLite</td><td>Local applications</td></tr><tr><td>PostgreSQL</td><td>Production relational metadata</td></tr><tr><td>MySQL</td><td>Relational metadata</td></tr><tr><td>MongoDB</td><td>Flexible document records</td></tr><tr><td>DynamoDB</td><td>Managed key-value/document workloads</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Example PostgreSQL schema</h3><pre>CREATE TABLE documents (\n    document_id UUID PRIMARY KEY,\n    tenant_id VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL,\n    filename VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL,\n    file_type VARCHAR(50),\n    original_storage_key TEXT,\n    extracted_storage_key TEXT,\n    content_hash VARCHAR(64),\n    status VARCHAR(30),\n    created_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT NOW()\n);</pre><p>This table stores document-level information. The actual PDF does not need to be stored inside this table.</p><h3>Document metadata vs document content</h3><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Metadata</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Document ID</td><td>Actual extracted text</td></tr><tr><td>Filename</td><td>Paragraphs</td></tr><tr><td>File type</td><td>Tables</td></tr><tr><td>Upload date</td><td>Document content</td></tr><tr><td>Tenant ID</td><td>Extracted information</td></tr><tr><td>Storage location</td><td>Text sections</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Metadata indexing</h3><p>Database indexes help retrieve records efficiently.</p><pre>CREATE INDEX idx_documents_tenant\nON documents(tenant_id);\n\nCREATE INDEX idx_documents_status\nON documents(status);</pre><p>Use indexes according to your query patterns rather than indexing every column.</p><h3>Practical exercise</h3><p>Create a PostgreSQL database with a documents table. Implement:</p><ol><li>Insert document metadata.</li><li>Retrieve by document ID.</li><li>Retrieve by tenant ID.</li><li>Filter by file type.</li><li>Filter by processing status.</li><li>Update metadata.</li></ol>" },
      { id: "p4-versions", title: "Document ID and version management", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> A stable document ID stays the same while versions change, and checksums tell you when a file changed.</p><h3>What is document ID management?</h3><p>Document ID management means maintaining unique and stable identifiers for documents throughout their lifecycle.</p><pre>doc_001\ndoc_002\ndoc_003</pre><p>A document ID allows the system to identify the same logical document across different processing stages.</p><h3>Why do we need stable document IDs?</h3><p>Suppose a user uploads employee_policy.pdf. You extract the content, generate chunks and create embeddings. Later, the user updates the same document. You should be able to identify the existing logical document and manage its updated version. Without stable identifiers, you may accidentally create duplicate document records.</p><h3>Document ID vs version ID vs chunk ID</h3><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>ID</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Document ID</td><td>Identifies the logical document</td></tr><tr><td>Version ID</td><td>Identifies a particular revision</td></tr><tr><td>Chunk ID</td><td>Identifies a particular chunk</td></tr></tbody></table></div><pre>Document: doc_001\n\nVersion 1:\n  version_id: ver_001\n  chunk_001\n  chunk_002\n\nVersion 2:\n  version_id: ver_002\n  chunk_001\n  chunk_002\n  chunk_003</pre><p>The document ID remains stable while its version changes.</p><h3>Document versioning</h3><p>Versioning means maintaining multiple revisions of a document: employee_policy.pdf – Version 1, Version 2, Version 3.</p><p>Why is it important?</p><ul><li>Track changes.</li><li>Restore older versions.</li><li>Compare documents.</li><li>Maintain audit history.</li><li>Identify outdated information.</li></ul><h3>Example version management table</h3><pre>CREATE TABLE document_versions (\n    version_id UUID PRIMARY KEY,\n    document_id UUID NOT NULL,\n    version_number INTEGER NOT NULL,\n    content_hash VARCHAR(64),\n    storage_key TEXT,\n    created_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT NOW(),\n\n    UNIQUE(document_id, version_number)\n);</pre><p>The <code>document_id</code> references the logical document record. In a complete database, define a foreign key referencing the documents table.</p><h3>Detect whether a document has changed</h3><p>Use content hashing.</p><pre>import hashlib\n\ndef calculate_file_hash(file_path):\n\n    sha256 = hashlib.sha256()\n\n    with open(file_path, \"rb\") as file:\n\n        for block in iter(\n            lambda: file.read(1024 * 1024),\n            b\"\"\n        ):\n            sha256.update(block)\n\n    return sha256.hexdigest()</pre><p>Compare the new hash with the previously stored hash.</p><ul><li>Same hash: file content is unchanged.</li><li>Different hash: file content has changed.</li></ul><h3>Versioning strategy</h3><p>A simple approach:</p><ol><li>Assign a stable document ID.</li><li>Generate a file checksum.</li><li>Check whether the document already exists.</li><li>Compare the checksum.</li><li>If unchanged, skip unnecessary processing.</li><li>If changed, create a new version.</li><li>Preserve the previous version.</li><li>Mark the new version as active.</li></ol><h3>Important considerations</h3><ul><li>Never reuse document IDs.</li><li>Keep historical version information.</li><li>Track the active version.</li><li>Preserve source information.</li><li>Use checksums for change detection.</li><li>Handle concurrent updates.</li><li>Keep chunk and embedding versions linked to the correct document version.</li></ul><h3>Practical exercise</h3><p>Build a version management system that:</p><ul><li>Accepts document uploads.</li><li>Generates document IDs.</li><li>Calculates checksums.</li><li>Detects modifications.</li><li>Creates new versions.</li><li>Lists document history.</li><li>Identifies the current active version.</li></ul>" },
      { id: "p4-update-delete", title: "Document update and deletion", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Updating means a new version and fresh embeddings; deleting means cleaning every place the document lives.</p><h3>What is document updating?</h3><p>Document updating means replacing or modifying an existing document's stored information when its source content changes.</p><p>Original policy: \"Employees receive 15 days of annual leave.\"</p><p>Updated policy: \"Employees receive 20 days of annual leave.\"</p><p>The RAG system must reflect the new information.</p><h3>Why is document updating important?</h3><p>Business documents change regularly: product prices, company policies, technical documentation, product manuals, customer information. If old content remains in the retrieval system, the LLM may return outdated information.</p><h3>Document update workflow</h3><ol><li>Receive the updated document.</li><li>Identify its logical document ID.</li><li>Calculate its new checksum.</li><li>Compare it with the existing version.</li><li>Create a new version.</li><li>Store the updated original file.</li><li>Extract and clean the new content.</li><li>Update the extracted-text records.</li><li>Regenerate affected chunks and embeddings.</li><li>Mark the new version as active.</li><li>Retire the old version from normal retrieval.</li></ol><h3>What happens to old embeddings?</h3><p>Suppose the original document has 10 chunks. After updating it, the new document contains 12 chunks. The old embeddings should not continue appearing as current information.</p><p>A reliable strategy is to associate every chunk and embedding with:</p><ul><li>Document ID.</li><li>Version ID.</li><li>Chunk ID.</li><li>Tenant ID, where applicable.</li></ul><p>Then deactivate or delete the old version's retrieval records after the new version is ready.</p><h3>What is document deletion?</h3><p>Document deletion means removing a document and its associated data from the application. Deleting only the original PDF is not enough. You may also need to remove:</p><ul><li>Extracted text.</li><li>Document metadata.</li><li>Chunk records.</li><li>Vector embeddings.</li><li>Cached content.</li><li>Search index entries.</li><li>Derived artifacts.</li></ul><h3>Deletion workflow</h3><pre>DELETE DOCUMENT REQUEST\n          |\n          v\n   Identify Document ID\n          |\n          v\n   Check Permissions\n          |\n          v\n   Mark Document as Deleting\n          |\n          v\n   Delete / Retire Chunks\n          |\n          v\n   Delete Vector Records\n          |\n          v\n   Delete Extracted Text\n          |\n          v\n   Delete Original Files\n          |\n          v\n   Update Metadata and Audit Log\n          |\n          v\n   VERIFY DELETION</pre><h3>Soft delete vs hard delete</h3><h4><b>Soft delete</b></h4><p>The document is marked as deleted but its data is retained temporarily or according to a retention policy.</p><pre>UPDATE documents\nSET deleted_at = NOW(),\n    status = 'deleted'\nWHERE document_id = '...';</pre><p>Advantages:</p><ul><li>Recovery.</li><li>Audit history.</li><li>Accidental deletion protection.</li></ul><p>Limitations:</p><ul><li>Storage remains occupied.</li><li>Queries must exclude deleted records.</li><li>It does not satisfy a requirement for complete data erasure.</li></ul><h4><b>Hard delete</b></h4><p>The document and its data are physically removed from the relevant storage systems.</p><pre>DELETE FROM documents\nWHERE document_id = '...';</pre><p>Actual hard deletion requires coordinated cleanup of related records and object storage, not just deleting one database row.</p><h3>Important considerations</h3><ul><li>Check authorization before deletion.</li><li>Avoid deleting another tenant's documents.</li><li>Handle partial failures.</li><li>Make deletion operations retryable.</li><li>Maintain audit records where legally permitted.</li><li>Consider backups and retention policies.</li><li>Ensure deleted data is excluded from retrieval immediately.</li><li>Account for asynchronous cleanup.</li></ul><h3>Practical exercise</h3><p>Build an application that:</p><ol><li>Uploads a document.</li><li>Updates its content.</li><li>Creates a new version.</li><li>Deactivates old retrieval records.</li><li>Soft-deletes a document.</li><li>Restores a soft-deleted document.</li><li>Hard-deletes a document and its associated records.</li></ol>" },
      { id: "p4-lifecycle", title: "Document lifecycle management", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> A document moves from upload to active, updated, archived and deleted, and each stage needs a status.</p><h3>What is document lifecycle management?</h3><p>Document lifecycle management is the process of managing a document from the moment it enters the system until it is updated, archived or deleted. A document does not simply get uploaded and forgotten. It passes through multiple stages.</p><h3>Document lifecycle stages</h3><ol><li><b>Created / uploaded</b>: the user uploads a document.</li><li><b>Stored</b>: the original document is stored.</li><li><b>Processing</b>: the document is loaded and parsed.</li><li><b>Extracted</b>: text and other relevant content are extracted.</li><li><b>Cleaned</b>: unnecessary content is removed.</li><li><b>Validated</b>: the extracted document is checked for quality.</li><li><b>Active</b>: the document is available for downstream RAG processing and retrieval.</li><li><b>Updated</b>: a new version is created when the source changes.</li><li><b>Archived</b>: the document is retained but no longer used for normal retrieval.</li><li><b>Deleted</b>: the document is removed according to the deletion and retention policy.</li></ol><h3>Document lifecycle diagram</h3><pre>       UPLOADED\n           |\n           v\n        STORED\n           |\n           v\n       PROCESSING\n           |\n      +----+----+\n      |         |\n   SUCCESS    FAILED\n      |         |\n      v         v\n   VALIDATED   RETRY\n      |\n      v\n     ACTIVE\n      |\n  +---+---+\n  |       |\nUPDATE  ARCHIVE\n  |       |\n  v       v\nNEW     RETAINED\nVERSION\n  |\n  v\nACTIVE\n  |\n  v\nDELETED</pre><h3>Document status management</h3><p>A document should have a processing or lifecycle status.</p><pre>{\n  \"document_id\": \"doc_001\",\n  \"status\": \"active\",\n  \"version\": 2,\n  \"created_at\": \"2026-11-22\",\n  \"updated_at\": \"2026-11-22\"\n}</pre><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Status</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>uploaded</td><td>File received</td></tr><tr><td>stored</td><td>Original file saved</td></tr><tr><td>processing</td><td>Being processed</td></tr><tr><td>failed</td><td>Processing failed</td></tr><tr><td>validated</td><td>Passed validation</td></tr><tr><td>active</td><td>Available for use</td></tr><tr><td>updating</td><td>New version being processed</td></tr><tr><td>archived</td><td>Retained but inactive</td></tr><tr><td>deleting</td><td>Deletion in progress</td></tr><tr><td>deleted</td><td>Deletion completed</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>These statuses should be defined carefully so that processing status and lifecycle status do not become confused. A separate processing-status field may be appropriate.</p><h3>Retention policies</h3><p>A retention policy defines how long documents and their related data should be stored.</p><ul><li>Keep temporary documents for 30 days.</li><li>Keep archived reports for 7 years.</li><li>Delete expired documents after the retention period.</li></ul><p>Actual retention periods must follow applicable business, contractual and legal requirements.</p><h3>Storage lifecycle policies</h3><p>Cloud storage services can automatically move objects between storage classes or delete them after a defined period. For example, AWS S3 Lifecycle can transition old documents to lower-cost storage classes.</p><p>This is useful for historical documents, archived reports, inactive datasets and backup files.</p><h3>Practical exercise</h3><p>Design a lifecycle manager that:</p><ul><li>Tracks document status.</li><li>Maintains version history.</li><li>Supports archival.</li><li>Supports soft deletion.</li><li>Identifies expired documents.</li><li>Generates lifecycle reports.</li></ul>" },
      { id: "p4-architecture", title: "Storage architecture", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Use the right store for each kind of data instead of putting everything in one place.</p><h3>What is storage architecture?</h3><p>Storage architecture defines how different types of data are stored, organized, connected and accessed within an application. In RAG, storing everything in one place is usually not the best architecture. Different storage systems serve different purposes.</p><h3>Main storage components in RAG</h3><h4><b>1. Object storage</b></h4><p>Stores original files. Examples: AWS S3, Azure Blob, Google Cloud Storage.</p><h4><b>2. Relational database</b></h4><p>Stores structured information and relationships. Examples: PostgreSQL, MySQL, SQLite.</p><h4><b>3. Document / NoSQL database</b></h4><p>Stores flexible document records. Examples: MongoDB, DynamoDB.</p><h4><b>4. Extracted content storage</b></h4><p>Stores parsed and cleaned content. Examples: JSONL, PostgreSQL, object storage.</p><h4><b>5. Vector database</b></h4><p>Stores embeddings and searchable vector records. Examples: Qdrant, Milvus, Weaviate, Pinecone.</p><p>Vector databases are introduced here as part of the overall architecture. Embeddings and vector storage are studied in later phases.</p><h3>Recommended RAG storage architecture</h3><pre>                  USERS\n                    |\n                    v\n              RAG APPLICATION\n                    |\n                    v\n              INGESTION API\n                    |\n                    v\n             DOCUMENT MANAGER\n                    |\n          +---------+---------+\n          |                   |\n          v                   v\n    ORIGINAL FILES       METADATA DB\n     AWS S3 / Azure       PostgreSQL\n          |                   |\n          v                   |\n     DOCUMENT PARSER          |\n          |                   |\n          v                   |\n     TEXT EXTRACTION          |\n          |                   |\n          v                   |\n     DATA CLEANING            |\n          |                   |\n          v                   |\n   EXTRACTED CONTENT          |\n    JSONL / Object Store      |\n          |                   |\n          +---------+---------+\n                    |\n                    v\n              FUTURE STAGES\n          CHUNKING AND EMBEDDINGS\n                    |\n                    v\n              VECTOR DATABASE</pre><h3>Example: production RAG storage design</h3><p>Imagine a SaaS application with multiple tenants. Each tenant uploads documents independently.</p><ul><li><b>Original files:</b> AWS S3</li><li><b>Document metadata:</b> PostgreSQL</li><li><b>Extracted content:</b> S3 or PostgreSQL, depending on size and access patterns</li><li><b>Chunks:</b> PostgreSQL or a suitable document store</li><li><b>Embeddings:</b> Qdrant, Milvus, Weaviate or another vector-capable database</li><li><b>Processing jobs:</b> a message queue such as Redis-backed workers, RabbitMQ or AWS SQS</li></ul><h3>Multi-tenant storage organization</h3><p>Example S3 object keys:</p><pre>rag-bucket/\n│\n├── tenant_001/\n│   ├── documents/\n│   │   ├── doc_001/\n│   │   │   └── original.pdf\n│   │   └── doc_002/\n│   │       └── original.docx\n│   │\n│   └── processed/\n│\n├── tenant_002/\n│   ├── documents/\n│   └── processed/</pre><p>Each document should also have a corresponding tenant ID in the metadata database.</p><div class=\"callout\"><b>Important</b>Prefix-based organization is not a security boundary by itself. Enforce tenant isolation through authorization checks, IAM policies, database access controls and vector-query filters.</div><h3>Storage architecture considerations</h3><ul><li>Scalability.</li><li>Availability.</li><li>Durability.</li><li>Security.</li><li>Data isolation.</li><li>Storage cost.</li><li>Retrieval latency.</li><li>Backup and recovery.</li><li>Version management.</li><li>Monitoring.</li><li>Data retention.</li></ul><h3>Practical exercise</h3><p>Draw a storage architecture for a multi-tenant RAG application. Your architecture must contain:</p><ul><li>Original file storage.</li><li>Extracted content storage.</li><li>Metadata database.</li><li>Document version management.</li><li>Chunk storage.</li><li>Vector storage.</li><li>Backup strategy.</li></ul>" },
      { id: "p4-sql-nosql", title: "Choosing between SQL, NoSQL and object storage", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Object storage for files, SQL for metadata and relationships, NoSQL for flexible records.</p><h3>What is SQL?</h3><p>SQL stands for Structured Query Language. SQL databases store information in structured tables with rows and columns. Examples: PostgreSQL, MySQL, SQLite, Microsoft SQL Server.</p><h3>Why use SQL in RAG?</h3><p>SQL is useful for storing:</p><ul><li>Document metadata.</li><li>User information.</li><li>Tenant information.</li><li>Document versions.</li><li>Processing statuses.</li><li>Relationships between documents and chunks.</li></ul><pre>CREATE TABLE documents (\n    document_id UUID PRIMARY KEY,\n    tenant_id UUID NOT NULL,\n    filename TEXT NOT NULL,\n    file_type VARCHAR(20),\n    status VARCHAR(30),\n    created_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT NOW()\n);</pre><p>Advantages:</p><ul><li>Structured schema.</li><li>ACID transactions.</li><li>Relationships.</li><li>Joins.</li><li>Constraints.</li><li>Powerful querying.</li></ul><p>Limitations:</p><ul><li>Schema changes require planning.</li><li>Large unstructured files are generally better stored elsewhere.</li><li>Complex scaling patterns may require additional architecture.</li></ul><h3>What is NoSQL?</h3><p>NoSQL refers to database systems that use data models other than the traditional relational table model.</p><h4><b>1. Document databases</b></h4><p>Examples: MongoDB, Couchbase. Store flexible JSON-like documents.</p><h4><b>2. Key-value databases</b></h4><p>Examples: Redis, DynamoDB in key-value/document usage patterns. Store values using keys.</p><h4><b>3. Wide-column databases</b></h4><p>Examples: Cassandra, HBase. Designed for distributed data workloads.</p><h3>Why use NoSQL in RAG?</h3><p>NoSQL can be useful when:</p><ul><li>Document structures vary.</li><li>Flexible schemas are needed.</li><li>Application records are nested.</li><li>High-throughput access is required.</li><li>Distributed data storage is needed.</li></ul><pre>{\n  \"_id\": \"doc_001\",\n  \"tenant_id\": \"tenant_001\",\n  \"filename\": \"policy.pdf\",\n  \"metadata\": {\n    \"department\": \"HR\",\n    \"category\": \"policy\",\n    \"tags\": [\"employee\", \"leave\"]\n  }\n}</pre><p>Advantages:</p><ul><li>Flexible schema.</li><li>Natural support for nested data.</li><li>Different scaling models.</li><li>Useful document-oriented queries.</li></ul><p>Limitations:</p><ul><li>Relational operations may be less convenient.</li><li>Data consistency and transactions depend on the system.</li><li>Flexible schemas require good application-level validation.</li></ul><h3>What is object storage?</h3><p>Object storage stores data as objects, each with data, an identifier or key, and metadata. Examples: AWS S3, Azure Blob Storage, Google Cloud Storage.</p><p>It is designed for large amounts of unstructured data: PDF files, DOCX files, images, videos, backups and extracted datasets.</p><p>Advantages:</p><ul><li>Highly scalable.</li><li>Cost-effective for large files.</li><li>High durability options.</li><li>Cloud-native integrations.</li><li>Lifecycle management.</li></ul><p>Limitations:</p><ul><li>Not designed for complex relational queries.</li><li>Objects are generally accessed using keys.</li><li>Updating an object is different from updating a database row.</li><li>Access patterns and request costs need consideration.</li></ul><h3>SQL vs NoSQL vs object storage</h3><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>SQL</th><th>NoSQL</th><th>Object Storage</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Data model</td><td>Tables</td><td>Flexible models</td><td>Objects</td></tr><tr><td>Schema</td><td>Usually predefined</td><td>Often flexible</td><td>Object metadata</td></tr><tr><td>Relationships</td><td>Strong support</td><td>Varies by system</td><td>Not relational</td></tr><tr><td>Large files</td><td>Generally unsuitable</td><td>Depends on system</td><td>Excellent</td></tr><tr><td>Transactions</td><td>Strong support</td><td>Depends on system</td><td>Different consistency model</td></tr><tr><td>Complex queries</td><td>Excellent</td><td>Varies</td><td>Limited</td></tr><tr><td>Best use</td><td>Metadata and relationships</td><td>Flexible records</td><td>Original files</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>What should you choose for RAG?</h3><p>A practical starting architecture:</p><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Requirement</th><th>Recommended storage</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Original PDF/DOCX</td><td>AWS S3 or local storage</td></tr><tr><td>Extracted documents</td><td>JSONL or object storage</td></tr><tr><td>Document metadata</td><td>PostgreSQL</td></tr><tr><td>User and tenant information</td><td>PostgreSQL</td></tr><tr><td>Flexible application records</td><td>MongoDB, when justified</td></tr><tr><td>Embeddings</td><td>Vector database</td></tr><tr><td>Temporary job data</td><td>Redis or a queue system</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Why PostgreSQL is a good starting choice</h3><p>For many RAG projects, PostgreSQL is a strong default for metadata and relational records because it supports:</p><ul><li>Structured document records.</li><li>Transactions.</li><li>Indexes.</li><li>Foreign keys.</li><li>JSONB.</li><li>Full-text search.</li><li>Vector search through the pgvector extension.</li></ul><p>This means you can initially use PostgreSQL for metadata and even vector search without introducing multiple database systems immediately. You can move to a dedicated vector database when scale, workload or operational requirements justify it.</p><h3>Practical exercise</h3><p>Create three storage experiments:</p><h4><b>Experiment 1 – SQL</b></h4><ul><li>Store document metadata in PostgreSQL.</li><li>Retrieve documents by tenant ID.</li><li>Update document status.</li></ul><h4><b>Experiment 2 – NoSQL</b></h4><ul><li>Store flexible document metadata in MongoDB.</li><li>Query nested metadata.</li><li>Update document records.</li></ul><h4><b>Experiment 3 – Object storage</b></h4><ul><li>Upload original documents to AWS S3.</li><li>Download files.</li><li>List objects.</li><li>Manage object versions.</li></ul><p>Compare implementation complexity, query capabilities, cost and performance.</p>" },
      { id: "p4-project", title: "Final project: document storage and management system", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Build a Python app that manages originals, extracted content, metadata and versions.</p><h3>Project objective</h3><p>Build a Python application that manages original documents, extracted content, metadata and document versions. This project should connect the outputs of Phases 01, 02 and 03.</p><h3>Technology stack</h3><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>Technology</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Language</td><td>Python</td></tr><tr><td>Package manager</td><td>uv</td></tr><tr><td>Original storage</td><td>Local filesystem initially</td></tr><tr><td>Metadata database</td><td>PostgreSQL</td></tr><tr><td>Extracted content</td><td>JSONL</td></tr><tr><td>Validation</td><td>Pydantic</td></tr><tr><td>File identification</td><td>hashlib</td></tr><tr><td>Document IDs</td><td>UUID</td></tr><tr><td>Cloud storage extension</td><td>AWS S3</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Project structure</h3><pre>rag-storage-management/\n│\n├── storage/\n│   ├── originals/\n│   ├── extracted/\n│   ├── archived/\n│   └── temporary/\n│\n├── src/\n│   ├── storage/\n│   │   ├── local_storage.py\n│   │   ├── s3_storage.py\n│   │   └── storage_manager.py\n│   │\n│   ├── database/\n│   │   ├── connection.py\n│   │   ├── document_repository.py\n│   │   └── version_repository.py\n│   │\n│   ├── documents/\n│   │   ├── document_service.py\n│   │   ├── version_manager.py\n│   │   └── lifecycle_manager.py\n│   │\n│   ├── utils/\n│   │   ├── id_generator.py\n│   │   └── checksum.py\n│   │\n│   ├── models/\n│   │   └── document.py\n│   │\n│   └── main.py\n│\n├── migrations/\n├── tests/\n├── pyproject.toml\n└── README.md</pre><h3>Required features</h3><ol><li>Upload original documents.</li><li>Store files locally.</li><li>Generate document IDs.</li><li>Calculate SHA-256 checksums.</li><li>Store extracted text.</li><li>Store metadata in PostgreSQL.</li><li>Support multiple tenants.</li><li>Retrieve documents by ID.</li><li>Update documents.</li><li>Create new document versions.</li><li>Maintain document history.</li><li>Soft-delete documents.</li><li>Hard-delete documents and associated data.</li><li>Archive old documents.</li><li>Track document lifecycle status.</li><li>Support cloud storage as an optional extension.</li></ol><h3>Expected database design</h3><h4><b>Documents table</b></h4><pre>CREATE TABLE documents (\n    document_id UUID PRIMARY KEY,\n    tenant_id UUID NOT NULL,\n    filename TEXT NOT NULL,\n    file_type VARCHAR(20),\n    current_version_id UUID,\n    status VARCHAR(30),\n    created_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT NOW(),\n    updated_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT NOW(),\n    deleted_at TIMESTAMPTZ\n);</pre><h4><b>Document versions table</b></h4><pre>CREATE TABLE document_versions (\n    version_id UUID PRIMARY KEY,\n    document_id UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES documents(document_id),\n    version_number INTEGER NOT NULL,\n    original_storage_key TEXT NOT NULL,\n    extracted_storage_key TEXT,\n    content_hash VARCHAR(64),\n    created_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT NOW(),\n\n    UNIQUE(document_id, version_number)\n);</pre><h4><b>Document processing table</b></h4><pre>CREATE TABLE document_processing (\n    processing_id UUID PRIMARY KEY,\n    document_id UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES documents(document_id),\n    version_id UUID NOT NULL REFERENCES document_versions(version_id),\n    status VARCHAR(30),\n    error_message TEXT,\n    started_at TIMESTAMPTZ,\n    completed_at TIMESTAMPTZ\n);</pre><h3>Project completion criteria</h3><ul><li>Original files are stored separately.</li><li>Extracted content is independently accessible.</li><li>Metadata is stored in a database.</li><li>Documents have stable IDs.</li><li>Versions are tracked.</li><li>Updates do not accidentally overwrite history.</li><li>Deletion is coordinated across storage systems.</li><li>Tenant access is isolated.</li><li>Processing status is maintained.</li><li>Storage operations are tested.</li></ul>" },
      { id: "p4-revision", title: "Revision checklist", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Twenty-two questions to answer before moving on.</p><p>Before moving forward, make sure you can explain:</p><ol><li>Why should original documents be stored?</li><li>What is local file storage?</li><li>What is object storage?</li><li>What is AWS S3?</li><li>What is Azure Blob Storage?</li><li>What is a bucket, object and object key?</li><li>Why store extracted text separately?</li><li>What is JSON vs JSONL?</li><li>Why store metadata in a database?</li><li>What is a stable document ID?</li><li>What is document versioning?</li><li>What is checksum-based change detection?</li><li>How do you update an existing document?</li><li>What is soft delete vs hard delete?</li><li>What is document lifecycle management?</li><li>What is storage architecture?</li><li>What is multi-tenant storage?</li><li>What is SQL?</li><li>What is NoSQL?</li><li>What is object storage?</li><li>When should you use PostgreSQL instead of MongoDB?</li><li>Why might you use a vector database separately?</li></ol>" },
      { id: "p4-next", title: "Next: document chunking", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> With storage in place, the next step is splitting documents into chunks for retrieval.</p><p>Once the original documents, extracted content and metadata are properly managed, we can move toward preparing smaller pieces of text for semantic retrieval.</p><p>Upcoming topics:</p><ol><li>What is document chunking?</li><li>Why is chunking required in RAG?</li><li>Document-level vs chunk-level processing</li><li>Fixed-size chunking</li><li>Character-based chunking</li><li>Token-based chunking</li><li>Sentence-based chunking</li><li>Paragraph-based chunking</li><li>Recursive character text splitting</li><li>Semantic chunking</li><li>Structure-aware chunking</li><li>Chunk size selection</li><li>Chunk overlap</li><li>Metadata inheritance</li><li>Parent-child chunking</li><li>Chunk quality evaluation</li></ol><div class=\"callout\"><b>Important</b>Storage is not just about saving files. A good RAG storage architecture must support document updates, version tracking, tenant isolation, efficient retrieval, recovery and deletion. These capabilities become especially important when moving from a local learning project to a production RAG application.</div>" },
      { id: "p5-what", title: "What is chunking?", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Chunking splits a large document into smaller pieces that can be embedded and retrieved on their own.</p><h3>Definition</h3><p>Chunking is the process of dividing a large document into smaller pieces of text called chunks. Each chunk contains a portion of the original document. These chunks are later converted into embeddings and stored in a vector database or another retrieval index.</p><p>Original document: \"RAG stands for Retrieval-Augmented Generation. It combines information retrieval with language generation. Embeddings represent text as numerical vectors. Vector databases store and retrieve these vectors.\"</p><p>After chunking:</p><ul><li>Chunk 1: \"RAG stands for Retrieval-Augmented Generation. It combines information retrieval with language generation.\"</li><li>Chunk 2: \"Embeddings represent text as numerical vectors.\"</li><li>Chunk 3: \"Vector databases store and retrieve these vectors.\"</li></ul><p>Each chunk can be processed and retrieved independently.</p><h3>What is a chunk?</h3><p>A chunk is a smaller unit of information extracted from a larger document. A chunk generally contains:</p><ul><li>Chunk ID.</li><li>Document ID.</li><li>Text content.</li><li>Metadata.</li><li>Position or sequence information.</li></ul><pre>{\n  \"chunk_id\": \"chunk_001\",\n  \"document_id\": \"doc_001\",\n  \"content\": \"RAG stands for Retrieval-Augmented Generation.\",\n  \"metadata\": {\n    \"source\": \"rag_intro.pdf\",\n    \"page_number\": 1\n  }\n}</pre><h3>Why is chunking an important RAG component?</h3><p>The quality of chunking directly affects what information the retrieval system can find. Suppose a document contains: \"Python is a programming language. It is widely used in AI development. Python supports libraries such as NumPy, Pandas and PyTorch.\"</p><p>If we split it incorrectly:</p><ul><li>Chunk 1: \"Python is a programming language. It is widely used in AI development. Python supports libraries such as\"</li><li>Chunk 2: \"NumPy, Pandas and PyTorch.\"</li></ul><p>The second chunk loses important context. A better strategy is to split the text at meaningful boundaries.</p><h3>Important concepts</h3><ul><li><b>Chunk</b>: a smaller piece of a document.</li><li><b>Chunk size</b>: the amount of content inside a chunk.</li><li><b>Chunk overlap</b>: content shared between consecutive chunks.</li><li><b>Chunk boundary</b>: the point where one chunk ends and another begins.</li><li><b>Chunk metadata</b>: information describing a chunk.</li><li><b>Parent document</b>: the original document from which chunks were created.</li></ul>" },
      { id: "p5-why", title: "Why chunking is required", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Chunking fits the context window, makes retrieval precise, and cuts token use.</p><h3>Context window limitations</h3><p>LLMs have a maximum context window. The context window defines how much input and output content a model can process within a request. Sending very large documents may exceed the available context window or consume too many tokens. Chunking helps retrieve only the necessary portions.</p><h3>Better information retrieval</h3><p>Suppose you have a 100-page document and a user asks: \"What is the company's work-from-home policy?\" Without chunking, retrieving the entire document is inefficient. With chunking, the retrieval system can find the specific section containing the work-from-home policy.</p><h3>Reduced token consumption</h3><p>Suppose the complete document is 100,000 tokens and the retrieved chunks are 2,000 tokens. Instead of sending the complete document, you send only the relevant content. This can significantly reduce context consumption.</p><h3>Improved semantic search</h3><p>Embeddings represent text as numerical vectors. A vector represents the semantic information of its input text. When large documents are embedded as a single unit, the resulting vector may represent multiple unrelated topics. Smaller, focused chunks can make semantic retrieval more precise.</p><p>However, chunks that are too small may lose context. The objective is to balance specificity and completeness.</p><h3>Easier document management</h3><p>Chunking makes it easier to:</p><ul><li>Retrieve specific sections.</li><li>Track document locations.</li><li>Update affected content.</li><li>Attach metadata.</li><li>Rank relevant information.</li><li>Control retrieval size.</li></ul><h3>Example</h3><pre>Chapter 1: Introduction to RAG\n\nRAG combines retrieval and generation.\n\nChapter 2: Embeddings\n\nEmbeddings represent text as vectors.\n\nChapter 3: Vector Databases\n\nVector databases store and retrieve embeddings.</pre><p>After chunking: Chunk 1 is Introduction to RAG, Chunk 2 is Embeddings, Chunk 3 is Vector Databases. Now each topic can be retrieved separately.</p><h3>Important consideration</h3><p>Chunking does not automatically improve retrieval. Poor chunking can create:</p><ul><li>Incomplete sentences.</li><li>Missing context.</li><li>Mixed topics.</li><li>Duplicate information.</li><li>Irrelevant retrieval results.</li></ul><p>The objective is to create chunks that represent meaningful units of information.</p>" },
      { id: "p5-levels", title: "Document-level vs chunk-level processing", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Document-level keeps whole documents as one unit; chunk-level gives focused, precise retrieval.</p><h3>What is document-level processing?</h3><p>Document-level processing means treating the complete document as one unit. A 50-page PDF is represented as one document containing all its extracted text.</p><pre>{\n  \"document_id\": \"doc_001\",\n  \"content\": \"Complete document content...\"\n}</pre><p>Advantages:</p><ul><li>Simple management.</li><li>Original document context remains available.</li><li>Fewer records.</li><li>Useful for document-level summaries and classification.</li></ul><p>Limitations:</p><ul><li>Large text representation.</li><li>Less precise retrieval.</li><li>May exceed embedding model input limits.</li><li>May combine unrelated topics.</li></ul><h3>What is chunk-level processing?</h3><p>Chunk-level processing means dividing the document into smaller units and managing those units separately.</p><pre>[\n  {\n    \"chunk_id\": \"chunk_001\",\n    \"content\": \"Introduction to RAG...\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"chunk_id\": \"chunk_002\",\n    \"content\": \"Embedding models...\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"chunk_id\": \"chunk_003\",\n    \"content\": \"Vector databases...\"\n  }\n]</pre><p>Advantages:</p><ul><li>More focused retrieval.</li><li>Smaller embedding inputs.</li><li>Easier semantic matching.</li><li>More granular metadata.</li><li>Better control over retrieval context.</li></ul><p>Limitations:</p><ul><li>More records.</li><li>More complex metadata management.</li><li>Possible loss of surrounding context.</li><li>More complex updates and deletions.</li></ul><h3>Comparison</h3><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Document-Level</th><th>Chunk-Level</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Processing unit</td><td>Complete document</td><td>Smaller text sections</td></tr><tr><td>Retrieval precision</td><td>Often lower</td><td>Can be higher</td></tr><tr><td>Number of records</td><td>Lower</td><td>Higher</td></tr><tr><td>Context</td><td>Complete document</td><td>Local context</td></tr><tr><td>Embedding input</td><td>Potentially very large</td><td>Controlled</td></tr><tr><td>Storage management</td><td>Simpler</td><td>More complex</td></tr><tr><td>Typical RAG usage</td><td>Document-level search</td><td>Detailed retrieval</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>When should you use each?</h3><p>Document-level processing suits document classification, summarization, document-level metadata and small documents.</p><p>Chunk-level processing suits question answering, semantic search, knowledge assistants and large documentation collections.</p><p>In many production RAG systems, both levels are maintained.</p>" },
      { id: "p5-fixed", title: "Fixed-size chunking", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Cut text into equal-size pieces: simple and predictable, but it can split words and sentences.</p><h3>What is fixed-size chunking?</h3><p>Fixed-size chunking divides text into pieces with approximately the same specified size. The size may be defined using characters, tokens or words.</p><p>Original text: \"RAG combines retrieval and generation. Embeddings represent text as vectors. Vector databases store embeddings.\"</p><p>If the chunk size is 50 characters:</p><ul><li>Chunk 1: \"RAG combines retrieval and generation. Embedd\"</li><li>Chunk 2: \"ings represent text as vectors. Vector databas\"</li><li>Chunk 3: \"es store embeddings.\"</li></ul><p>This example illustrates why naive fixed-size splitting can break words and sentences.</p><h3>Why use fixed-size chunking?</h3><ul><li>Simple implementation.</li><li>Easy to control.</li><li>Predictable chunk lengths.</li><li>Useful for experimentation.</li><li>Easy to benchmark.</li></ul><h3>Python example</h3><pre>def fixed_size_chunking(text, chunk_size):\n\n    chunks = []\n\n    for i in range(0, len(text), chunk_size):\n\n        chunk = text[i:i + chunk_size]\n\n        chunks.append(chunk)\n\n    return chunks\n\n\ntext = \"\"\"\nRAG combines retrieval and generation.\nEmbeddings represent text as vectors.\nVector databases store embeddings.\n\"\"\"\n\nchunks = fixed_size_chunking(text, 50)\n\nfor chunk in chunks:\n    print(chunk)</pre><h3>Advantages</h3><ul><li>Easy to implement.</li><li>Fast processing.</li><li>Predictable output size.</li><li>Useful as a baseline.</li></ul><h3>Limitations</h3><ul><li>Can split sentences.</li><li>Can split words.</li><li>Can separate questions from answers.</li><li>Can destroy document structure.</li><li>Does not understand semantic meaning.</li></ul><h3>Practical exercise</h3><p>Create a fixed-size chunker using 100, 300, 500 and 1,000 characters. Compare how each setting divides the same document.</p>" },
      { id: "p5-character", title: "Character-based chunking", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Split by a number of characters, optionally with overlap; easy, but characters are not tokens.</p><h3>What is character-based chunking?</h3><p>Character-based chunking divides text based on a specified number of characters. With a chunk size of 100 characters, every chunk contains up to 100 characters. This is one of the simplest chunking methods.</p><h3>Character count vs token count</h3><p>These are different measurements.</p><pre>text = \"Hello RAG\"\n\nprint(len(text))</pre><p>Output:</p><pre>9</pre><p>Token count depends on the tokenizer used by the language model. The same text may produce a different number of tokens depending on the tokenizer. For example:</p><ul><li>English words may sometimes correspond to one token.</li><li>Long or uncommon words may be split into multiple tokens.</li><li>Punctuation and whitespace can affect tokenization.</li><li>Telugu and other multilingual text may tokenize differently.</li></ul><h3>Character-based chunking with overlap</h3><pre>def character_chunking(\n    text,\n    chunk_size=100,\n    overlap=20\n):\n\n    if chunk_size &lt;= 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Chunk size must be positive\")\n\n    if overlap &lt; 0 or overlap &gt;= chunk_size:\n        raise ValueError(\"Overlap must be smaller than chunk size\")\n\n    chunks = []\n\n    start = 0\n\n    while start &lt; len(text):\n\n        end = start + chunk_size\n\n        chunks.append(text[start:end])\n\n        start += chunk_size - overlap\n\n    return chunks</pre><h3>Advantages</h3><ul><li>Easy to implement.</li><li>No external tokenizer required.</li><li>Predictable character limits.</li><li>Useful for simple text processing.</li></ul><h3>Limitations</h3><ul><li>Does not measure actual LLM tokens.</li><li>Can break words and sentences.</li><li>Different languages have different character distributions.</li><li>Not always suitable for model context limits.</li></ul><h3>Practical exercise</h3><p>Implement character-based chunking with 200, 500 and 1,000 characters. Use 10% overlap and compare the generated chunks.</p>" },
      { id: "p5-token", title: "Token-based chunking", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Split by token count so chunks respect the real limits of embedding models and LLMs.</p><h3>What is a token?</h3><p>A token is a unit of text processed by a language model. A token may represent:</p><ul><li>A complete word.</li><li>Part of a word.</li><li>Punctuation.</li><li>A character or character sequence.</li><li>Other text units.</li></ul><p>A tokenizer might split \"Retrieval-Augmented Generation\" into multiple tokens depending on the tokenizer vocabulary. There is no universal rule that one word equals one token.</p><h3>What is token-based chunking?</h3><p>Token-based chunking divides text according to a maximum token count rather than a character count. With a chunk size of 100 tokens, each chunk is designed to contain approximately 100 tokens or fewer, according to the selected tokenizer.</p><h3>Why use token-based chunking?</h3><p>LLMs and embedding models have token limits. A document may contain 2,000 characters but produce a different token count depending on its language and content. Token-based chunking gives better control over:</p><ul><li>Embedding model input limits.</li><li>LLM context windows.</li><li>Prompt size.</li><li>Token consumption.</li><li>Chunk size consistency.</li></ul><h3>Popular tokenizers</h3><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Tokenizer</th><th>Ecosystem / use</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>tiktoken</td><td>OpenAI-compatible tokenization</td></tr><tr><td>Hugging Face Tokenizers</td><td>Transformer models</td></tr><tr><td>SentencePiece</td><td>Many multilingual and generative models</td></tr><tr><td>Transformers AutoTokenizer</td><td>Model-specific tokenizers</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Always use the tokenizer appropriate to the target embedding model when accurate token limits matter.</p><h3>Install tiktoken</h3><pre>uv add tiktoken</pre><h3>Count tokens</h3><pre>import tiktoken\n\nencoding = tiktoken.get_encoding(\"cl100k_base\")\n\ntext = \"RAG combines retrieval and generation.\"\n\ntokens = encoding.encode(text)\n\nprint(tokens)\nprint(\"Token count:\", len(tokens))</pre><h3>Token-based chunking example</h3><pre>import tiktoken\n\nencoding = tiktoken.get_encoding(\"cl100k_base\")\n\n\ndef token_chunking(text, chunk_size=100):\n\n    token_ids = encoding.encode(text)\n\n    chunks = []\n\n    for i in range(0, len(token_ids), chunk_size):\n\n        chunk_tokens = token_ids[i:i + chunk_size]\n\n        chunk_text = encoding.decode(chunk_tokens)\n\n        chunks.append(chunk_text)\n\n    return chunks\n\n\ntext = \"RAG combines retrieval and generation. \" * 100\n\nchunks = token_chunking(text, chunk_size=100)\n\nfor chunk in chunks:\n    print(len(encoding.encode(chunk)))</pre><h3>Important considerations</h3><ul><li>Different models may use different tokenizers.</li><li>Token limits may include special tokens.</li><li>Some APIs reserve context for instructions and output.</li><li>Token-based boundaries can still split sentences.</li><li>Always check the actual embedding model's maximum input size.</li></ul><h3>Practical exercise</h3><p>Take a long technical document. Generate chunks using 100, 200, 300 and 500 tokens. Compare the resulting text and actual token counts.</p>" },
      { id: "p5-sentence", title: "Sentence-based chunking", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Keep complete sentences together, then group a few sentences into each chunk.</p><h3>What is sentence-based chunking?</h3><p>Sentence-based chunking divides documents using sentence boundaries. Instead of splitting after an arbitrary number of characters, it tries to keep complete sentences together.</p><p>Original: \"RAG combines retrieval and generation. Embeddings represent text as vectors. Vector databases store embeddings.\"</p><ul><li>Chunk 1: \"RAG combines retrieval and generation.\"</li><li>Chunk 2: \"Embeddings represent text as vectors.\"</li><li>Chunk 3: \"Vector databases store embeddings.\"</li></ul><h3>Why use sentence-based chunking?</h3><p>Sentences usually contain a complete thought. Keeping sentences intact can reduce the risk of breaking semantic relationships. Useful for articles, blogs, FAQs, knowledge base content and general explanatory documents.</p><h3>Popular sentence splitting tools</h3><ul><li>NLTK.</li><li>spaCy.</li><li>PySBD.</li><li>Python regular expressions for simple cases.</li></ul><h3>Install NLTK</h3><pre>uv add nltk</pre><h3>Sentence splitting example</h3><pre>import nltk\n\nnltk.download(\"punkt_tab\")\n\nfrom nltk.tokenize import sent_tokenize\n\ntext = \"\"\"\nRAG combines retrieval and generation.\nEmbeddings represent text as vectors.\nVector databases store embeddings.\n\"\"\"\n\nsentences = sent_tokenize(text)\n\nfor sentence in sentences:\n    print(sentence)</pre><h3>Group sentences into chunks</h3><p>Instead of creating one chunk for every sentence, combine multiple sentences.</p><pre>from nltk.tokenize import sent_tokenize\n\n\ndef sentence_chunking(text, sentences_per_chunk=3):\n\n    sentences = sent_tokenize(text)\n\n    chunks = []\n\n    for i in range(0, len(sentences), sentences_per_chunk):\n\n        group = sentences[i:i + sentences_per_chunk]\n\n        chunks.append(\" \".join(group))\n\n    return chunks</pre><h3>Advantages</h3><ul><li>Preserves sentence boundaries.</li><li>More readable chunks.</li><li>Better than arbitrary character splitting for many documents.</li><li>Simple to understand.</li></ul><h3>Limitations</h3><ul><li>Sentence lengths vary.</li><li>One sentence may be extremely long.</li><li>Context may span multiple sentences.</li><li>Sentence detection can fail with abbreviations, lists and multilingual text.</li></ul><h3>Practical exercise</h3><p>Create sentence-based chunks containing 2, 3 and 5 sentences. Compare retrieval usefulness for a sample FAQ dataset.</p>" },
      { id: "p5-paragraph", title: "Paragraph-based chunking", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Use paragraph boundaries, and split oversized paragraphs further.</p><h3>What is paragraph-based chunking?</h3><p>Paragraph-based chunking splits a document using paragraph boundaries. A paragraph generally contains multiple sentences discussing one related idea.</p><ul><li>Paragraph 1: \"RAG combines retrieval and generation. It allows an LLM to use external knowledge.\"</li><li>Paragraph 2: \"Embeddings convert text into numerical vectors. These vectors represent semantic information.\"</li><li>Paragraph 3: \"Vector databases store and retrieve embeddings using similarity search.\"</li></ul><p>Each paragraph can become a separate chunk.</p><h3>Why use paragraph-based chunking?</h3><p>Paragraphs usually represent a coherent section of information.</p><p>Advantages:</p><ul><li>Preserves related sentences.</li><li>Keeps explanations together.</li><li>Maintains readability.</li><li>Works well for articles and reports.</li></ul><h3>Python example</h3><pre>def paragraph_chunking(text):\n\n    paragraphs = text.split(\"\\n\\n\")\n\n    chunks = []\n\n    for paragraph in paragraphs:\n\n        paragraph = paragraph.strip()\n\n        if paragraph:\n            chunks.append(paragraph)\n\n    return chunks</pre><h3>What if a paragraph is too large?</h3><p>A paragraph may contain 1,500 words and exceed the embedding model's input limit. A practical solution is hierarchical splitting:</p><ol><li>Split by paragraphs.</li><li>Check each paragraph's token count.</li><li>Keep paragraphs within the target size.</li><li>Split oversized paragraphs into sentences.</li><li>Combine smaller adjacent paragraphs when appropriate.</li></ol><h3>Practical exercise</h3><p>Take a 10-page article. Extract its paragraphs and create paragraph-based chunks. Identify very short paragraphs, very large paragraphs and paragraphs containing multiple topics, then decide how to handle each case.</p>" },
      { id: "p5-recursive", title: "Recursive character chunking", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Try big boundaries first (paragraphs), then smaller ones, until each piece fits the size.</p><h3>What is recursive character chunking?</h3><p>Recursive character chunking is a splitting strategy that attempts to divide text using meaningful separators in a preferred order. Instead of immediately cutting text at a fixed character position, it first tries larger structural boundaries.</p><p>A common separator hierarchy:</p><ol><li>Paragraph boundary: <code>\"\\n\\n\"</code></li><li>Line boundary: <code>\"\\n\"</code></li><li>Sentence boundary: <code>\". \"</code></li><li>Word boundary: <code>\" \"</code></li><li>Character boundary: <code>\"\"</code></li></ol><p>If a text section is too large, the algorithm tries a smaller separator.</p><h3>Why use recursive chunking?</h3><p>Documents have different structures. Some contain paragraphs, sentences, lists or lines. Recursive splitting attempts to preserve as much structure as possible while respecting a target chunk size.</p><h3>Example</h3><pre>RAG combines retrieval and generation.\n\nEmbeddings represent text as vectors.\n\nVector databases store and retrieve embeddings.</pre><p>Suppose the target chunk size is 60 characters. The splitter first attempts paragraph boundaries. If a paragraph exceeds the limit, it tries smaller boundaries such as sentences or words.</p><h3>LangChain RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter</h3><p>Install:</p><pre>uv add langchain-text-splitters</pre><pre>from langchain_text_splitters import RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter\n\ntext = \"\"\"\nRAG combines retrieval and generation.\n\nEmbeddings represent text as vectors.\n\nVector databases store and retrieve embeddings.\n\"\"\"\n\nsplitter = RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter(\n    chunk_size=100,\n    chunk_overlap=20,\n    separators=[\"\\n\\n\", \"\\n\", \". \", \" \", \"\"]\n)\n\nchunks = splitter.split_text(text)\n\nfor chunk in chunks:\n    print(chunk)</pre><h3>Important parameters</h3><ul><li><b>chunk_size</b>: maximum target size, measured using the configured length function.</li><li><b>chunk_overlap</b>: amount of shared content between consecutive chunks.</li><li><b>separators</b>: the preferred boundaries used for splitting.</li><li><b>length_function</b>: the function used to measure chunk length. By default, character count is used.</li></ul><h3>Token-based recursive splitting</h3><p>You can configure a tokenizer-based length function when required.</p><pre>import tiktoken\n\nfrom langchain_text_splitters import RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter\n\nencoding = tiktoken.get_encoding(\"cl100k_base\")\n\nsplitter = RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter(\n    chunk_size=300,\n    chunk_overlap=50,\n    length_function=lambda text: len(encoding.encode(text))\n)</pre><h3>Advantages</h3><ul><li>Preserves paragraph and sentence boundaries when possible.</li><li>More flexible than fixed-size splitting.</li><li>Easy to configure.</li><li>Useful for general-purpose RAG systems.</li><li>Supports custom separators and length functions.</li></ul><h3>Limitations</h3><ul><li>Does not truly understand semantic meaning.</li><li>Separator selection affects output quality.</li><li>A chunk can still contain unrelated topics.</li><li>It may split tables or code incorrectly without custom handling.</li></ul><h3>Practical exercise</h3><p>Use RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter with chunk size 200 and overlap 20, chunk size 400 and overlap 50, and chunk size 800 and overlap 100. Compare the results using the same source document.</p>" },
      { id: "p5-semantic", title: "Semantic chunking", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Split where the meaning changes, found by comparing sentence embeddings with cosine similarity.</p><h3>What is semantic chunking?</h3><p>Semantic chunking divides text based on changes in meaning rather than relying only on character count or sentence boundaries. The goal is to keep semantically related sentences together.</p><ul><li>Sentence 1: \"RAG combines retrieval and generation.\"</li><li>Sentence 2: \"Retrieval finds relevant information from external sources.\"</li><li>Sentence 3: \"Embeddings convert text into numerical vectors.\"</li></ul><p>Sentences 1 and 2 discuss retrieval-augmented generation and retrieval. Sentence 3 introduces a related but different concept. A semantic chunker may place the first two sentences together and separate the third, depending on its similarity threshold.</p><h3>Why use semantic chunking?</h3><p>Fixed-size chunking may divide related information or combine unrelated topics. Semantic chunking attempts to create boundaries where the topic or meaning changes.</p><p>Potential benefits:</p><ul><li>Topic-focused chunks.</li><li>More coherent context.</li><li>Better preservation of semantic relationships.</li><li>Useful for complex technical documentation.</li></ul><h3>How does semantic chunking work?</h3><p>A common approach:</p><ol><li>Split the document into sentences.</li><li>Generate an embedding for each sentence.</li><li>Compare embeddings of neighboring sentences.</li><li>Calculate semantic similarity.</li><li>Identify significant changes in similarity.</li><li>Create chunk boundaries.</li><li>Combine related sentences into chunks.</li></ol><h3>Cosine similarity</h3><p>Cosine similarity measures the angle-based similarity between two vectors. For vectors A and B:</p><pre>Cosine Similarity = (A · B) / (||A|| × ||B||)</pre><p>The value is commonly interpreted as:</p><ul><li>Closer to 1: similar direction.</li><li>Around 0: little directional similarity.</li><li>Closer to -1: opposite direction.</li></ul><p>The interpretation depends on the embedding model and vector normalization.</p><h3>Semantic chunking workflow</h3><pre>          DOCUMENT\n              |\n              v\n       SPLIT INTO SENTENCES\n              |\n              v\n       GENERATE EMBEDDINGS\n              |\n              v\n       COMPARE ADJACENT\n          SENTENCES\n              |\n              v\n       CALCULATE SIMILARITY\n              |\n              v\n       DETECT TOPIC CHANGES\n              |\n              v\n        CREATE CHUNKS</pre><h3>Simple semantic similarity example</h3><p>Install:</p><pre>uv add sentence-transformers scikit-learn</pre><pre>from sentence_transformers import SentenceTransformer\nfrom sklearn.metrics.pairwise import cosine_similarity\n\nmodel = SentenceTransformer(\n    \"sentence-transformers/all-MiniLM-L6-v2\"\n)\n\nsentences = [\n    \"RAG combines retrieval and generation.\",\n    \"Retrieval finds relevant external information.\",\n    \"Embeddings represent text as vectors.\"\n]\n\nembeddings = model.encode(sentences)\n\nfor i in range(len(sentences) - 1):\n\n    similarity = cosine_similarity(\n        [embeddings[i]],\n        [embeddings[i + 1]]\n    )[0][0]\n\n    print(similarity)</pre><p>This example measures similarity between adjacent sentences. It does not implement a complete semantic chunker by itself.</p><h3>Considerations</h3><ul><li>Requires an embedding model.</li><li>More computationally expensive.</li><li>Similarity thresholds require tuning.</li><li>Sentence segmentation quality matters.</li><li>May produce inconsistent chunk sizes.</li><li>Semantic similarity does not always equal topical relevance.</li></ul><h3>Practical exercise</h3><p>Take a technical document containing at least five different topics. Implement semantic chunking using sentence embeddings. Compare its output with recursive character chunking and evaluate which method keeps related concepts together.</p>" },
      { id: "p5-sliding", title: "Sliding window chunking", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Overlapping windows keep context across chunk boundaries at the cost of duplicate text.</p><h3>What is sliding window chunking?</h3><p>Sliding window chunking divides text into fixed-size sections where each new chunk starts before the previous chunk ends. This creates overlapping windows of text.</p><p>Original: \"RAG combines retrieval and generation. Embeddings represent text as vectors. Vector databases store embeddings.\"</p><ul><li>Chunk 1: \"RAG combines retrieval and generation. Embeddings represent text\"</li><li>Chunk 2: \"Embeddings represent text as vectors. Vector databases store embeddings.\"</li></ul><p>Part of Chunk 1 appears again in Chunk 2. This shared information is called overlap.</p><h3>Why use sliding windows?</h3><p>Information near chunk boundaries may be lost when chunks are separated. Overlap helps preserve some surrounding context. Useful for long technical documents, explanations with connected sentences, and documents where important information spans boundaries.</p><h3>Sliding window example</h3><pre>def sliding_window_chunking(\n    text,\n    window_size=100,\n    stride=80\n):\n\n    if window_size &lt;= 0 or stride &lt;= 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Window size and stride must be positive\")\n\n    if stride &gt; window_size:\n        raise ValueError(\"Stride cannot exceed window size\")\n\n    chunks = []\n\n    for start in range(0, len(text), stride):\n\n        chunk = text[start:start + window_size]\n\n        if not chunk:\n            break\n\n        chunks.append(chunk)\n\n        if start + window_size &gt;= len(text):\n            break\n\n    return chunks</pre><h3>Window size vs stride</h3><ul><li><b>Window size</b>: the size of each chunk.</li><li><b>Stride</b>: the distance between the starting positions of consecutive chunks.</li></ul><pre>Overlap = Window Size - Stride</pre><p>Example: window size 500 characters, stride 400 characters, so overlap is 100 characters.</p><h3>Advantages</h3><ul><li>Preserves neighboring context.</li><li>Simple to implement.</li><li>Useful for continuous text.</li><li>Helps reduce boundary-related information loss.</li></ul><h3>Limitations</h3><ul><li>Creates duplicate content.</li><li>Increases storage and embedding costs.</li><li>May retrieve repeated information.</li><li>Does not understand topic boundaries.</li></ul><h3>Practical exercise</h3><p>Create sliding windows with window 500 and stride 400, window 500 and stride 350, and window 1,000 and stride 800. Compare overlap and the number of chunks produced.</p>" },
      { id: "p5-parent-child", title: "Parent-child chunking", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Search small child chunks for precision, then return the larger parent for context.</p><h3>What is parent-child chunking?</h3><p>Parent-child chunking is a hierarchical chunking approach. A large document section is divided into smaller child chunks. The larger section is called the parent, and the smaller pieces are called child chunks.</p><p>Parent: \"Chapter 2 – Embeddings. Embeddings represent text as vectors. They capture semantic information. Vector similarity helps retrieve related content.\"</p><ul><li>Child 1: \"Embeddings represent text as vectors.\"</li><li>Child 2: \"They capture semantic information.\"</li><li>Child 3: \"Vector similarity helps retrieve related content.\"</li></ul><h3>Why use parent-child chunking?</h3><p>Small chunks can improve retrieval precision but may lose surrounding context. Large chunks preserve more context but may be less precise. Parent-child chunking attempts to combine both benefits.</p><h3>How does it work?</h3><ol><li>Divide the original document into large parent sections.</li><li>Divide each parent into smaller child chunks.</li><li>Generate embeddings for child chunks.</li><li>Store child embeddings in the retrieval index.</li><li>Search for relevant child chunks.</li><li>Identify the corresponding parent.</li><li>Return the parent content or an appropriate surrounding section to the LLM.</li></ol><h3>Example structure</h3><pre>{\n  \"parent_id\": \"parent_001\",\n  \"content\": \"Complete section about embeddings.\",\n  \"children\": [\n    {\n      \"chunk_id\": \"child_001\",\n      \"content\": \"Embeddings represent text as vectors.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"chunk_id\": \"child_002\",\n      \"content\": \"Vector similarity helps retrieval.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}</pre><h3>Advantages</h3><ul><li>Fine-grained retrieval.</li><li>Larger contextual information.</li><li>Preserves section relationships.</li><li>Useful for complex documentation.</li><li>Reduces the need to embed large sections directly.</li></ul><h3>Limitations</h3><ul><li>More complex data management.</li><li>Requires parent-child relationships.</li><li>Parent retrieval can introduce irrelevant content.</li><li>More storage may be required.</li><li>Updates must maintain consistent relationships.</li></ul><h3>Practical exercise</h3><p>Create a document containing five sections. For each section:</p><ol><li>Create one parent.</li><li>Generate three to five children.</li><li>Assign unique IDs.</li><li>Maintain parent-child relationships.</li><li>Search using a child chunk.</li><li>Return the corresponding parent section.</li></ol>" },
      { id: "p5-size", title: "Chunk size selection", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> There is no best size: test several sizes on your own documents and questions.</p><h3>What is chunk size?</h3><p>Chunk size defines how much content a chunk can contain. It may be measured using characters, tokens, sentences or paragraphs. A chunk size of 300 tokens means each chunk is designed to contain approximately 300 tokens.</p><h3>Why does chunk size matter?</h3><p>Chunk size influences retrieval behavior.</p><h4><b>Small chunks</b></h4><p>Advantages:</p><ul><li>More focused information.</li><li>Less irrelevant context.</li><li>More granular retrieval.</li></ul><p>Disadvantages:</p><ul><li>May lose context.</li><li>Can split explanations.</li><li>Creates more records.</li></ul><h4><b>Large chunks</b></h4><p>Advantages:</p><ul><li>More surrounding context.</li><li>Fewer records.</li><li>May preserve complete explanations.</li></ul><p>Disadvantages:</p><ul><li>Can combine unrelated topics.</li><li>May retrieve unnecessary information.</li><li>Can consume more context tokens.</li></ul><h3>Example chunk sizes</h3><p>These are experimental starting points, not universal best practices.</p><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Chunk size</th><th>Possible application</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>100 tokens</td><td>Short facts or FAQs</td></tr><tr><td>200 tokens</td><td>Small knowledge sections</td></tr><tr><td>300 tokens</td><td>Focused technical content</td></tr><tr><td>500 tokens</td><td>General documentation</td></tr><tr><td>800 tokens</td><td>Longer explanations</td></tr><tr><td>1,000+ tokens</td><td>Large contextual sections</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>How to select chunk size</h3><p>Consider:</p><ol><li>Document structure.</li><li>Average paragraph length.</li><li>Embedding model token limit.</li><li>Retrieval requirements.</li><li>Query complexity.</li><li>LLM context window.</li><li>Storage and embedding cost.</li></ol><h3>Chunk size experiment</h3><p>Take the same document and generate chunks using 100, 200, 300, 500 and 800 tokens. Use the same embedding model and test questions for every configuration. Compare:</p><ul><li>Retrieval relevance.</li><li>Answer accuracy.</li><li>Context completeness.</li><li>Number of retrieved chunks.</li><li>Token consumption.</li></ul><div class=\"callout\"><b>Important</b>There is no universally best chunk size. The correct chunk size depends on the documents, queries, embedding model, retrieval strategy and downstream LLM.</div><h3>Practical exercise</h3><p>Build a chunk-size comparison experiment and prepare a report identifying which configuration performs best on your test dataset.</p>" },
      { id: "p5-overlap", title: "Chunk overlap selection", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Overlap shares text between neighbors to keep context, but too much wastes storage.</p><h3>What is chunk overlap?</h3><p>Chunk overlap is the amount of text shared between consecutive chunks.</p><ul><li>Chunk 1: \"RAG combines retrieval and generation. Embeddings represent text.\"</li><li>Chunk 2: \"Embeddings represent text. Vector databases store vectors.\"</li></ul><p>The sentence \"Embeddings represent text\" appears in both chunks. That is overlap.</p><h3>Why is overlap required?</h3><p>Suppose an important explanation starts near the end of Chunk 1 and continues into Chunk 2. Without overlap, the two pieces may become disconnected. Overlap preserves some of the surrounding context.</p><h3>Overlap example</h3><p>Chunk size is 500 tokens and overlap is 100 tokens. The next chunk starts approximately 400 tokens after the previous chunk's starting position.</p><pre>Chunk 1:\n[------------------------------------]\n0                                  500\n\nChunk 2:\n                    [------------------------------------]\n                   400                                  900\n\nShared region:\n                   [100 tokens]</pre><h3>Common overlap ratios</h3><p>Experimental examples, not fixed rules:</p><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Chunk size</th><th>Overlap</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>200</td><td>20</td></tr><tr><td>300</td><td>30</td></tr><tr><td>500</td><td>50</td></tr><tr><td>500</td><td>100</td></tr><tr><td>800</td><td>100</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Overlap calculation</h3><pre>Overlap percentage = (Overlap / Chunk Size) × 100</pre><p>Example: chunk size 500 tokens, overlap 50 tokens, so the overlap percentage is 10%.</p><h3>Too much vs too little overlap</h3><h4><b>Too little overlap:</b></h4><ul><li>May lose context across boundaries.</li><li>Can split related information.</li></ul><h4><b>Too much overlap:</b></h4><ul><li>Creates more duplicate text.</li><li>Increases embedding costs.</li><li>Increases vector storage.</li><li>May return redundant retrieval results.</li></ul><h3>Practical exercise</h3><p>Generate chunks using 0%, 10%, 20% and 30% overlap. Compare retrieval results and storage requirements.</p>" },
      { id: "p5-metadata", title: "Chunk metadata", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Each chunk carries its source, position, version and tenant so it can be cited and filtered.</p><h3>What is chunk metadata?</h3><p>Chunk metadata is additional information associated with an individual chunk. It describes:</p><ul><li>Where the chunk came from.</li><li>Which document it belongs to.</li><li>Its position.</li><li>Its version.</li><li>Its section.</li><li>Its tenant or access scope.</li></ul><h3>Why is chunk metadata important?</h3><p>Imagine retrieving this text: \"Employees are eligible for 20 days of annual leave.\" Without metadata, the system may not know:</p><ul><li>Which document contains it.</li><li>Which page it came from.</li><li>Which company owns it.</li><li>Whether it belongs to the latest version.</li><li>Which section it belongs to.</li></ul><p>Metadata provides that information.</p><h3>Common chunk metadata fields</h3><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>chunk_id</td><td>Unique chunk identifier</td></tr><tr><td>document_id</td><td>Parent document</td></tr><tr><td>version_id</td><td>Document version</td></tr><tr><td>tenant_id</td><td>Tenant ownership</td></tr><tr><td>source</td><td>Original file</td></tr><tr><td>page_number</td><td>Page location</td></tr><tr><td>section</td><td>Document section</td></tr><tr><td>chunk_index</td><td>Chunk position</td></tr><tr><td>content_hash</td><td>Content identification</td></tr><tr><td>created_at</td><td>Creation timestamp</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Example chunk with metadata</h3><pre>{\n  \"chunk_id\": \"chunk_001\",\n  \"document_id\": \"doc_001\",\n  \"content\": \"Employees are eligible for 20 days of annual leave.\",\n  \"metadata\": {\n    \"tenant_id\": \"tenant_001\",\n    \"version_id\": \"ver_002\",\n    \"source\": \"employee_policy.pdf\",\n    \"page_number\": 5,\n    \"section\": \"Annual Leave\",\n    \"chunk_index\": 0\n  }\n}</pre><h3>Metadata inheritance</h3><p>Chunks should inherit relevant metadata from their parent documents.</p><p>Parent document:</p><pre>{\n  \"document_id\": \"doc_001\",\n  \"tenant_id\": \"tenant_001\",\n  \"source\": \"policy.pdf\"\n}</pre><p>Child chunk:</p><pre>{\n  \"chunk_id\": \"chunk_001\",\n  \"document_id\": \"doc_001\",\n  \"metadata\": {\n    \"tenant_id\": \"tenant_001\",\n    \"source\": \"policy.pdf\"\n  }\n}</pre><p>You can also add chunk-specific fields such as page number and section title.</p><h3>Metadata and security</h3><p>For multi-tenant RAG systems, metadata is particularly important. A retrieval query must ensure that users can access only authorized tenant documents.</p><div class=\"callout\"><b>Important</b>Do not rely only on metadata supplied by the uploaded file. Tenant IDs and access permissions must be assigned and validated by your application.</div><h3>Practical exercise</h3><p>Generate chunks from a PDF and attach document ID, chunk ID, page number, source, section title, version ID, tenant ID and chunk index.</p>" },
      { id: "p5-chunk-id", title: "Chunk ID generation", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Every chunk needs a unique ID, and deterministic IDs make results reproducible.</p><h3>What is a chunk ID?</h3><p>A chunk ID is a unique identifier assigned to each chunk. A document may contain hundreds or thousands of chunks, and each chunk must be independently identifiable.</p><pre>doc_001\n  |\n  |-- chunk_001\n  |-- chunk_002\n  |-- chunk_003\n  |-- chunk_004</pre><h3>Why do we need chunk IDs?</h3><ul><li>Retrieve individual chunks.</li><li>Update chunk records.</li><li>Delete chunks.</li><li>Maintain parent-child relationships.</li><li>Track chunk positions.</li><li>Link embeddings to original content.</li><li>Avoid accidental record duplication.</li></ul><h3>UUID-based chunk IDs</h3><pre>import uuid\n\nchunk_id = str(uuid.uuid4())\n\nprint(chunk_id)</pre><h3>Sequential chunk IDs</h3><pre>def generate_chunk_id(document_id, index):\n\n    return f\"{document_id}_chunk_{index:04d}\"\n\n\nprint(generate_chunk_id(\"doc_001\", 1))\nprint(generate_chunk_id(\"doc_001\", 2))</pre><p>Output:</p><pre>doc_001_chunk_0001\ndoc_001_chunk_0002</pre><h3>Deterministic chunk IDs</h3><p>Sometimes it is useful to generate the same ID for the same document version and chunk position. For example, UUID5 can generate deterministic identifiers.</p><pre>import uuid\n\nnamespace = uuid.NAMESPACE_URL\n\nchunk_id = uuid.uuid5(\n    namespace,\n    \"doc_001:version_002:chunk_001\"\n)\n\nprint(chunk_id)</pre><p>A deterministic ID is useful when you want reproducible identifiers. However, if the chunking strategy or chunk boundaries change, the meaning associated with a position-based ID may change too.</p><p>A robust identity can include:</p><ul><li>Document ID.</li><li>Version ID.</li><li>Chunking strategy version.</li><li>Chunk position or content hash.</li></ul><h3>Chunk ID vs embedding ID</h3><p>A chunk ID identifies the text chunk. An embedding ID identifies the corresponding vector record in the embedding or vector storage system. They may be the same identifier in a simple architecture, but conceptually they represent different records.</p><h3>Practical exercise</h3><p>Build a chunk ID generator that:</p><ol><li>Accepts a document ID.</li><li>Accepts a version ID.</li><li>Generates sequential chunk IDs.</li><li>Generates deterministic IDs.</li><li>Stores chunk-to-document relationships.</li></ol>" },
      { id: "p5-quality", title: "Chunk quality evaluation", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Check that chunks are complete, coherent, well-bounded and correctly sourced.</p><h3>What is chunk quality evaluation?</h3><p>Chunk quality evaluation is the process of checking whether generated chunks are suitable for semantic retrieval and question answering. Just because a document has been successfully divided does not mean the chunks are good.</p><p>A poor chunk: \"Because of this, it is important to\". It has no meaningful standalone context.</p><p>A better chunk: \"RAG combines information retrieval with language generation to provide LLMs with relevant external knowledge.\" It communicates a complete idea.</p><h3>Why evaluate chunk quality?</h3><p>Poor chunking can lead to:</p><ul><li>Missing context.</li><li>Incorrect retrieval.</li><li>Incomplete answers.</li><li>Repeated information.</li><li>Unnecessary tokens.</li><li>Reduced answer quality.</li></ul><h3>Important chunk quality dimensions</h3><h4><b>1. Completeness</b></h4><p>Does the chunk contain a complete thought?</p><h4><b>2. Coherence</b></h4><p>Are the sentences logically related?</p><h4><b>3. Context preservation</b></h4><p>Does the chunk retain enough information to understand its content?</p><h4><b>4. Boundary quality</b></h4><p>Are the beginning and ending boundaries meaningful?</p><h4><b>5. Size consistency</b></h4><p>Are chunk sizes within the intended range?</p><h4><b>6. Redundancy</b></h4><p>Does the chunk repeat unnecessary information?</p><h4><b>7. Metadata accuracy</b></h4><p>Does the chunk point to the correct source and location?</p><h3>Basic chunk quality checks</h3><p>You can automatically check:</p><ul><li>Empty chunks.</li><li>Duplicate chunks.</li><li>Extremely short chunks.</li><li>Chunks exceeding a token limit.</li></ul>" },
      { id: "p6-intro", title: "Tokenization and token management", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Models read tokens, not text, so tokenization decides chunk size, limits and cost.</p><p>In RAG systems, we work with documents, text chunks, embedding models and LLMs. However, models do not directly process human-readable text in the same way humans do. They process numerical representations of text called tokens. Before sending text to an embedding model or an LLM, the text is tokenized.</p><p>Understanding tokenization is important because it determines:</p><ul><li>How much text a model can process.</li><li>How large your document chunks should be.</li><li>How many tokens an embedding request consumes.</li><li>How much an LLM request costs.</li><li>Whether a document exceeds a model's input limit.</li><li>How to efficiently process large documents.</li></ul><h4><b>RAG pipeline connection:</b></h4><pre>Original Document\n       |\n       v\nText Extraction\n       |\n       v\nText Cleaning\n       |\n       v\nTokenization\n       |\n       v\nToken-Based Chunking\n       |\n       v\nEmbedding Generation\n       |\n       v\nVector Database\n       |\n       v\nRetrieval\n       |\n       v\nLLM Context\n       |\n       v\nGenerated Answer</pre>" },
      { id: "p6-tokens", title: "What are tokens?", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> A token is a small unit of text a model works with, and it is not always a word.</p><p>A token is a small unit of text that a language model uses to process language. A token can represent:</p><ul><li>A complete word.</li><li>Part of a word.</li><li>A character or punctuation mark.</li><li>A combination of characters.</li><li>Spaces or other text representations.</li></ul><pre>Input:\nI love machine learning.</pre><p>A tokenizer might divide it into:</p><pre>[\"I\", \" love\", \" machine\", \" learning\", \".\"]</pre><p>This example is illustrative. The actual token boundaries depend on the tokenizer. Each token is converted into a numerical token ID before being processed by the model.</p><div class=\"callout\"><b>Important</b>A token is not necessarily equal to one word.</div><h3>Why are tokens used?</h3><p>Language models operate on numerical data rather than directly understanding raw text. Tokens provide a manageable vocabulary that allows models to:</p><ol><li>Convert text into numerical IDs.</li><li>Process text using neural network architectures.</li><li>Predict the next token.</li><li>Generate text token by token.</li></ol><h3>Example</h3><pre>Human-readable text:\nHello, how are you?\n\nPossible tokens:\n[\"Hello\", \",\", \" how\", \" are\", \" you\", \"?\"]\n\nPossible token IDs:\n[15496, 11, 703, 389, 345, 30]</pre><p>The token IDs above are illustrative, not guaranteed IDs for a particular tokenizer.</p><h3>Where are tokens used?</h3><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>LLM</td><td>Processes and generates tokens</td></tr><tr><td>Tokenizer</td><td>Converts text into tokens and IDs</td></tr><tr><td>Embedding model</td><td>Converts tokenized input into vectors</td></tr><tr><td>RAG chunker</td><td>Splits documents according to token budgets</td></tr><tr><td>API</td><td>Measures input and output usage</td></tr><tr><td>Billing</td><td>Often calculates cost using token consumption</td></tr></tbody></table></div>" },
      { id: "p6-measures", title: "Characters vs words vs tokens", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Characters, words and tokens are different counts, and token counts depend on the tokenizer.</p><h3>Characters</h3><p>Characters are individual text symbols.</p><pre>Text: Cat\n\nCharacters:\nC, a, t\n\nCharacter count: 3</pre><h3>Words</h3><p>Words are language units separated by spaces or punctuation, depending on the counting method.</p><pre>Text: I love Python programming.\n\nWords:\nI\nlove\nPython\nprogramming\n\nWord count: 4</pre><h3>Tokens</h3><p>Tokens are units produced by a specific tokenizer.</p><pre>Text: I love Python programming.\n\nPossible tokens:\n[\"I\", \" love\", \" Python\", \" programming\", \".\"]\n\nToken count: 5</pre><p>The token count may differ between tokenizers.</p><h3>Comparison</h3><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Measurement</th><th>Example</th><th>Count</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Characters</td><td>Cat</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>Words</td><td>Cat is good</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>Tokens</td><td>Depends on tokenizer</td><td>Varies</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Why does this matter in RAG?</h3><p>Suppose you want to create chunks containing 500 units. With character-based chunking you get about 500 characters. With word-based chunking you get about 500 words. With token-based chunking you get about 500 tokens according to a particular tokenizer. These are not equivalent.</p><p>Practical recommendation: Use token counts when controlling the input size of embedding models and LLMs.</p>" },
      { id: "p6-how", title: "How tokenizers work", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> A tokenizer normalizes text, splits it into subwords and maps each piece to an ID.</p><p>A tokenizer converts text into tokens and then maps those tokens to numerical IDs. A simplified tokenizer pipeline:</p><pre>Input Text\n    |\n    v\nText Normalization\n    |\n    v\nPre-tokenization\n    |\n    v\nSubword Tokenization\n    |\n    v\nToken ID Mapping\n    |\n    v\nNumerical Token IDs</pre><h3>Step 1: Input text</h3><pre>I am learning RAG.</pre><h3>Step 2: Text normalization</h3><p>Depending on the tokenizer, text may be normalized. Possible operations:</p><ul><li>Unicode normalization.</li><li>Whitespace handling.</li><li>Case normalization.</li><li>Special character handling.</li></ul><p>Not every tokenizer performs the same normalization.</p><h3>Step 3: Pre-tokenization</h3><p>The tokenizer identifies preliminary text boundaries. \"I am learning RAG.\" could become:</p><pre>[\"I\", \"am\", \"learning\", \"RAG\", \".\"]</pre><h3>Step 4: Subword tokenization</h3><p>Words may be split into smaller pieces. For example, a tokenizer might split \"unbelievable\" into:</p><pre>[\"un\", \"believ\", \"able\"]</pre><p>This is only an illustration.</p><h3>Step 5: Token ID mapping</h3><p>Each token is mapped to an ID from the tokenizer's vocabulary.</p><pre>Tokens:\n[\"I\", \"am\", \"learning\"]\n\nToken IDs:\n[101, 2026, 4083]</pre><p>These IDs are illustrative.</p><h3>Step 6: Model processing</h3><p>The model receives token IDs and converts them into internal vector representations before further neural network processing.</p><h3>Important distinction</h3><p>Tokenization and embedding are not the same operation.</p><ul><li>Tokenization converts text into token IDs.</li><li>Embedding converts text or token representations into numerical vectors.</li></ul><p>In a transformer, token IDs are generally mapped to learned token embeddings internally. A dedicated embedding model can also return a vector representing the input text.</p>" },
      { id: "p6-ids", title: "Token IDs", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> A token ID is the number a tokenizer's vocabulary assigns to a token.</p><p>A token ID is a numerical identifier assigned to a token in a tokenizer's vocabulary.</p><pre>Vocabulary:\n\n\"hello\"  -&gt; 101\n\"world\"  -&gt; 205\n\"python\" -&gt; 305</pre><p>Input \"hello world\" gives token IDs:</p><pre>[101, 205]</pre><p>The actual IDs depend on the tokenizer.</p><h3>Why are token IDs required?</h3><p>Neural networks operate on numerical values. Token IDs allow models to:</p><ol><li>Look up token embeddings.</li><li>Process text efficiently.</li><li>Generate predictions.</li><li>Convert generated IDs back into readable text.</li></ol><h3>Tokenizer vocabulary</h3><p>A vocabulary is the collection of tokens supported by a tokenizer. It may contain:</p><ul><li>Common words.</li><li>Subword units.</li><li>Punctuation.</li><li>Whitespace-related tokens.</li><li>Special tokens.</li></ul><h3>Special tokens</h3><p>Some tokenizers define special tokens to represent structural information. Examples include:</p><ul><li>Beginning of sequence.</li><li>End of sequence.</li><li>Unknown token.</li><li>Padding token.</li><li>Separator token.</li><li>Instruction or chat boundary markers.</li></ul><p>Their exact names and behavior vary by model.</p><h3>Python example</h3><pre>from transformers import AutoTokenizer\n\ntokenizer = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(\n    \"bert-base-uncased\"\n)\n\ntext = \"I am learning RAG.\"\n\ntokens = tokenizer.tokenize(text)\ntoken_ids = tokenizer.encode(text)\n\nprint(\"Tokens:\", tokens)\nprint(\"Token IDs:\", token_ids)</pre><p>Notice that <code>encode()</code> may add special tokens automatically depending on the tokenizer configuration. To inspect the IDs without automatic special tokens:</p><pre>token_ids = tokenizer.encode(\n    text,\n    add_special_tokens=False\n)</pre>" },
      { id: "p6-bpe", title: "BPE (Byte Pair Encoding)", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> BPE builds a vocabulary by repeatedly merging the most frequent adjacent pairs.</p><p>BPE stands for Byte Pair Encoding. It is a subword tokenization algorithm used by several language-model tokenizer families. The basic idea is to build a vocabulary by repeatedly merging frequently occurring adjacent units.</p><h3>Why BPE?</h3><p>Consider a language with millions of possible words. Creating one vocabulary entry for every possible word would be inefficient. BPE helps by learning reusable subword units. For example, \"playing\", \"played\" and \"player\" may share pieces such as:</p><pre>play\ning\ned\ner</pre><p>The actual segmentation depends on the learned vocabulary and encoding.</p><h3>Simplified BPE working process</h3><p>Suppose our training text contains \"low\", \"lower\" and \"lowest\". Initially, words can be represented as smaller units:</p><pre>l o w\nl o w e r\nl o w e s t</pre><p>The algorithm counts adjacent pairs. Frequently occurring pairs are merged:</p><pre>l + o  -&gt; lo\nlo + w -&gt; low</pre><p>The process continues until the vocabulary reaches its configured size or another stopping condition.</p><h3>Advantages</h3><ul><li>Handles uncommon words through smaller units.</li><li>Can represent many words using a limited vocabulary.</li><li>Reuses common subword patterns.</li><li>Works well across many language-model applications.</li></ul><h3>Limitations</h3><ul><li>Token boundaries may not match linguistic boundaries.</li><li>Different BPE vocabularies produce different token counts.</li><li>Some languages and unusual text may tokenize inefficiently.</li></ul><h3>Where is BPE used?</h3><p>BPE or closely related byte-level BPE approaches are used in multiple model families. The exact tokenizer implementation differs between models.</p>" },
      { id: "p6-wordpiece", title: "WordPiece", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> WordPiece splits words into reusable pieces, marking continuations with ##, and is used by BERT.</p><p>WordPiece is another subword tokenization approach, commonly associated with BERT-family models. Instead of treating every word as an independent vocabulary entry, WordPiece represents words using reusable subword pieces.</p><p>\"playing\" might be represented as:</p><pre>[\"play\", \"##ing\"]</pre><p>The <code>##</code> notation indicates that the piece continues a preceding word.</p><h3>How does WordPiece work?</h3><p>A simplified explanation:</p><ol><li>Start with a vocabulary of smaller units.</li><li>Learn useful subword combinations from training data.</li><li>Use the learned vocabulary to segment input text.</li><li>Represent unknown or rare words using available subword pieces where possible.</li></ol><p>WordPiece vocabulary construction uses a different objective from standard BPE.</p><h3>Example in Python</h3><pre>from transformers import AutoTokenizer\n\ntokenizer = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(\n    \"bert-base-uncased\"\n)\n\ntext = \"unbelievable\"\n\nprint(tokenizer.tokenize(text))</pre><p>Possible output:</p><pre>[\"un\", \"##bel\", \"##ievable\"]</pre><p>The actual output depends on the vocabulary.</p><h3>Advantages</h3><ul><li>Handles words not explicitly present as complete vocabulary entries.</li><li>Reuses subword units.</li><li>Useful for language understanding tasks.</li></ul><h3>Where is it used?</h3><p>BERT and several related model families use WordPiece-style tokenization.</p>" },
      { id: "p6-sentencepiece", title: "SentencePiece", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> SentencePiece tokenizes raw text directly, which suits multilingual text.</p><p>SentencePiece is a tokenizer library and framework that supports subword tokenization algorithms such as BPE and the Unigram Language Model. It is designed to work directly from raw text without requiring conventional word-level pre-tokenization. This is particularly useful for multilingual text and languages that do not consistently use spaces between words.</p><p>Input \"I am learning machine learning.\" might produce pieces such as:</p><pre>[\"▁I\", \"▁am\", \"▁learning\", \"▁machine\", \"▁learning\", \".\"]</pre><p>The <code>▁</code> marker commonly represents a whitespace boundary. This is illustrative; real output depends on the model vocabulary.</p><h3>Why SentencePiece?</h3><p>Traditional tokenization often relies on spaces to identify words. However, many languages do not use spaces in the same way. SentencePiece helps tokenize raw text consistently across different languages.</p><h3>Advantages</h3><ul><li>Works directly with raw text.</li><li>Supports multilingual applications.</li><li>Supports BPE and Unigram algorithms.</li><li>Does not require conventional whitespace-based word splitting.</li></ul><h3>Python example</h3><pre>import sentencepiece as spm\n\nsp = spm.SentencePieceProcessor(\n    model_file=\"tokenizer.model\"\n)\n\ntext = \"I am learning RAG.\"\n\npieces = sp.encode(\n    text,\n    out_type=str\n)\n\nids = sp.encode(\n    text,\n    out_type=int\n)\n\nprint(\"Pieces:\", pieces)\nprint(\"IDs:\", ids)</pre><p>You need a compatible trained SentencePiece model file to run this example.</p>" },
      { id: "p6-model-specific", title: "Model-specific tokenizers", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Different models tokenize differently, so always count with the target model's tokenizer.</p><p>Different models can use different tokenizers. This is one of the most important concepts in RAG token management. The same text can produce different token counts when processed by different tokenizers.</p><p>Text: \"Artificial intelligence is changing technology.\"</p><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Tokenizer</th><th>Possible behavior</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>BERT tokenizer</td><td>Uses WordPiece-style segmentation</td></tr><tr><td>GPT-family tokenizer</td><td>Uses its own vocabulary and encoding</td></tr><tr><td>T5 tokenizer</td><td>Commonly uses SentencePiece</td></tr><tr><td>Llama-family tokenizer</td><td>Depends on the model's tokenizer configuration</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Why does this matter?</h3><p>Suppose you have an embedding model with a maximum input size of 8,192 tokens. You create a chunk containing 7,500 tokens using a GPT tokenizer. You then send that chunk to an embedding model that uses a different tokenizer. It might contain more than 8,192 tokens under the embedding model's tokenizer, and your request may fail.</p><h3>Golden rule</h3><h4><b>Always count tokens using the tokenizer associated with the model that will receive the text.</b></h4><ul><li>Count embedding input using the embedding model's tokenizer.</li><li>Count LLM context using the LLM's tokenizer.</li><li>Count generated output using the relevant model's usage information.</li></ul><p>Do not assume one tokenizer's count is valid for every model.</p>" },
      { id: "p6-counting", title: "How to count tokens", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Use tiktoken, Hugging Face tokenizers or Sentence Transformers, matched to your model.</p><h3>Method 1: Using tiktoken</h3><p><code>tiktoken</code> is a fast tokenizer library commonly used with OpenAI model encodings. Install:</p><pre>uv add tiktoken</pre><pre>import tiktoken\n\ntext = \"I am learning Retrieval Augmented Generation.\"\n\nencoding = tiktoken.get_encoding(\"cl100k_base\")\n\ntokens = encoding.encode(text)\n\nprint(\"Token IDs:\", tokens)\nprint(\"Token count:\", len(tokens))</pre><p>Count tokens using a model name:</p><pre>import tiktoken\n\ntext = \"I am learning RAG.\"\n\nencoding = tiktoken.encoding_for_model(\n    \"gpt-4o\"\n)\n\ntokens = encoding.encode(text)\n\nprint(len(tokens))</pre><p>The model name must be supported by the installed <code>tiktoken</code> version. If the model is not recognized, consult its official tokenizer documentation rather than guessing an encoding.</p><h3>Method 2: Using Hugging Face Transformers</h3><p>Install:</p><pre>uv add transformers</pre><pre>from transformers import AutoTokenizer\n\nmodel_name = \"bert-base-uncased\"\n\ntokenizer = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained(\n    model_name\n)\n\ntext = \"I am learning RAG.\"\n\ntokens = tokenizer.tokenize(text)\n\ntoken_ids = tokenizer.encode(\n    text,\n    add_special_tokens=False\n)\n\nprint(\"Tokens:\", tokens)\nprint(\"Token count:\", len(token_ids))</pre><h3>Method 3: Using Sentence Transformers</h3><p>Sentence Transformers models generally use Hugging Face tokenizers internally.</p><pre>from sentence_transformers import SentenceTransformer\n\nmodel = SentenceTransformer(\n    \"sentence-transformers/all-MiniLM-L6-v2\"\n)\n\ntokenizer = model.tokenizer\n\ntext = \"I am learning RAG.\"\n\nencoded = tokenizer(\n    text,\n    add_special_tokens=True\n)\n\nprint(\"Token count:\", len(encoded[\"input_ids\"]))</pre><p>This counts the input including the configured special tokens.</p><h3>Which method should you use?</h3><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th>Recommended approach</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>OpenAI-compatible model</td><td>Model's documented tokenizer, often tiktoken</td></tr><tr><td>Hugging Face model</td><td>AutoTokenizer</td></tr><tr><td>Sentence Transformers</td><td><code>model.tokenizer</code></td></tr><tr><td>Unknown provider/model</td><td>Official model tokenizer or provider token-count API</td></tr></tbody></table></div>" },
      { id: "p6-embedding-limits", title: "Token limits in embedding models", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Embedding models reject or truncate input past their token limit, so check before embedding.</p><p>Embedding models have maximum input token limits. An embedding model converts text into a vector representation, but it cannot necessarily process an unlimited amount of text in one request.</p><pre>Embedding model maximum input:\n8,192 tokens\n\nYour document chunk:\n10,000 tokens\n\nResult:\nInput exceeds model limit</pre><p>The exact limit depends on the selected model and its configuration.</p><h3>Why do embedding models have limits?</h3><p>Models have architectural and operational constraints. Longer inputs can also:</p><ul><li>Increase processing time.</li><li>Increase memory consumption.</li><li>Increase cost.</li><li>Reduce the usefulness of a single representation when the text contains unrelated topics.</li></ul><h3>Example: RAG embedding workflow</h3><pre>Document\n   |\n   v\nCleaned Text\n   |\n   v\nToken Count\n   |\n   v\nIs input within embedding limit?\n   |\n   +---- Yes ----&gt; Generate Embedding\n   |\n   +---- No -----&gt; Split into Smaller Chunks</pre><h3>Important considerations</h3><p>When selecting an embedding model, check:</p><ol><li>Maximum input token length.</li><li>Embedding vector dimensions.</li><li>Supported languages.</li><li>Whether truncation is automatic.</li><li>Whether special tokens count toward the limit.</li><li>Batch input limits.</li><li>Provider request-size limits.</li></ol><div class=\"callout\"><b>Important</b>Never assume that an API automatically handles long documents safely. Some models or libraries truncate excess input, while others return errors. Silent truncation can cause information loss.</div>" },
      { id: "p6-llm-limits", title: "Token limits in LLMs", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> The context window must hold instructions, history, question, retrieved chunks and the answer.</p><p>LLMs also have token limits. A context window defines how much tokenized information a model can handle in a request. The context may include:</p><ul><li>System instructions.</li><li>Developer instructions.</li><li>Conversation history.</li><li>User question.</li><li>Retrieved RAG chunks.</li><li>Tool descriptions and tool results.</li><li>Other formatting and special tokens.</li></ul><h3>Example</h3><p>Suppose an LLM has a context window of 32,000 tokens. Your request contains:</p><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>Tokens</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>System instructions</td><td>1,000</td></tr><tr><td>Conversation history</td><td>5,000</td></tr><tr><td>User question</td><td>500</td></tr><tr><td>Retrieved documents</td><td>20,000</td></tr><tr><td>Other overhead</td><td>500</td></tr><tr><td>Total input</td><td>27,000</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>You have approximately 5,000 tokens remaining in the context window, but you may also need to reserve space for output.</p><h3>Context budget</h3><pre>Total Context Window\n        |\n        +---- Instructions\n        |\n        +---- Conversation History\n        |\n        +---- User Query\n        |\n        +---- Retrieved Context\n        |\n        +---- Formatting / Tool Overhead\n        |\n        +---- Output Budget</pre><h3>Important distinction</h3><p>Context window and maximum output tokens are not necessarily the same thing. A model may have a large context window but a smaller maximum output limit. Always check both limits.</p><h3>RAG example</h3><p>Suppose your retriever returns 20 chunks, each containing 1,000 tokens.</p><pre>20 chunks × 1,000 tokens = 20,000 tokens</pre><p>If your LLM only has 16,000 tokens available for retrieved context, you cannot simply send all 20 chunks. You need to:</p><ul><li>Reduce the number of retrieved chunks.</li><li>Reduce chunk sizes.</li><li>Rerank results.</li><li>Remove redundant content.</li><li>Compress or summarize context when appropriate.</li></ul>" },
      { id: "p6-token-chunking", title: "Token-based chunking", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Chunk by token windows with overlap, then use a boundary-aware chunker for production.</p><p>Token-based chunking divides documents according to token counts rather than character counts or word counts. It is useful when working with embedding models and LLMs that have specific token limits.</p><p>Original document:</p><pre>RAG combines information retrieval with language generation.\nIt retrieves relevant documents and uses them to answer questions.\nIt can use different embedding models and vector databases.</pre><p>Suppose we configure:</p><pre>Chunk size: 20 tokens\nOverlap: 5 tokens</pre><p>The chunker divides the document into token windows while preserving the requested overlap.</p><h3>Why use token-based chunking?</h3><ul><li>Controls input size.</li><li>Helps avoid model-limit errors.</li><li>Supports predictable embedding requests.</li><li>Helps manage LLM context budgets.</li><li>Makes token consumption easier to estimate.</li></ul><h3>Python implementation using tiktoken</h3><pre>import tiktoken\n\nclass TokenChunker:\n\n    def __init__(\n        self,\n        chunk_size: int = 100,\n        overlap: int = 20,\n        encoding_name: str = \"cl100k_base\"\n    ):\n        if chunk_size &lt;= 0:\n            raise ValueError(\"chunk_size must be positive\")\n\n        if overlap &lt; 0 or overlap &gt;= chunk_size:\n            raise ValueError(\n                \"overlap must be &gt;= 0 and smaller than chunk_size\"\n            )\n\n        self.encoding = tiktoken.get_encoding(\n            encoding_name\n        )\n\n        self.chunk_size = chunk_size\n        self.overlap = overlap\n\n    def split(self, text: str) -&gt; list[str]:\n\n        token_ids = self.encoding.encode(text)\n\n        chunks = []\n\n        step = self.chunk_size - self.overlap\n\n        for start in range(0, len(token_ids), step):\n\n            end = start + self.chunk_size\n\n            chunk_ids = token_ids[start:end]\n\n            chunk_text = self.encoding.decode(\n                chunk_ids\n            )\n\n            if chunk_text.strip():\n                chunks.append(chunk_text)\n\n            if end &gt;= len(token_ids):\n                break\n\n        return chunks\n\n\ntext = \"\"\"\nRetrieval Augmented Generation combines retrieval\nsystems with large language models. It retrieves\nrelevant information from external sources and uses\nthat information to generate grounded responses.\n\"\"\"\n\nchunker = TokenChunker(\n    chunk_size=20,\n    overlap=5\n)\n\nchunks = chunker.split(text)\n\nfor index, chunk in enumerate(chunks):\n    token_count = len(\n        chunker.encoding.encode(chunk)\n    )\n\n    print(f\"Chunk {index + 1}\")\n    print(\"Tokens:\", token_count)\n    print(chunk)\n    print(\"-\" * 40)</pre><h3>Important implementation note</h3><p>This is a basic token-window chunker. It does not preserve sentence or paragraph boundaries intentionally. For production RAG, consider a boundary-aware token chunker:</p><ol><li>Split text into paragraphs.</li><li>Split oversized paragraphs into sentences.</li><li>Group sentences while respecting the token budget.</li><li>Split individual oversized sentences only when necessary.</li><li>Apply overlap carefully.</li><li>Recount every final chunk using the target model's tokenizer.</li></ol><p>This helps preserve semantic meaning while maintaining token limits.</p>" },
      { id: "p6-cost", title: "Token consumption and cost", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> APIs bill by input and output tokens, so token counts let you estimate and reduce cost.</p><p>Many LLM and embedding APIs measure usage in tokens, and token consumption affects API cost. A common pricing model separates input tokens, output tokens, sometimes cached input tokens, and sometimes other model-specific usage categories.</p><h3>Example</h3><p>Suppose an LLM charges $2 per 1 million input tokens and $8 per 1 million output tokens. A request consumes 10,000 input tokens and 2,000 output tokens.</p><pre>Input cost:\n10,000 / 1,000,000 × $2 = $0.02\n\nOutput cost:\n2,000 / 1,000,000 × $8 = $0.016\n\nTotal:\n$0.02 + $0.016 = $0.036</pre><p>These prices are hypothetical examples, not current provider pricing.</p><h3>Cost in a RAG system</h3><p>RAG systems can consume tokens at multiple stages.</p><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>Token usage</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Embedding generation</td><td>Document input tokens</td></tr><tr><td>Query embedding</td><td>User query tokens</td></tr><tr><td>LLM generation</td><td>Instructions + retrieved context + question</td></tr><tr><td>LLM output</td><td>Generated answer tokens</td></tr><tr><td>Reranking</td><td>Depends on the reranker and its input format</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Example: document embedding cost</h3><p>Suppose you have 1,000 documents with an average of 2,000 tokens each.</p><pre>Total embedding input:\n1,000 × 2,000 = 2,000,000 tokens\n\nIf the embedding model costs $0.10 per million tokens:\n2,000,000 / 1,000,000 × $0.10 = $0.20</pre><p>Again, this is a hypothetical rate.</p><h3>How can you reduce token cost?</h3><ol><li>Remove duplicate content.</li><li>Clean unnecessary text.</li><li>Avoid embedding the same unchanged document repeatedly.</li><li>Use appropriate chunk sizes.</li><li>Avoid retrieving irrelevant chunks.</li><li>Rerank before sending context to the LLM.</li><li>Limit unnecessary conversation history.</li><li>Use caching where appropriate.</li><li>Monitor input and output tokens separately.</li><li>Select models based on quality and cost requirements.</li></ol>" },
      { id: "p6-long-docs", title: "Handling long documents", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Split, validate, batch, track progress, retry and update incrementally.</p><p>Real-world documents can contain thousands or millions of tokens: large PDFs, technical manuals, research papers, books, legal documents, company knowledge bases, or multiple documents combined. You should not assume that an entire document can be sent to an embedding model in one request.</p><h3>Recommended processing pipeline</h3><pre>Large Document\n      |\n      v\nExtract Text\n      |\n      v\nClean Text\n      |\n      v\nCount Tokens\n      |\n      v\nSplit into Chunks\n      |\n      v\nValidate Chunk Sizes\n      |\n      v\nBatch Embedding Requests\n      |\n      v\nStore Embeddings</pre><h3>Strategy 1: Split into chunks</h3><p>Divide the document into manageable sections.</p><pre>Book.pdf\n   |\n   +---- Chunk 001\n   +---- Chunk 002\n   +---- Chunk 003\n   +---- Chunk 004\n   +---- Chunk 005</pre><h3>Strategy 2: Batch processing</h3><p>Instead of sending one request for every chunk, send multiple chunks in one batch if the provider supports it.</p><pre>chunks = [\n    \"First document chunk\",\n    \"Second document chunk\",\n    \"Third document chunk\"\n]\n\n# Pass the list to your embedding model.</pre><p>Batch limits and request-size limits still apply.</p><h3>Strategy 3: Track processing progress</h3><p>For large datasets, store processing status.</p><pre>{\n  \"document_id\": \"doc_001\",\n  \"total_chunks\": 100,\n  \"processed_chunks\": 65,\n  \"status\": \"processing\"\n}</pre><h3>Strategy 4: Handle failures and retries</h3><p>A production system should support:</p><ul><li>Retry with exponential backoff.</li><li>Rate-limit handling.</li><li>Failed chunk tracking.</li><li>Idempotent processing.</li><li>Progress checkpoints.</li><li>Duplicate prevention.</li></ul><h3>Strategy 5: Incremental updates</h3><p>If a document changes, avoid reprocessing everything unnecessarily. Possible approaches:</p><ul><li>Compare document versions.</li><li>Calculate content hashes.</li><li>Reprocess changed sections.</li><li>Delete obsolete chunk records.</li><li>Generate embeddings for new chunks.</li></ul>" },
      { id: "p6-chunk-size", title: "Selecting chunk size using token counts", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Start from a baseline like 512 tokens with overlap, then measure retrieval quality.</p><p>Chunk size directly affects RAG retrieval quality. A chunk that is too small may lose context. A chunk that is too large may contain unrelated information and produce less focused retrieval results.</p><h3>Example configurations</h3><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Chunk size</th><th>Overlap</th><th>Potential use</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>128 tokens</td><td>20</td><td>Small, focused passages</td></tr><tr><td>256 tokens</td><td>40</td><td>Short answers and compact content</td></tr><tr><td>512 tokens</td><td>75</td><td>General-purpose starting point</td></tr><tr><td>768 tokens</td><td>100</td><td>Longer explanations</td></tr><tr><td>1,024 tokens</td><td>150</td><td>Longer passages and context</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>These are experimental starting points, not universal best settings.</p><h3>Factors affecting chunk size</h3><ol><li>Embedding model input limit.</li><li>Document structure.</li><li>Average paragraph length.</li><li>Type of questions users ask.</li><li>Retrieval quality.</li><li>LLM context window.</li><li>Cost requirements.</li><li>Language and tokenizer behavior.</li></ol><h3>Recommended approach</h3><p>Start with a baseline such as chunk size 512 tokens and overlap 75 tokens. Then test multiple configurations using the same documents and evaluation questions. Measure:</p><ul><li>Recall@K.</li><li>Precision@K.</li><li>Mean Reciprocal Rank (MRR).</li><li>Normalized Discounted Cumulative Gain (NDCG).</li><li>Answer correctness.</li><li>Latency.</li><li>Cost per query.</li></ul><div class=\"callout\"><b>Important</b>Do not choose a chunk size only because it fits the embedding model's maximum input limit. The maximum input size is a hard constraint, not necessarily the optimal chunk size. The best chunk size is the one that provides the best retrieval and answer quality for your use case.</div>" },
      { id: "p6-project", title: "Practical project: tokenization and token management engine", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Build a small package that tokenizes, counts, chunks and validates against model limits.</p><h3>Project objective</h3><p>Build a small Python package that can tokenize documents, count tokens, split text into token-based chunks, and validate chunks against model limits.</p><h3>Features</h3><ol><li>Read TXT and Markdown documents.</li><li>Select a tokenizer.</li><li>Convert text into token IDs.</li><li>Count tokens.</li><li>Split documents using token limits.</li><li>Configure chunk size and overlap.</li><li>Validate chunk token counts.</li><li>Export chunks to JSONL.</li><li>Generate a token usage report.</li><li>Compare token counts across tokenizers.</li></ol><h3>Suggested folder structure</h3><pre>token-management/\n│\n├── data/\n│   ├── input/\n│   └── output/\n│\n├── src/\n│   └── token_management/\n│       ├── __init__.py\n│       ├── tokenizer.py\n│       ├── token_counter.py\n│       ├── chunker.py\n│       ├── validator.py\n│       ├── document_loader.py\n│       └── reporter.py\n│\n├── tests/\n│   ├── test_tokenizer.py\n│   ├── test_counter.py\n│   ├── test_chunker.py\n│   └── test_validator.py\n│\n├── pyproject.toml\n└── README.md</pre><h3>Expected output</h3><p>Example token report:</p><pre>{\n  \"document_id\": \"doc_001\",\n  \"tokenizer\": \"cl100k_base\",\n  \"total_tokens\": 2500,\n  \"chunk_size\": 512,\n  \"overlap\": 75,\n  \"total_chunks\": 6,\n  \"max_chunk_tokens\": 512,\n  \"validation_status\": \"passed\"\n}</pre><h3>Completion criteria</h3><ul><li>You can load a text document.</li><li>You can tokenize the content.</li><li>You can display token IDs.</li><li>You can count tokens accurately for the selected tokenizer.</li><li>You can split the document using token limits.</li><li>You can validate the final chunks.</li><li>You can export chunks and metadata to JSONL.</li><li>You can compare token counts from two different tokenizers.</li><li>You understand why token counts differ between models.</li></ul>" },
      { id: "p6-best-practices", title: "Best practices", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Twelve rules for reliable, cost-efficient token handling.</p><ol><li>Always use the tokenizer associated with the target model.</li><li>Do not assume one word equals one token.</li><li>Do not use character counts as a replacement for token counts when enforcing model limits.</li><li>Include special tokens when checking the actual model input if applicable.</li><li>Leave a safety margin below maximum input limits.</li><li>Avoid silent truncation.</li><li>Validate every final chunk after chunking.</li><li>Track token consumption for embedding and generation separately.</li><li>Reuse embeddings for unchanged content where appropriate.</li><li>Test chunk sizes using retrieval evaluation.</li><li>Monitor provider-specific request and batch limits.</li><li>Store tokenizer and model information alongside processing metadata.</li></ol>" },
      { id: "p6-revision", title: "Quick revision", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> One-line answers to the key questions of this phase.</p><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Answer</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>What is a token?</td><td>A unit of text processed by a model</td></tr><tr><td>What is tokenization?</td><td>Converting text into tokens and token IDs</td></tr><tr><td>What is a token ID?</td><td>Numerical identifier of a token</td></tr><tr><td>What is BPE?</td><td>A subword tokenization algorithm based on merging frequent pairs</td></tr><tr><td>What is WordPiece?</td><td>A subword tokenization method used by BERT-family models</td></tr><tr><td>What is SentencePiece?</td><td>A tokenizer framework supporting BPE and Unigram</td></tr><tr><td>Why model-specific tokenizers?</td><td>Different models can produce different token boundaries and counts</td></tr><tr><td>Why count tokens?</td><td>To control model input sizes and estimate usage</td></tr><tr><td>What is a context window?</td><td>The maximum amount of context a model can handle</td></tr><tr><td>What is token-based chunking?</td><td>Splitting text according to token counts</td></tr><tr><td>Why use overlap?</td><td>To preserve context across adjacent chunks</td></tr><tr><td>Why validate chunks?</td><td>To ensure they fit within model limits</td></tr></tbody></table></div>" },
      { id: "p6-exercises", title: "Hands-on exercises", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Beginner, intermediate and advanced practice tasks.</p><h3>Beginner</h3><ol><li>Install <code>tiktoken</code>.</li><li>Count tokens in five different sentences.</li><li>Compare character count, word count, and token count.</li><li>Print token IDs for a paragraph.</li><li>Decode token IDs back into text.</li></ol><h3>Intermediate</h3><ol><li>Load a PDF's extracted text.</li><li>Count the total tokens.</li><li>Split the text into 256-token chunks.</li><li>Add 40-token overlap.</li><li>Validate each chunk.</li><li>Export the chunks to JSONL.</li></ol><h3>Advanced</h3><ol><li>Compare token counts using two different model tokenizers.</li><li>Implement batch token counting.</li><li>Create a tokenizer abstraction.</li><li>Build a token budget calculator for LLM context.</li><li>Track token consumption and estimated cost.</li><li>Add tests for empty text, special characters, multilingual text, and oversized documents.</li></ol>" },
      { id: "p6-final", title: "Final understanding", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Tokenization sits between raw text and model processing in every RAG step.</p><p>By completing this phase, you should understand that tokenization is a critical layer between raw document text and model processing. In RAG:</p><ul><li>Documents are extracted and cleaned.</li><li>Text is tokenized.</li><li>Documents are divided into chunks using token budgets.</li><li>Chunks are sent to embedding models.</li><li>Retrieved chunks are selected using relevance.</li><li>Relevant context is sent to the LLM within its context budget.</li></ul><p>Token management helps build RAG systems that are reliable, cost-efficient, and compatible with model input limits.</p><p>Your next step: Build the Token Management Engine before moving to embedding generation.</p>" },
      { id: "p6-next", title: "Next: embedding models and embedding generation", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Phase 07 turns text into vectors.</p><p>Topics to learn next:</p><ol><li>What are embeddings?</li><li>Why are embeddings required in RAG?</li><li>How text is converted into vectors</li><li>Understanding vector dimensions</li><li>Embedding models</li><li>Word embeddings vs sentence embeddings</li><li>Transformer-based embeddings</li><li>OpenAI embedding models</li><li>Hugging Face embedding models</li><li>Sentence Transformers</li><li>Choosing an embedding model</li><li>Generating embeddings using Python</li><li>Batch embedding generation</li><li>Embedding normalization</li><li>Embedding dimensions</li><li>Embedding storage format</li><li>Embedding model evaluation</li></ol>" },
      { id: "p7-intro", title: "Embeddings and embedding model selection", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Embeddings turn chunks and queries into vectors so RAG can find content by meaning.</p><div class=\"callout\"><b>Important</b>Goal: Convert document chunks and user queries into numerical vectors so that a RAG system can retrieve semantically relevant information.</div><p>In previous phases, we learned:</p><ul><li>How to collect documents.</li><li>How to extract text.</li><li>How to clean and preprocess text.</li><li>How to store documents.</li><li>How to split documents into chunks.</li><li>How to tokenize text and manage token limits.</li></ul><p>Now we reach an important part of RAG: <b>Embedding Generation</b>.</p><p>A computer cannot directly compare the meaning of two paragraphs in the same way a human does.</p><p>Consider these two sentences:</p><pre>Sentence 1:\nHow can I reset my password?\n\nSentence 2:\nWhat should I do if I forget my login credentials?</pre><p>The words are different, but the meaning is similar.</p><p>A keyword-based search might not recognize this relationship effectively.</p><p>An embedding model converts these sentences into numerical vectors that represent their semantic information.</p><p>The vectors can then be compared to identify semantically similar content.</p><h3>Where embeddings fit in RAG</h3><pre>DOCUMENT INGESTION\n\nDocuments\n    |\n    v\nText Extraction\n    |\n    v\nCleaning\n    |\n    v\nChunking\n    |\n    v\nEmbedding Model\n    |\n    v\nDocument Vectors\n    |\n    v\nVector Database\n\n\nUSER QUERY\n\nUser Question\n    |\n    v\nEmbedding Model\n    |\n    v\nQuery Vector\n    |\n    v\nSimilarity Search\n    |\n    v\nRelevant Document Chunks\n    |\n    v\nLLM\n    |\n    v\nFinal Answer</pre><p>The central idea is:</p><h4><b>Convert both documents and queries into vectors, then compare their vector representations to find relevant information.</b></h4>" },
      { id: "p7-1", title: "What Are Embeddings?", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> An embedding is a list of numbers that represents the meaning of text.</p><p>An embedding is a numerical representation of data in a vector space.</p><p>An embedding model converts text into a list of floating-point numbers.</p><p>Example:</p><pre>Input:\nI love machine learning.</pre><p>Possible illustrative embedding:</p><pre>[\n    0.125,\n    -0.342,\n    0.871,\n    0.219,\n    -0.104\n]</pre><p>This list of numbers is called an embedding vector.</p><p>Real embedding models commonly generate vectors with hundreds or thousands of dimensions.</p><p>For example:</p><pre>Text\n |\n v\nEmbedding Model\n |\n v\n[0.125, -0.342, 0.871, 0.219, ...]\n |\n v\nVector Representation</pre><h3>Why are they called vectors?</h3><p>A vector is an ordered collection of numerical values.</p><p>For example:</p><pre>A = [0.2, 0.5, 0.8]</pre><p>This vector has three dimensions.</p><p>Similarly:</p><pre>B = [0.3, 0.4, 0.9]</pre><p>A text embedding is a vector with a much larger number of dimensions.</p><h3>What do the numbers represent?</h3><p>The values are learned numerical features that collectively represent information about the input.</p><p>They are not usually individually interpretable as specific concepts.</p><p>For example, you should not assume:</p><ul><li>Dimension 1 represents animals.</li><li>Dimension 2 represents technology.</li><li>Dimension 3 represents emotions.</li></ul><p>Meaning is distributed across the vector dimensions.</p><h3>Important characteristics</h3><p>An embedding represents information in a mathematical space.</p><p>The embedding model is trained to make useful relationships between inputs detectable through vector operations.</p><p>For semantic search, we want related text to have similar vector representations according to the model's learned representation space.</p><h3>Applications of embeddings</h3><ul><li>Semantic search.</li><li>RAG retrieval.</li><li>Document similarity.</li><li>Recommendation systems.</li><li>Duplicate detection.</li><li>Clustering.</li><li>Classification.</li><li>Image and multimodal search.</li></ul>" },
      { id: "p7-2", title: "Why RAG Needs Embeddings", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Embeddings find relevant text even when the wording is different.</p><p>Suppose you have a knowledge base containing 10,000 documents.</p><p>A user asks:</p><pre>How do I change my account password?</pre><p>Your knowledge base contains:</p><pre>Document 1:\nSteps to update your login credentials.\n\nDocument 2:\nHow to configure email notifications.\n\nDocument 3:\nHow to delete your account.\n\nDocument 4:\nPassword recovery and reset instructions.</pre><p>A simple keyword search might miss relevant documents because the user and document use different words.</p><p>Embeddings help identify similar meaning even when the exact words differ.</p><h3>Without embeddings</h3><pre>User Query\n    |\n    v\nKeyword Matching\n    |\n    v\nFind Exact Word Matches</pre><p>Limitations:</p><ul><li>Synonyms may be missed.</li><li>Different sentence structures may be missed.</li><li>Meaning may not be captured.</li><li>Context can be difficult to compare.</li></ul><h3>With embeddings</h3><pre>User Query\n    |\n    v\nQuery Embedding\n    |\n    v\nVector Similarity Search\n    |\n    v\nFind Semantically Similar Chunks</pre><h3>Example</h3><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>User query</th><th>Document text</th><th>Expected semantic relationship</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>How do I reset my password?</td><td>Password recovery instructions</td><td>High</td></tr><tr><td>How can I sign in?</td><td>Login troubleshooting guide</td><td>Potentially high</td></tr><tr><td>How do I change my profile picture?</td><td>Profile customization instructions</td><td>Lower</td></tr><tr><td>What is machine learning?</td><td>Introduction to neural networks</td><td>Depends on context</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Why embeddings are important in RAG</h3><ol><li>They support semantic retrieval.</li><li>They help find relevant information beyond exact keywords.</li><li>They allow large collections of documents to be searched efficiently using vector indexes.</li><li>They provide a numerical representation that can be compared mathematically.</li><li>They help retrieve useful context before sending information to an LLM.</li></ol><div class=\"callout\"><b>Important</b>Embeddings do not guarantee correct retrieval. Quality depends on the embedding model, data, chunking, query formulation, indexing, and retrieval configuration.</div>" },
      { id: "p7-3", title: "How Text Converts into Vectors", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Text is tokenized, passed through a transformer, then pooled into one vector.</p><p>An embedding model transforms input text into a numerical representation.</p><p>A simplified process looks like this:</p><pre>Input Text\n    |\n    v\nTokenization\n    |\n    v\nToken IDs\n    |\n    v\nToken Embeddings\n    |\n    v\nTransformer Layers\n    |\n    v\nContextual Representations\n    |\n    v\nPooling / Projection\n    |\n    v\nFinal Embedding Vector</pre><p>Let's understand each step.</p><h3>Step 1: Input text</h3><pre>Machine learning is useful.</pre><h3>Step 2: Tokenization</h3><p>The tokenizer divides the input into tokens.</p><p>Illustrative output:</p><pre>[\"Machine\", \" learning\", \" is\", \" useful\", \".\"]</pre><h3>Step 3: Token IDs</h3><p>Each token is converted into a numerical identifier.</p><pre>[101, 305, 204, 900, 102]</pre><p>These IDs are illustrative.</p><h3>Step 4: Token embeddings</h3><p>The model maps token IDs into learned vectors using an embedding layer.</p><p>Each token receives an initial numerical representation.</p><h3>Step 5: Transformer processing</h3><p>Transformer layers process the token representations.</p><p>They use mechanisms such as self-attention to build contextual representations.</p><p>For example, the meaning of a word can depend on the surrounding words.</p><h3>Step 6: Pooling</h3><p>A sentence embedding model generally needs to convert multiple token representations into one fixed-size vector.</p><p>Common approaches include:</p><ul><li>Mean pooling.</li><li>CLS-token pooling.</li><li>Max pooling.</li><li>Model-specific pooling or projection layers.</li></ul><p>The appropriate method depends on the model architecture and its training.</p><h3>Step 7: Final vector</h3><p>The model produces one vector representing the input text.</p><pre>[0.21, -0.12, 0.53, 0.87, ...]</pre><h3>Important distinction</h3><p>A token embedding is not necessarily the same as a sentence embedding.</p><ul><li>Token embeddings represent individual token positions.</li><li>Sentence embeddings represent an entire sentence or text passage.</li><li>Document embeddings represent a larger input, usually through pooling or another document-level strategy.</li></ul>" },
      { id: "p7-4", title: "Embedding Model Architecture", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Most embedding models are transformer encoders with pooling and optional normalization.</p><p>Most modern text embedding models use neural network architectures.</p><p>Many popular embedding models are based on Transformer architectures.</p><p>A simplified architecture:</p><pre>Input Text\n    |\n    v\nTokenizer\n    |\n    v\nToken IDs\n    |\n    v\nEmbedding Layer\n    |\n    v\nTransformer Encoder\n    |\n    v\nPooling Layer\n    |\n    v\nOptional Projection Layer\n    |\n    v\nNormalization\n    |\n    v\nFinal Embedding</pre><h3>1. Tokenizer</h3><p>Converts text into token IDs.</p><h3>2. Embedding layer</h3><p>Maps token IDs into initial vector representations.</p><h3>3. Transformer encoder</h3><p>Processes token representations and captures contextual relationships.</p><p>The model may use:</p><ul><li>Self-attention.</li><li>Feed-forward neural networks.</li><li>Layer normalization.</li><li>Residual connections.</li><li>Positional information.</li></ul><h3>4. Pooling layer</h3><p>Combines token-level representations into a single vector.</p><p>For example, mean pooling calculates the average of the relevant token vectors.</p><pre>E = (1) / (N)Σ(i=1..N) h_i</pre><p>Where:</p><ul><li><code>E</code> = final pooled vector.</li><li><code>N</code> = number of included token positions.</li><li><code>h_i</code> = contextual representation of token <code>i</code>.</li></ul><p>Padding tokens are normally excluded using an attention mask.</p><h3>5. Projection layer</h3><p>Some models apply a learned transformation to convert the pooled representation into the final embedding dimensions.</p><h3>6. Normalization</h3><p>Some models normalize the final vector.</p><p>For L2 normalization:</p><pre>Ê = (E) / (‖E‖₂)</pre><p>This produces a vector with unit length.</p><p>Not every model requires you to normalize its output manually. Follow the model's instructions.</p><h3>Important architecture distinction</h3><p>Not all embedding models have exactly the same architecture.</p><p>Some use encoder-only Transformers, some use other architectures, and some use specialized retrieval training or projection heads.</p><p>Do not assume that every embedding model uses the exact same pipeline.</p>" },
      { id: "p7-5", title: "Input Text and Output Vectors", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> N texts in gives N vectors out, each with a fixed number of dimensions.</p><p>Every embedding model accepts a particular type of input and returns vectors with a defined dimensionality.</p><p>Example:</p><pre>text = \"Retrieval Augmented Generation\"</pre><p>Input:</p><pre>\"Retrieval Augmented Generation\"</pre><p>Output:</p><pre>[\n    0.123,\n    -0.456,\n    0.789,\n    0.234,\n    ...\n]</pre><p>Suppose the model returns 768 values.</p><p>Then:</p><pre>Input:\n1 text passage\n\nOutput:\n1 vector containing 768 numbers</pre><p>If you send 100 text passages, a batch embedding API may return 100 vectors.</p><pre>100 Text Chunks\n       |\n       v\nEmbedding Model\n       |\n       v\n100 Embedding Vectors</pre><h3>Python example using Sentence Transformers</h3><p>Install:</p><pre>uv add sentence-transformers</pre><p>Implementation:</p><pre>from sentence_transformers import SentenceTransformer\n\nmodel = SentenceTransformer(\n    \"sentence-transformers/all-MiniLM-L6-v2\"\n)\n\ntexts = [\n    \"Machine learning is a branch of AI.\",\n    \"Deep learning uses neural networks.\",\n    \"RAG combines retrieval with generation.\"\n]\n\nembeddings = model.encode(texts)\n\nprint(\"Shape:\", embeddings.shape)\nprint(\"First vector:\", embeddings[0])</pre><p>The output shape should be:</p><pre>(3, 384)</pre><p>This means:</p><ul><li>3 input text passages.</li><li>Each passage is represented by 384 numbers.</li></ul><p>The model may download files on its first run.</p><h3>Check a single embedding</h3><pre>text = \"Machine learning is a branch of AI.\"\n\nembedding = model.encode(text)\n\nprint(type(embedding))\nprint(len(embedding))</pre><h3>Check multiple embeddings</h3><pre>embeddings = model.encode(texts)\n\nfor index, vector in enumerate(embeddings):\n    print(index, len(vector))</pre><h3>Important</h3><p>Always verify:</p><ul><li>Number of input texts.</li><li>Number of output vectors.</li><li>Vector dimensions.</li><li>Data type.</li><li>Whether vectors are normalized.</li><li>Whether the model supports the input length.</li></ul>" },
      { id: "p7-6", title: "Embedding Dimensions", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Dimensions set vector size, which drives storage, speed and compatibility.</p><p>Embedding dimensions represent the number of numerical values in a vector.</p><p>Example:</p><pre>E = [0.12, 0.34, 0.56, 0.78]</pre><p>This embedding has four dimensions.</p><p>Real models may produce:</p><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Dimensions</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>384</td><td>384 values per vector</td></tr><tr><td>768</td><td>768 values per vector</td></tr><tr><td>1,024</td><td>1,024 values per vector</td></tr><tr><td>1,536</td><td>1,536 values per vector</td></tr><tr><td>3,072</td><td>3,072 values per vector</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>These are examples, not a ranking of model quality.</p><h3>Why do dimensions matter?</h3><p>Dimensions affect:</p><ol><li>Vector storage.</li><li>Memory consumption.</li><li>Index size.</li><li>Search performance.</li><li>Compatibility with vector databases.</li><li>Retrieval quality.</li></ol><h3>Storage calculation</h3><p>Suppose:</p><pre>Number of chunks: 1,000,000\nDimensions: 768\nData type: float32</pre><p>A float32 value requires 4 bytes.</p><p>Calculation:</p><pre>1,000,000 × 768 × 4</pre><p>Result:</p><pre>3,072,000,000 bytes\nApproximately 3.07 GB</pre><p>This is the raw vector storage estimate.</p><p>It excludes metadata, database indexes, replicas, and storage overhead.</p><h3>Compare dimensions</h3><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Dimensions</th><th>Bytes per vector (float32)</th><th>1 million vectors</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>384</td><td>1,536</td><td>1.536 GB</td></tr><tr><td>768</td><td>3,072</td><td>3.072 GB</td></tr><tr><td>1,024</td><td>4,096</td><td>4.096 GB</td></tr><tr><td>1,536</td><td>6,144</td><td>6.144 GB</td></tr><tr><td>3,072</td><td>12,288</td><td>12.288 GB</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Does a higher dimension mean a better embedding?</h3><p>Not necessarily.</p><p>Higher dimensions may provide more representational capacity, but they also increase storage and computational requirements.</p><p>A smaller model can outperform a larger one on a particular retrieval task.</p><p>Evaluate models using your actual documents and questions.</p>" },
      { id: "p7-7", title: "Dense vs Sparse Embeddings", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Dense vectors capture meaning; sparse ones capture exact terms; hybrid search uses both.</p><p>There are two important types of vector representations used in information retrieval.</p><h3>Dense embeddings</h3><p>Dense embeddings contain many numerical values, most of which are non-zero.</p><p>Example:</p><pre>[\n    0.12,\n    -0.45,\n    0.87,\n    0.32,\n    -0.19\n]</pre><p>They are commonly used for semantic similarity.</p><p>Example:</p><pre>Query:\nHow do I reset my password?\n\nDocument:\nInstructions for recovering forgotten login credentials.</pre><p>Dense retrieval can identify semantic relationships even when the words differ.</p><h3>Sparse representations</h3><p>Sparse representations have a very large vocabulary space, but only a relatively small number of non-zero values.</p><p>Example:</p><pre>{\n    15: 0.8,\n    240: 0.6,\n    910: 0.9\n}</pre><p>The indices represent vocabulary features or terms, and the values represent their weights.</p><p>Traditional TF-IDF and BM25 are sparse lexical retrieval approaches. Learned sparse models also exist.</p><h3>Comparison</h3><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Dense</th><th>Sparse</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Representation</td><td>Dense numerical vector</td><td>Mostly zero-valued features</td></tr><tr><td>Focus</td><td>Semantic relationships</td><td>Terms and lexical features</td></tr><tr><td>Synonym handling</td><td>Often strong</td><td>Depends on method</td></tr><tr><td>Exact keyword matching</td><td>May be weaker</td><td>Often strong</td></tr><tr><td>Storage</td><td>Can be substantial</td><td>Depends on sparsity</td></tr><tr><td>Example</td><td>BGE, E5</td><td>BM25, SPLADE</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Hybrid search</h3><p>Many production RAG systems combine dense and sparse retrieval.</p><pre>                User Query\n                    |\n           +--------+--------+\n           |                 |\n           v                 v\n      Dense Search       Sparse Search\n           |                 |\n           v                 v\n      Semantic Results   Keyword Results\n           |                 |\n           +--------+--------+\n                    |\n                    v\n              Result Fusion\n                    |\n                    v\n             Final Retrieval</pre><p>This is called hybrid search.</p><p>It can help when a question contains both semantic intent and exact terms such as product codes, error messages, or technical identifiers.</p>" },
      { id: "p7-8", title: "Document Embeddings vs Query Embeddings", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Chunks and queries are embedded at different times and may need different prefixes.</p><p>In a RAG system, you generally generate embeddings for two types of text:</p><ol><li>Document chunks.</li><li>User queries.</li></ol><h3>Document embeddings</h3><p>Generated during the ingestion process.</p><p>Example:</p><pre>Document Chunk:\nA password can be reset through the account settings page.</pre><p>Embedding model:</p><pre>Document Text\n     |\n     v\nEmbedding Model\n     |\n     v\nDocument Vector</pre><p>The vector is stored in a vector database along with the chunk and metadata.</p><h3>Query embeddings</h3><p>Generated when a user asks a question.</p><p>Example:</p><pre>User Query:\nWhere can I change my password?</pre><p>Embedding model:</p><pre>User Question\n     |\n     v\nEmbedding Model\n     |\n     v\nQuery Vector</pre><p>The query vector is compared with stored document vectors.</p><h3>Comparison</h3><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Document embedding</th><th>Query embedding</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Represents document content</td><td>Represents user intent or question</td></tr><tr><td>Usually generated during ingestion</td><td>Generated during retrieval</td></tr><tr><td>Stored in the vector database</td><td>Usually temporary</td></tr><tr><td>Can be generated in batches</td><td>Usually generated per query</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Important: Query and document compatibility</h3><p>Some embedding models are trained for asymmetric retrieval.</p><p>This means queries and documents have different roles and may require different input instructions or prefixes.</p><p>Example:</p><pre>Query:\nquery: How do I reset my password?\n\nDocument:\npassage: Password reset instructions...</pre><p>The prefixes above are illustrative. Use the exact format required by the model.</p><p>For other embedding models, the same encoding method may be used for both inputs.</p><h4><b>Never assume every model needs query/document prefixes.</b></h4><p>Always follow its model card or API documentation.</p>" },
      { id: "p7-9", title: "Similarity Between Embeddings", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Similarity compares a query vector to chunk vectors using cosine, dot product or distance.</p><p>After converting text into vectors, we need to compare them.</p><p>Suppose:</p><pre>Query:\nHow do I reset my password?\n\nDocument A:\nInstructions for password recovery.\n\nDocument B:\nHow to prepare a vegetable salad.</pre><p>We generate vectors:</p><pre>Q = [0.2, 0.4, 0.8]</pre><pre>A = [0.3, 0.5, 0.7]</pre><pre>B = [-0.8, 0.1, -0.4]</pre><p>These are illustrative vectors.</p><p>We calculate similarity between:</p><ul><li>Query and Document A.</li><li>Query and Document B.</li></ul><p>The document with the stronger similarity may be considered more relevant.</p><h3>Common similarity and distance methods</h3><ol><li>Cosine similarity.</li><li>Dot product.</li><li>Euclidean distance.</li></ol><p>These methods measure different mathematical relationships.</p>" },
      { id: "p7-10", title: "Cosine Similarity", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Cosine similarity compares the direction of two vectors.</p><p>Cosine similarity measures the cosine of the angle between two vectors.</p><p>It is commonly used for semantic similarity.</p><p>The formula is:</p><pre>cosine(A,B) = (A · B) / (‖A‖₂ ‖B‖₂)</pre><p>Where:</p><ul><li><code>A</code> = first vector.</li><li><code>B</code> = second vector.</li><li><code>A · B</code> = dot product.</li><li><code>‖A‖₂</code> = L2 magnitude of vector A.</li><li><code>‖B‖₂</code> = L2 magnitude of vector B.</li></ul><h3>Understanding the result</h3><p>For non-zero vectors:</p><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Similarity</th><th>Interpretation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>+1</td><td>Same direction</td></tr><tr><td>0</td><td>Orthogonal vectors</td></tr><tr><td>-1</td><td>Opposite direction</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>In practical embedding systems, scores often occupy a narrower range.</p><p>A cosine score of 0.8 does not automatically mean that two passages are 80% semantically similar.</p><h3>Example</h3><pre>import numpy as np\n\nA = np.array([1, 2, 3])\nB = np.array([2, 3, 4])\n\nsimilarity = np.dot(A, B) / (\n    np.linalg.norm(A) * np.linalg.norm(B)\n)\n\nprint(similarity)</pre><h3>When is cosine similarity useful?</h3><ul><li>Semantic search.</li><li>Document similarity.</li><li>Query-to-document retrieval.</li><li>Clustering.</li><li>Recommendation systems.</li></ul><h3>Important</h3><p>Cosine similarity is scale-independent: multiplying a vector by a positive constant does not change its direction.</p><p>This makes it useful when vector magnitude should not influence the comparison.</p>" },
      { id: "p7-11", title: "Dot Product", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Dot product multiplies matching values and sums them.</p><p>The dot product is an operation between two vectors of equal dimensions.</p><p>Formula:</p><pre>A · B = Σ(i=1..n) A_i B_i</pre><p>For example:</p><pre>A = [1,2,3]</pre><pre>B = [4,5,6]</pre><p>Then:</p><pre>A · B = (1 × 4)+(2 × 5)+(3 × 6)</pre><pre>=4+10+18=32</pre><h3>Python implementation</h3><pre>import numpy as np\n\nA = np.array([1, 2, 3])\nB = np.array([4, 5, 6])\n\nresult = np.dot(A, B)\n\nprint(result)</pre><p>Output:</p><pre>32</pre><h3>Dot product in RAG</h3><p>A vector database can use dot product as a retrieval scoring function.</p><p>It measures both vector direction and magnitude.</p><h3>Relationship with cosine similarity</h3><p>If both vectors are L2-normalized:</p><pre>‖A‖₂ = 1,   ‖B‖₂ = 1</pre><p>Then:</p><pre>A · B = cosine(A,B)</pre><p>This is why dot product can be efficient for cosine-style retrieval when vectors are normalized.</p><h3>When to use it</h3><p>Use dot product when:</p><ul><li>The model recommends dot-product scoring.</li><li>Vectors are trained for maximum inner-product retrieval.</li><li>Your vector database supports efficient inner-product search.</li></ul><p>Do not assume dot product and cosine similarity produce the same ranking for unnormalized vectors.</p>" },
      { id: "p7-12", title: "Euclidean Distance", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Euclidean distance is the straight-line distance, where smaller means closer.</p><p>Euclidean distance measures the straight-line distance between two points in vector space.</p><p>Formula:</p><pre>d(A,B)=√(Σ(i=1..n)(A_i-B_i)²)</pre><p>Example:</p><pre>A=[1,2,3]</pre><pre>B=[4,5,6]</pre><p>Calculation:</p><pre>d(A,B)=√((1-4)²+(2-5)²+(3-6)²)</pre><pre>=√(9+9+9)</pre><pre>=√(27)</pre><pre>≈ 5.196</pre><h3>Python implementation</h3><pre>import numpy as np\n\nA = np.array([1, 2, 3])\nB = np.array([4, 5, 6])\n\ndistance = np.linalg.norm(A - B)\n\nprint(distance)</pre><h3>Interpretation</h3><p>Unlike similarity scores, smaller Euclidean distances indicate closer vectors.</p><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Distance</th><th>Interpretation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0</td><td>Identical vectors</td></tr><tr><td>Small</td><td>Close vectors</td></tr><tr><td>Large</td><td>Farther vectors</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>When is it useful?</h3><ul><li>Vector retrieval.</li><li>Clustering.</li><li>Nearest-neighbor search.</li><li>Numerical analysis.</li></ul><h3>Comparison of the three methods</h3><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Measures</th><th>Better result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Cosine similarity</td><td>Directional similarity</td><td>Higher</td></tr><tr><td>Dot product</td><td>Inner product</td><td>Depends on scoring setup; often higher</td></tr><tr><td>Euclidean distance</td><td>Straight-line distance</td><td>Lower</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Important RAG rule</h3><p>Choose the distance metric recommended by your embedding model.</p><p>The metric used to build the vector index must match the intended retrieval scoring behavior.</p><p>For normalized vectors, cosine similarity, dot product, and Euclidean distance have closely related rankings. Without normalization, their rankings can differ.</p>" },
      { id: "p7-13", title: "How to Select an Embedding Model", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Pick an embedding model by testing it on your own data, not by popularity.</p><p>Selecting an embedding model is an important architecture decision.</p><p>A model that performs well on a public benchmark may not perform equally well on your business documents.</p><p>For example, your RAG application may contain:</p><ul><li>Technical documentation.</li><li>Legal documents.</li><li>Financial reports.</li><li>Product descriptions.</li><li>Telugu and English documents.</li><li>Customer support conversations.</li></ul><p>Different models may perform differently across these datasets.</p><h3>Selection criteria</h3><p>Evaluate the following:</p><ol><li>Retrieval accuracy.</li><li>Semantic understanding.</li><li>Model architecture.</li><li>Embedding dimensions.</li><li>Maximum input token length.</li><li>Language support.</li><li>Query/document compatibility.</li><li>Model size.</li><li>Inference latency.</li><li>Hardware requirements.</li><li>API pricing.</li><li>Licensing.</li><li>Deployment requirements.</li><li>Benchmark performance.</li></ol><h3>Recommended selection process</h3><pre>Define RAG Use Case\n       |\n       v\nIdentify Data Types\n       |\n       v\nPrepare Evaluation Dataset\n       |\n       v\nShortlist Embedding Models\n       |\n       v\nGenerate Embeddings\n       |\n       v\nRun Retrieval Evaluation\n       |\n       v\nMeasure Latency and Cost\n       |\n       v\nCompare Results\n       |\n       v\nSelect Model</pre><h3>Important</h3><p>Do not select an embedding model only based on:</p><ul><li>Popularity.</li><li>Number of dimensions.</li><li>Model size.</li><li>Benchmark ranking.</li><li>API price.</li></ul><p>Choose based on your actual retrieval requirements.</p>" },
      { id: "p7-14", title: "Open-Source vs Proprietary Models", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Open-weight models run on your hardware; API models are managed for you.</p><p>Embedding models can be accessed through different distribution and deployment approaches.</p><h3>Open-source / open-weight models</h3><p>These models can often be downloaded and executed locally, subject to their licenses and usage conditions.</p><p>Examples:</p><ul><li>BGE.</li><li>E5.</li><li>Nomic Embed.</li><li>GTE.</li><li>Many Sentence Transformers models.</li></ul><p>Advantages:</p><ul><li>Local deployment.</li><li>Greater infrastructure control.</li><li>No per-request API fee when self-hosted.</li><li>Customization and fine-tuning may be possible.</li><li>Can support private data processing.</li></ul><p>Limitations:</p><ul><li>Infrastructure costs.</li><li>GPU or CPU requirements.</li><li>Model management.</li><li>Scaling complexity.</li><li>Operational maintenance.</li><li>License restrictions may apply.</li></ul><h3>Proprietary API-based models</h3><p>These models are accessed through provider APIs.</p><p>Examples:</p><ul><li>OpenAI Embeddings.</li><li>Cohere Embed.</li></ul><p>Advantages:</p><ul><li>Managed infrastructure.</li><li>Easy integration.</li><li>No need to operate model servers.</li><li>Provider handles much of the model serving.</li></ul><p>Limitations:</p><ul><li>API costs.</li><li>Network latency.</li><li>Provider dependency.</li><li>Data governance considerations.</li><li>Rate limits.</li><li>Model availability and version changes.</li></ul><h3>Comparison</h3><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Factor</th><th>Open-weight / local</th><th>Proprietary API</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Deployment</td><td>Self-hosted</td><td>Provider-hosted</td></tr><tr><td>Infrastructure</td><td>Your responsibility</td><td>Provider-managed</td></tr><tr><td>Pricing</td><td>Infrastructure and operations</td><td>API usage</td></tr><tr><td>Customization</td><td>Depends on model and license</td><td>Usually more limited</td></tr><tr><td>Data control</td><td>Greater local control</td><td>Depends on provider terms</td></tr><tr><td>Scaling</td><td>Your responsibility</td><td>Provider-managed within limits</td></tr><tr><td>Maintenance</td><td>Your responsibility</td><td>Provider-managed</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"callout\"><b>Important</b>Open-weight does not automatically mean unrestricted commercial use. Review the exact license.</div>" },
      { id: "p7-15", title: "Model Size and Performance", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Bigger models are not always better, so measure quality against cost and speed.</p><p>Embedding models come in different sizes.</p><p>Model size can affect:</p><ul><li>Memory usage.</li><li>CPU/GPU requirements.</li><li>Inference latency.</li><li>Throughput.</li><li>Deployment cost.</li></ul><h3>Example</h3><p>Imagine comparing three models:</p><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Relative size</th><th>Potential deployment requirement</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Small</td><td>Low</td><td>CPU or small GPU</td></tr><tr><td>Base</td><td>Medium</td><td>More memory and compute</td></tr><tr><td>Large</td><td>High</td><td>More compute resources</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>These are conceptual categories, not exact specifications.</p><h3>Is a larger model always better?</h3><p>No.</p><p>A smaller model may:</p><ul><li>Respond faster.</li><li>Cost less to host.</li><li>Use less memory.</li><li>Perform sufficiently well for your dataset.</li></ul><p>A larger model may offer better retrieval quality for some tasks, but you must measure the difference.</p><h3>What should you benchmark?</h3><ul><li>Recall@K.</li><li>MRR.</li><li>NDCG.</li><li>p50 and p95 latency.</li><li>Documents processed per second.</li><li>Memory consumption.</li><li>Cost per 1,000 queries.</li></ul><p>Choose a model based on the trade-off between quality and operational cost.</p>" },
      { id: "p7-16", title: "Embedding Dimensions and Storage", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Storage is vectors × dimensions × bytes per value.</p><p>Embedding dimensions determine how many values are stored for each vector.</p><p>Example:</p><pre>Model A:\n384 dimensions\n\nModel B:\n768 dimensions\n\nModel C:\n1536 dimensions</pre><h3>Storage calculation</h3><p>Formula:</p><pre>Storage = N × D × B</pre><p>Where:</p><ul><li><code>N</code> = number of vectors.</li><li><code>D</code> = vector dimensions.</li><li><code>B</code> = bytes per numerical value.</li></ul><p>For float32:</p><pre>B=4</pre><p>Example:</p><pre>Vectors: 500,000\nDimensions: 768\nData type: float32</pre><pre>500,000 × 768 × 4</pre><pre>1,536,000,000 bytes\nApproximately 1.536 GB</pre><h3>Things to consider</h3><ul><li>Vector dimensions.</li><li>Numerical precision.</li><li>Index overhead.</li><li>Metadata size.</li><li>Replication.</li><li>Quantization.</li><li>Database storage format.</li></ul><h3>Can dimensions be reduced?</h3><p>Some models support configurable embedding dimensions or Matryoshka-style representations.</p><p>Dimensionality reduction techniques may also be applied in certain workflows.</p><p>However, reducing dimensions can affect retrieval quality.</p><p>Always validate the reduced representation before production use.</p>" },
      { id: "p7-17", title: "Maximum Input Token Length", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Every embedding model has a maximum input size, which is a limit rather than a target.</p><p>Every embedding model has an input-length constraint.</p><p>Example:</p><pre>Embedding model maximum input:\n8,192 tokens\n\nInput chunk:\n9,000 tokens\n\nResult:\nInput exceeds the supported limit.</pre><p>The exact maximum depends on the model and provider.</p><h3>Why does this matter?</h3><p>Suppose you created chunks of 2,000 tokens in Phase 05.</p><p>You select a model that supports a maximum of 512 tokens.</p><p>Your existing chunks may not fit.</p><p>You need to revisit chunking.</p><h3>Selection checklist</h3><p>Before choosing an embedding model, check:</p><ul><li>Maximum input tokens.</li><li>Tokenizer.</li><li>Whether the limit includes special tokens.</li><li>Whether long inputs are truncated or rejected.</li><li>Maximum batch size.</li><li>Maximum request payload.</li><li>Recommended input length.</li></ul><h3>Important</h3><p>Maximum input length is a technical limit, not a recommendation for optimal chunk size.</p><p>A model may accept a 4,000-token chunk, but smaller chunks may provide better retrieval quality for your dataset.</p>" },
      { id: "p7-18", title: "Multilingual Embedding Models", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Multilingual models embed several languages into one space and need testing per language.</p><p>Multilingual embedding models support semantic representation across multiple languages.</p><p>This is important when your documents and queries use different languages.</p><p>Example:</p><pre>Document:\nYour password can be reset from account settings.\n\nQuery:\nనేను నా పాస్‌వర్డ్‌ను ఎలా మార్చాలి?</pre><p>The document is in English.</p><p>The query is in Telugu.</p><p>A suitable multilingual embedding model may retrieve the English document for the Telugu question.</p><h3>Where multilingual embeddings are useful</h3><ul><li>International customer support.</li><li>Multilingual company knowledge bases.</li><li>Regional-language search.</li><li>Cross-language retrieval.</li><li>Multilingual RAG applications.</li></ul><h3>What to evaluate?</h3><ol><li>Language coverage.</li><li>Cross-language retrieval quality.</li><li>Script support.</li><li>Code-switching support.</li><li>Performance on regional languages.</li><li>Domain-specific terminology.</li><li>Query/document compatibility.</li></ol><h3>Important</h3><p>A model claiming multilingual support does not guarantee equally strong performance for every language.</p><p>Create evaluation queries in the actual languages your users will use.</p><p>For example, if your knowledge base contains English, Telugu, and Hindi, test all three.</p>" },
      { id: "p7-19", title: "Domain-Specific Embedding Models", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Domain-specific models may help specialized text, but must beat general models on your tests.</p><p>A general embedding model is trained to represent broad language patterns.</p><p>However, specialized domains can contain terminology that general models may not represent optimally.</p><p>Examples:</p><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Example terminology</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Healthcare</td><td>Diagnosis, clinical terminology</td></tr><tr><td>Finance</td><td>Assets, liabilities, derivatives</td></tr><tr><td>Legal</td><td>Jurisdiction, indemnification</td></tr><tr><td>Software</td><td>Stack trace, dependency injection</td></tr><tr><td>Scientific research</td><td>Molecular structures, experimental terminology</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Domain-specific models</h3><p>Some models are designed or fine-tuned for particular domains or retrieval tasks.</p><p>They may provide better retrieval for specialized datasets.</p><h3>Example</h3><p>User query:</p><pre>How does dependency injection work?</pre><p>A general model may retrieve a basic software engineering explanation.</p><p>A model adapted for software engineering retrieval may perform better on technical documentation.</p><h3>Should you always use a domain-specific model?</h3><p>No.</p><p>Evaluate general-purpose and domain-specific models using the same test dataset.</p><p>A domain-specific model is useful only when it improves the results enough to justify its additional constraints or operational costs.</p>" },
      { id: "p7-20", title: "Query and Document Compatibility", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Queries and documents must use the same embedding model and configuration.</p><p>This is a critical topic.</p><p>The query and document embeddings must belong to compatible vector spaces.</p><p>Suppose:</p><pre>Document embedding model:\nModel A\n\nQuery embedding model:\nModel B</pre><p>Even if both models return 768-dimensional vectors, their vectors may not be comparable.</p><h3>Why?</h3><p>Embedding models learn their own representation spaces.</p><p>The meaning of the numerical coordinates depends on the model's training and learned parameters.</p><p>Matching dimensions does not mean matching vector spaces.</p><h3>Example</h3><pre>Document:\n    |\n    v\nEmbedding Model A\n    |\n    v\nDocument Vector\n\n\nQuery:\n    |\n    v\nEmbedding Model B\n    |\n    v\nQuery Vector</pre><p>These vectors should not be assumed compatible.</p><h3>Golden rule</h3><h4><b>Use the same embedding model and compatible configuration for document and query embeddings.</b></h4><p>For models with asymmetric retrieval training, use the model's documented query and passage instructions.</p><h3>What if you change the model?</h3><p>If you change the embedding model, you generally need to:</p><ol><li>Select the new model.</li><li>Generate embeddings for all document chunks again.</li><li>Rebuild or replace the vector index.</li><li>Update the query embedding pipeline.</li><li>Validate retrieval quality.</li><li>Deploy the new index and model configuration together.</li></ol><p>Do not mix old and new embeddings in the same retrieval space unless the model explicitly supports that use case.</p>" },
      { id: "p7-21", title: "Embedding Model Benchmarking", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Benchmark candidate models on the same dataset and the same steps.</p><p>Benchmarking means comparing models using the same evaluation dataset and evaluation process.</p><p>Do not choose a model based only on its published benchmark score.</p><h3>Step 1: Prepare a dataset</h3><p>Example:</p><pre>[\n  {\n    \"query\": \"How can I reset my password?\",\n    \"relevant_chunk_ids\": [\"chunk_001\", \"chunk_004\"]\n  },\n  {\n    \"query\": \"How do I configure email notifications?\",\n    \"relevant_chunk_ids\": [\"chunk_010\"]\n  }\n]</pre><h3>Step 2: Select candidate models</h3><p>Example:</p><pre>Model A: BGE\nModel B: E5\nModel C: Nomic Embed\nModel D: OpenAI Embeddings</pre><h3>Step 3: Generate document embeddings</h3><p>Use each model to generate embeddings for the same document chunks.</p><h3>Step 4: Generate query embeddings</h3><p>Use the compatible query encoding method for each model.</p><h3>Step 5: Retrieve the top K chunks</h3><p>For each query, retrieve the top 5 or top 10 results.</p><h3>Step 6: Evaluate the results</h3><p>Measure:</p><ul><li>Recall@K.</li><li>Precision@K.</li><li>MRR.</li><li>NDCG.</li><li>Latency.</li><li>Storage.</li><li>Cost.</li></ul><h3>Example benchmark report</h3><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Metric</th><th>Model A</th><th>Model B</th><th>Model C</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Recall@5</td><td>0.82</td><td>0.88</td><td>0.85</td></tr><tr><td>MRR@10</td><td>0.71</td><td>0.79</td><td>0.76</td></tr><tr><td>p95 latency</td><td>120 ms</td><td>160 ms</td><td>110 ms</td></tr><tr><td>Dimensions</td><td>384</td><td>768</td><td>768</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>These values are illustrative only.</p><h3>Why benchmark?</h3><p>It helps you answer:</p><ul><li>Which model retrieves the right information?</li><li>Which model is faster?</li><li>Which model costs less?</li><li>Which model performs better on your language and domain?</li><li>Which model is suitable for production?</li></ul>" },
      { id: "p7-22", title: "Retrieval Accuracy Evaluation", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Judge embeddings by retrieval results using Precision@K, Recall@K, MRR and NDCG.</p><p>An embedding model should be evaluated by its retrieval results, not just by the appearance of its vectors.</p><h3>Precision@K</h3><p>Measures the proportion of retrieved results that are relevant.</p><pre>Precision@K = (Relevant retrieved results) / (K)</pre><p>Example:</p><pre>Retrieved 5 chunks.\n\nRelevant chunks = 4.\n\nPrecision@5 = 4 / 5 = 0.8</pre><h3>Recall@K</h3><p>Measures how many of the known relevant results were retrieved.</p><pre>Recall@K = (Relevant retrieved results) / (Total relevant results)</pre><p>Example:</p><pre>Total relevant chunks = 4.\n\nRetrieved relevant chunks = 3.\n\nRecall@5 = 3 / 4 = 0.75</pre><h3>Mean Reciprocal Rank (MRR)</h3><p>Measures how highly the first relevant result appears.</p><pre>RR = (1) / (Rank of first relevant result)</pre><p>MRR is the average reciprocal rank across queries.</p><p>Example:</p><pre>First relevant result appears at rank 2.\n\nRR = 1 / 2 = 0.5</pre><h3>Normalized Discounted Cumulative Gain (NDCG)</h3><p>Measures ranking quality while considering the relevance of each result and its position.</p><p>It is particularly useful when relevance has multiple grades, such as:</p><ul><li>Highly relevant.</li><li>Partially relevant.</li><li>Not relevant.</li></ul><h3>Which metrics should you use?</h3><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Metric</th><th>What it helps measure</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Precision@K</td><td>Quality of retrieved results</td></tr><tr><td>Recall@K</td><td>Coverage of relevant results</td></tr><tr><td>MRR</td><td>Position of the first relevant result</td></tr><tr><td>NDCG</td><td>Overall ranking quality</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Important distinction</h3><p>Retrieval accuracy is not the same as final answer accuracy.</p><p>A complete RAG evaluation should also measure:</p><ul><li>Faithfulness.</li><li>Answer relevance.</li><li>Context relevance.</li><li>Correctness.</li><li>Citation quality, where applicable.</li></ul><p>An embedding model is only one part of the RAG system.</p>" },
      { id: "p7-23", title: "Latency and Inference Cost", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Compare latency, throughput and cost, not just quality.</p><p>Embedding generation consumes compute resources.</p><p>Latency is the time taken to generate embeddings.</p><p>Cost is the expense of running the embedding model.</p><h3>API-based model costs</h3><p>API providers may charge based on:</p><ul><li>Input tokens.</li><li>Request volume.</li><li>Model-specific pricing.</li></ul><p>Check the provider's current pricing and usage limits.</p><h3>Local model costs</h3><p>Local models may have no per-request API fee, but they still consume resources.</p><p>Consider:</p><ul><li>CPU usage.</li><li>GPU usage.</li><li>RAM.</li><li>VRAM.</li><li>Electricity.</li><li>Infrastructure hosting.</li><li>Maintenance.</li></ul><h3>Important metrics</h3><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Metric</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Latency</td><td>Time taken per request</td></tr><tr><td>Throughput</td><td>Inputs processed per unit of time</td></tr><tr><td>p50 latency</td><td>Median latency</td></tr><tr><td>p95 latency</td><td>Latency below which 95% of requests complete</td></tr><tr><td>Cost per 1K queries</td><td>Estimated query processing cost</td></tr><tr><td>Embeddings per second</td><td>Processing throughput</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Example</h3><p>Suppose:</p><pre>Model A:\nLatency: 50 ms\nRecall@5: 0.80\n\nModel B:\nLatency: 120 ms\nRecall@5: 0.91</pre><p>Which one is better?</p><p>It depends on your application.</p><p>For an internal research assistant, higher retrieval quality may justify additional latency.</p><p>For a high-volume real-time application, you may need a different trade-off.</p>" },
      { id: "p7-24", title: "Local vs API-Based Embedding Models", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Local models give control; API models give simplicity.</p><p>There are two common ways to generate embeddings.</p><h3>Option A: Local embedding generation</h3><p>You download a model and execute it using your own infrastructure.</p><p>Example:</p><pre>from sentence_transformers import SentenceTransformer\n\nmodel = SentenceTransformer(\n    \"BAAI/bge-small-en-v1.5\"\n)\n\ntexts = [\n    \"What is machine learning?\",\n    \"What is deep learning?\"\n]\n\nembeddings = model.encode(texts)\n\nprint(embeddings.shape)</pre><p>Advantages:</p><ul><li>Greater control over infrastructure.</li><li>Local processing.</li><li>Can reduce per-request API costs at scale.</li><li>Can work without an external inference API after model setup.</li></ul><p>Limitations:</p><ul><li>Requires infrastructure.</li><li>Model downloads and updates.</li><li>Resource management.</li><li>Scaling and monitoring.</li><li>Hardware performance constraints.</li></ul><h3>Option B: API-based embedding generation</h3><p>You send text to an embedding provider and receive vectors.</p><p>Conceptual example using the OpenAI Python SDK:</p><pre>from openai import OpenAI\n\nclient = OpenAI()\n\nresponse = client.embeddings.create(\n    model=\"text-embedding-3-small\",\n    input=[\n        \"What is machine learning?\",\n        \"What is deep learning?\"\n    ]\n)\n\nembeddings = [\n    item.embedding\n    for item in response.data\n]\n\nprint(len(embeddings))</pre><p>Install:</p><pre>uv add openai</pre><p>Configure your API key securely using an environment variable rather than hardcoding it.</p><p>Advantages:</p><ul><li>Easy integration.</li><li>No need to operate a model server.</li><li>Managed infrastructure.</li><li>Straightforward batch requests.</li></ul><p>Limitations:</p><ul><li>API usage costs.</li><li>External dependency.</li><li>Rate limits.</li><li>Network latency.</li><li>Data governance considerations.</li></ul><h3>Comparison</h3><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Factor</th><th>Local</th><th>API</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Model hosting</td><td>Your infrastructure</td><td>Provider infrastructure</td></tr><tr><td>Initial setup</td><td>More involved</td><td>Usually simpler</td></tr><tr><td>Scaling</td><td>Your responsibility</td><td>Provider-managed</td></tr><tr><td>Per-request API cost</td><td>No external API fee</td><td>Usually charged</td></tr><tr><td>Hardware cost</td><td>Your responsibility</td><td>Included in provider service</td></tr><tr><td>Data control</td><td>Greater control</td><td>Depends on provider terms</td></tr><tr><td>Maintenance</td><td>Your responsibility</td><td>Provider-managed model serving</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>How to choose?</h3><p>Choose local models when you need infrastructure control, customization, or local processing.</p><p>Choose API models when you need simple integration and managed inference.</p><p>You can also design your RAG system to support both through a common embedding interface.</p><p>These are model families and services worth studying. Their exact model versions, dimensions, token limits, licenses, and capabilities vary.</p>" },
      { id: "p7-25", title: "BGE – BAAI General Embedding", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> BGE is a family of embedding models from BAAI.</p><p>BGE stands for BAAI General Embedding.</p><p>It is a family of embedding models developed by the Beijing Academy of Artificial Intelligence.</p><p>Examples:</p><ul><li>BGE-small.</li><li>BGE-base.</li><li>BGE-large.</li><li>BGE-M3.</li></ul><h3>What to study</h3><ul><li>BGE model architecture.</li><li>Query instructions.</li><li>Passage instructions.</li><li>Dense retrieval.</li><li>Sparse retrieval capabilities in relevant variants.</li><li>Reranking models.</li><li>Multilingual support.</li></ul><h3>Python example</h3><pre>from sentence_transformers import SentenceTransformer\n\nmodel = SentenceTransformer(\n    \"BAAI/bge-small-en-v1.5\"\n)\n\ndocuments = [\n    \"RAG combines retrieval with generation.\",\n    \"Machine learning uses data to learn patterns.\"\n]\n\nembeddings = model.encode(documents)\n\nprint(embeddings.shape)</pre><div class=\"callout\"><b>Note</b>Search for the BAAI BGE models and their official model cards.</div>" },
      { id: "p7-26", title: "E5 Embeddings", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> E5 models often need query: and passage: prefixes.</p><p>E5 is a family of text embedding models developed for general-purpose embedding and retrieval tasks.</p><p>Examples include:</p><ul><li>E5-small.</li><li>E5-base.</li><li>E5-large.</li><li>Multilingual E5 variants.</li></ul><h3>Important concept</h3><p>Some E5 models use specific prefixes:</p><pre>query: ...\npassage: ...</pre><p>Example:</p><pre>from sentence_transformers import SentenceTransformer\n\nmodel = SentenceTransformer(\n    \"intfloat/e5-base-v2\"\n)\n\nqueries = [\n    \"query: What is retrieval augmented generation?\"\n]\n\ndocuments = [\n    \"passage: RAG combines retrieval systems with language models.\"\n]\n\nquery_embedding = model.encode(queries)\n\ndocument_embedding = model.encode(documents)\n\nprint(query_embedding.shape)\nprint(document_embedding.shape)</pre><p>Follow the exact instructions for the chosen E5 variant.</p>" },
      { id: "p7-27", title: "Nomic Embed", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Nomic Embed supports long context and task prefixes.</p><p>Nomic Embed is a family of embedding models developed by Nomic.</p><p>Example:</p><pre>nomic-ai/nomic-embed-text-v1.5</pre><h3>What to study</h3><ul><li>Long-context embedding support.</li><li>Task prefixes.</li><li>Matryoshka representation learning in supported variants.</li><li>Local inference.</li><li>Retrieval quality.</li><li>Dimension configuration.</li></ul><p>Some Nomic model variants use prefixes such as:</p><pre>search_query:\nsearch_document:</pre><p>Check the exact model card before implementation.</p>" },
      { id: "p7-28", title: "GTE – General Text Embeddings", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> GTE is a family of general text embedding models.</p><p>GTE is a family of general text embedding models.</p><p>Examples include:</p><ul><li>GTE-small.</li><li>GTE-base.</li><li>GTE-large.</li><li>GTE multilingual variants.</li></ul><h3>What to study</h3><ul><li>Model size.</li><li>Retrieval performance.</li><li>Input token limits.</li><li>Multilingual capabilities.</li><li>Embedding dimensions.</li><li>Model licensing.</li></ul><p>GTE models are available through model repositories such as Hugging Face.</p>" },
      { id: "p7-29", title: "Sentence Transformers", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Sentence Transformers is the Python framework for local embeddings.</p><p>Sentence Transformers is a Python framework and ecosystem for generating sentence and text embeddings.</p><p>It supports many pretrained embedding models.</p><h3>Why study it?</h3><ul><li>Easy model loading.</li><li>Local embedding generation.</li><li>Batch encoding.</li><li>Similarity calculation.</li><li>Model evaluation.</li><li>Training and fine-tuning workflows.</li></ul><h3>Installation</h3><pre>uv add sentence-transformers</pre><h3>Example</h3><pre>from sentence_transformers import SentenceTransformer\n\nmodel = SentenceTransformer(\n    \"sentence-transformers/all-MiniLM-L6-v2\"\n)\n\nsentences = [\n    \"The cat sits on the mat.\",\n    \"A cat is sitting on a rug.\",\n    \"The stock market is changing.\"\n]\n\nembeddings = model.encode(\n    sentences,\n    normalize_embeddings=True\n)\n\nprint(embeddings.shape)</pre><h3>Similarity example</h3><pre>from sentence_transformers import util\n\nsimilarity = util.cos_sim(\n    embeddings[0],\n    embeddings[1]\n)\n\nprint(similarity)</pre><h3>What to study</h3><ul><li>SentenceTransformer.</li><li><code>encode()</code>.</li><li><code>normalize_embeddings</code>.</li><li>Cosine similarity.</li><li>Pretrained models.</li><li>Cross-encoder rerankers.</li><li>Evaluation tools.</li></ul>" },
      { id: "p7-30", title: "OpenAI Embeddings", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> OpenAI offers embedding models through its API.</p><p>OpenAI provides embedding models through its API.</p><p>Examples include:</p><ul><li><code>text-embedding-3-small</code></li><li><code>text-embedding-3-large</code></li></ul><h3>What to study</h3><ul><li>API integration.</li><li>Input token limits.</li><li>Dimensions.</li><li>Optional dimension configuration where supported.</li><li>Batch processing.</li><li>Token usage.</li><li>API pricing.</li><li>Error handling.</li><li>Rate limits.</li></ul><h3>Example</h3><pre>from openai import OpenAI\n\nclient = OpenAI()\n\nresponse = client.embeddings.create(\n    model=\"text-embedding-3-small\",\n    input=\"Retrieval Augmented Generation\"\n)\n\nembedding = response.data[0].embedding\n\nprint(\"Dimensions:\", len(embedding))</pre><p>The exact output dimensions and available configuration depend on the selected model and API options.</p>" },
      { id: "p7-31", title: "Cohere Embed", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Cohere Embed uses input types for queries and documents.</p><p>Cohere provides embedding models through its API.</p><p>Its Embed model family includes models designed for different retrieval and language use cases.</p><h3>What to study</h3><ul><li>Embed API.</li><li>Query and document input types.</li><li>Input type configuration.</li><li>Multilingual retrieval.</li><li>Embedding dimensions.</li><li>API usage and pricing.</li></ul><h3>Important concept</h3><p>Some Cohere embedding workflows explicitly distinguish between:</p><ul><li>Search queries.</li><li>Search documents.</li><li>Classification.</li><li>Clustering.</li></ul><p>Use the input type required by the selected model and API version.</p>" },
      { id: "p7-32", title: "Project: Build an Embedding Generation Engine", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Build one reusable engine that embeds, compares and saves vectors with metadata.</p><h3>Objective</h3><p>Build a reusable Python component that accepts document chunks, generates embeddings using multiple models, evaluates similarity, and saves vectors with metadata.</p><h3>Features</h3><ol><li>Load chunks from JSONL.</li><li>Select an embedding model.</li><li>Generate embeddings.</li><li>Generate embeddings in batches.</li><li>Generate query embeddings.</li><li>Validate vector dimensions.</li><li>Calculate cosine similarity.</li><li>Calculate dot product.</li><li>Calculate Euclidean distance.</li><li>Compare multiple embedding models.</li><li>Measure embedding latency.</li><li>Save vectors and metadata.</li><li>Generate a model comparison report.</li></ol><h3>Suggested folder structure</h3><pre>embedding-engine/\n│\n├── data/\n│   ├── input/\n│   ├── chunks/\n│   └── embeddings/\n│\n├── src/\n│   └── embedding_engine/\n│       ├── __init__.py\n│       ├── base.py\n│       ├── local_embedding.py\n│       ├── api_embedding.py\n│       ├── model_registry.py\n│       ├── similarity.py\n│       ├── batch_processor.py\n│       ├── validator.py\n│       ├── storage.py\n│       └── evaluator.py\n│\n├── tests/\n│   ├── test_embedding.py\n│   ├── test_similarity.py\n│   ├── test_dimensions.py\n│   └── test_evaluator.py\n│\n├── pyproject.toml\n└── README.md</pre><h3>Recommended abstraction</h3><p>Create a common interface for embedding models.</p><pre>from abc import ABC, abstractmethod\n\n\nclass BaseEmbeddingModel(ABC):\n\n    @abstractmethod\n    def embed_documents(\n        self,\n        texts: list[str]\n    ):\n        pass\n\n    @abstractmethod\n    def embed_query(\n        self,\n        text: str\n    ):\n        pass\n\n    @abstractmethod\n    def get_dimensions(self) -&gt; int:\n        pass</pre><p>Then implement different providers:</p><pre>BaseEmbeddingModel\n       |\n       +---- SentenceTransformerEmbedding\n       |\n       +---- OpenAIEmbedding\n       |\n       +---- CohereEmbedding\n       |\n       +---- LocalBGEEmbedding\n       |\n       +---- LocalE5Embedding</pre><p>This design allows you to switch embedding providers without rewriting your entire RAG pipeline.</p><h3>Example embedding record</h3><pre>{\n  \"chunk_id\": \"chunk_001\",\n  \"document_id\": \"doc_001\",\n  \"embedding_model\": \"all-MiniLM-L6-v2\",\n  \"embedding_dimensions\": 384,\n  \"text\": \"RAG combines retrieval with generation.\",\n  \"embedding\": [0.12, -0.23, 0.45],\n  \"metadata\": {\n    \"page\": 1,\n    \"source\": \"rag_guide.pdf\"\n  }\n}</pre><p>The vector above is shortened for illustration. A real record must contain the complete vector.</p><h3>Completion criteria</h3><ul><li>You can load existing chunks.</li><li>You can generate vectors.</li><li>You can generate query vectors.</li><li>You understand model-specific input formatting.</li><li>You can inspect vector dimensions.</li><li>You can calculate similarity.</li><li>You can compare two embedding models.</li><li>You can save vectors and associated metadata.</li><li>You can measure latency.</li><li>You can validate model compatibility.</li></ul>" },
      { id: "p7-33", title: "Important Rules for Production RAG", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Fifteen rules for using embeddings in production RAG.</p><ol><li>Select embedding models based on retrieval evaluation.</li><li>Use compatible query and document embedding configurations.</li><li>Follow model-specific prefixes and instructions.</li><li>Never mix unrelated embedding spaces.</li><li>Record model name and version.</li><li>Record embedding dimensions.</li><li>Record the similarity metric.</li><li>Track tokenizer and input limits.</li><li>Use batching where supported.</li><li>Validate vector dimensions before storage.</li><li>Monitor embedding latency and cost.</li><li>Evaluate multilingual performance using real queries.</li><li>Re-embed documents when changing embedding models.</li><li>Avoid unnecessary repeated embedding generation.</li><li>Test retrieval quality before deploying a model.</li></ol><h3>Important metadata to store</h3><pre>{\n  \"embedding_model\": \"model-name\",\n  \"model_version\": \"version\",\n  \"embedding_dimensions\": 768,\n  \"similarity_metric\": \"cosine\",\n  \"normalized\": true,\n  \"created_at\": \"2026-11-22\"\n}</pre><p>This is example metadata. Store the actual model version and configuration used by your system.</p>" },
      { id: "p7-34", title: "Quick Revision", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> One-line answers to the key ideas of this phase.</p><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Topic</th><th>What you should understand</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Embedding</td><td>Numerical representation of data</td></tr><tr><td>Vector</td><td>Ordered collection of numerical values</td></tr><tr><td>Dimensions</td><td>Number of values in a vector</td></tr><tr><td>Dense embedding</td><td>Dense numerical representation</td></tr><tr><td>Sparse representation</td><td>Mostly zero-valued features</td></tr><tr><td>Document embedding</td><td>Vector representing a document chunk</td></tr><tr><td>Query embedding</td><td>Vector representing a search query</td></tr><tr><td>Cosine similarity</td><td>Similarity based on vector direction</td></tr><tr><td>Dot product</td><td>Sum of corresponding vector products</td></tr><tr><td>Euclidean distance</td><td>Straight-line vector distance</td></tr><tr><td>Model compatibility</td><td>Query and document vectors must share a compatible representation space</td></tr><tr><td>Benchmarking</td><td>Comparing models on the same evaluation task</td></tr></tbody></table></div>" },
      { id: "p7-35", title: "Hands-On Exercises", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Beginner, intermediate and advanced practice tasks.</p><h3>Beginner</h3><ol><li>Install Sentence Transformers.</li><li>Load <code>all-MiniLM-L6-v2</code>.</li><li>Generate embeddings for five sentences.</li><li>Print the embedding dimensions.</li><li>Calculate cosine similarity between two sentences.</li><li>Compare two related sentences and two unrelated sentences.</li></ol><h3>Intermediate</h3><ol><li>Load chunks from your previous chunking project.</li><li>Generate document embeddings.</li><li>Generate a query embedding.</li><li>Calculate similarity scores.</li><li>Rank chunks by similarity.</li><li>Return the top five chunks.</li><li>Save vectors and metadata to JSONL.</li></ol><h3>Advanced</h3><ol><li>Compare BGE, E5, and MiniLM.</li><li>Test query/document prefixes.</li><li>Compare cosine similarity and dot product.</li><li>Benchmark multiple embedding dimensions where supported.</li><li>Measure latency and throughput.</li><li>Build a multilingual retrieval test.</li><li>Evaluate Recall@5, MRR@10, and NDCG@10.</li><li>Create a model selection report.</li></ol><p>You should now be able to explain:</p><ul><li>What an embedding is.</li><li>How text becomes a numerical vector.</li><li>How embedding models work internally.</li><li>Why vector dimensions matter.</li><li>How dense and sparse retrieval differ.</li><li>How document and query embeddings work.</li><li>How similarity calculations work.</li><li>How to select an embedding model.</li><li>How to compare open-weight and proprietary models.</li><li>How to evaluate embedding quality.</li><li>How to generate embeddings using Python.</li><li>How to design a reusable embedding component.</li></ul><div class=\"callout\"><b>Important</b>Do not move directly to a vector database after generating your first embeddings. First implement similarity search yourself using NumPy. Understand how vectors are compared, how results are ranked, and how top-K retrieval works.</div>" },
      { id: "p7-next", title: "Next: Phase 08 – Vector databases and vector storage", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Phase 08 stores and searches embeddings in vector databases.</p><p>Suggested topics:</p><ol><li>What Is a Vector Database?</li><li>Why RAG Needs Vector Storage</li><li>Vector Database vs Traditional Database</li><li>Vector Storage Fundamentals</li><li>Storing Embeddings and Metadata</li><li>Similarity Search</li><li>Top-K Retrieval</li><li>Exact Nearest Neighbor Search</li><li>Approximate Nearest Neighbor Search</li><li>Vector Indexing</li><li>HNSW Index</li><li>IVF Index</li><li>Distance Metrics in Vector Databases</li><li>Filtering with Metadata</li><li>CRUD Operations</li><li>Collections and Namespaces</li><li>Choosing a Vector Database</li><li>FAISS</li><li>Chroma</li><li>Qdrant</li><li>Milvus</li><li>Weaviate</li><li>Pinecone</li><li>PostgreSQL with pgvector</li><li>Building a Vector Storage Pipeline</li></ol><p><b>Next practical milestone:</b> Build a small semantic search engine using NumPy first, then implement the same retrieval workflow using FAISS or Chroma.</p>" },
      { id: "p8-intro", title: "Vector databases and indexing", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> A vector database stores embeddings with their text and metadata and finds the nearest ones fast.</p><p>Goal: Store embeddings, document chunks, and metadata, and make them searchable using vector similarity.</p><p>In Phase 07, we learned how to convert text chunks into numerical vectors using embedding models. Now we need somewhere to store these vectors. Imagine you have 10,000 documents, 100,000 chunks and 100,000 embedding vectors. You need a system that can efficiently search these vectors and return the most relevant chunks when a user asks a question. This is where vector databases and vector indexes are used.</p><h3>RAG architecture</h3><pre>DOCUMENT INGESTION\n\nDocuments\n    |\n    v\nText Extraction\n    |\n    v\nCleaning\n    |\n    v\nChunking\n    |\n    v\nEmbedding Generation\n    |\n    v\nVector Database\n    |\n    v\nVector Index\n\n\nQUERY RETRIEVAL\n\nUser Query\n    |\n    v\nQuery Embedding\n    |\n    v\nVector Search\n    |\n    v\nTop-K Results\n    |\n    v\nRelevant Chunks</pre>" },
      { id: "p8-what", title: "What is a vector database?", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> A database built to store, index and search vectors together with their records.</p><p>A vector database is a database designed to store, index, and search numerical vectors. These vectors are commonly generated by embedding models.</p><pre>embedding = [\n    0.123,\n    -0.234,\n    0.567,\n    0.891\n]</pre><p>A vector database stores vectors along with associated information such as:</p><ul><li>Document IDs.</li><li>Chunk IDs.</li><li>Original text.</li><li>Metadata.</li><li>Embedding model information.</li></ul><p>Example record:</p><pre>{\n  \"id\": \"chunk_001\",\n  \"vector\": [0.12, -0.34, 0.56],\n  \"text\": \"RAG combines retrieval with generation.\",\n  \"metadata\": {\n    \"document_id\": \"doc_001\",\n    \"source\": \"rag_guide.pdf\",\n    \"page\": 5\n  }\n}</pre><p>The vector above is abbreviated for illustration.</p><h3>What does a vector database do?</h3><ol><li>Stores vectors.</li><li>Creates vector indexes.</li><li>Searches for similar vectors.</li><li>Supports metadata filtering.</li><li>Retrieves associated records.</li><li>Supports insertion, updates, and deletion.</li></ol>" },
      { id: "p8-why", title: "Why vector databases?", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> An index avoids comparing the query against every vector.</p><p>Suppose you store one million embedding vectors. When a user submits a query, you need to find the vectors most similar to the query embedding. A naive implementation compares the query against every stored vector. For small datasets, this is manageable. For large datasets, searching every vector can become expensive. Vector databases provide indexing and retrieval algorithms that make large-scale similarity search more practical.</p><h3>Without a vector index</h3><pre>Query Vector\n     |\n     v\nCompare against Vector 1\n     |\n     v\nCompare against Vector 2\n     |\n     v\nCompare against Vector 3\n     |\n     v\n...\n     |\n     v\nCompare against Vector 1,000,000\n     |\n     v\nSort Results</pre><h3>With a vector index</h3><pre>Query Vector\n     |\n     v\nVector Index\n     |\n     v\nSearch Candidate Vectors\n     |\n     v\nRank Results\n     |\n     v\nTop-K Results</pre><h3>Benefits</h3><ul><li>Efficient similarity search.</li><li>Approximate nearest-neighbor indexing.</li><li>Metadata filtering.</li><li>Persistent vector storage.</li><li>Scalable retrieval.</li><li>Integration with RAG pipelines.</li></ul><div class=\"callout\"><b>Important</b>A vector database is not automatically better than every alternative. For small projects, NumPy or FAISS may be sufficient.</div>" },
      { id: "p8-architecture", title: "Vector storage architecture", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Originals, chunks and embeddings are three kinds of data and need not share one database.</p><p>A typical RAG storage architecture contains three logical types of information.</p><h3>A. Original documents</h3><p>Store the original uploaded files, such as PDF, DOCX, TXT and Markdown. Possible storage systems: local filesystem, AWS S3, Azure Blob Storage, Google Cloud Storage.</p><h3>B. Document chunks</h3><p>Store extracted and processed text.</p><pre>{\n  \"chunk_id\": \"chunk_001\",\n  \"document_id\": \"doc_001\",\n  \"text\": \"RAG combines retrieval with generation.\",\n  \"page\": 5\n}</pre><h3>C. Embeddings</h3><p>Store numerical vectors associated with the chunks.</p><pre>{\n  \"chunk_id\": \"chunk_001\",\n  \"embedding\": [0.12, -0.34, 0.56]\n}</pre><h3>Complete architecture</h3><pre>             Document Storage\n                    |\n                    v\n              Original Files\n                    |\n                    v\n             Document Processing\n                    |\n                    v\n               Chunk Storage\n                    |\n                    v\n            Embedding Generation\n                    |\n                    v\n              Vector Database\n              /             \\\n             /               \\\n      Vector Index       Metadata Index\n             \\               /\n              \\             /\n               Search Results</pre><h3>Important architecture decision</h3><p>You do not necessarily need to store every piece of information in the same database. A production system might use:</p><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Information</th><th>Storage</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Original files</td><td>S3 or filesystem</td></tr><tr><td>Document metadata</td><td>PostgreSQL</td></tr><tr><td>Chunk text</td><td>PostgreSQL or vector database</td></tr><tr><td>Embeddings</td><td>Vector database</td></tr><tr><td>Processing status</td><td>PostgreSQL</td></tr><tr><td>Search index</td><td>Vector database</td></tr></tbody></table></div>" },
      { id: "p8-store-embeddings", title: "Store embeddings", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Each record needs an ID, a vector of the right size, and its metadata.</p><p>An embedding is a numerical vector. To store it, you need:</p><ul><li>Unique record ID.</li><li>Vector values.</li><li>Vector dimensions.</li><li>Associated metadata.</li></ul><p>Example using ChromaDB. Install:</p><pre>uv add chromadb sentence-transformers</pre><pre>import chromadb\nfrom sentence_transformers import SentenceTransformer\n\nmodel = SentenceTransformer(\n    \"sentence-transformers/all-MiniLM-L6-v2\"\n)\n\nclient = chromadb.PersistentClient(\n    path=\"./chroma_db\"\n)\n\ncollection = client.get_or_create_collection(\n    name=\"rag_documents\"\n)\n\ntexts = [\n    \"RAG combines retrieval with generation.\",\n    \"Machine learning uses data to learn patterns.\",\n    \"Deep learning uses neural networks.\"\n]\n\nembeddings = model.encode(texts).tolist()\n\ncollection.add(\n    ids=[\"chunk_001\", \"chunk_002\", \"chunk_003\"],\n    embeddings=embeddings,\n    documents=texts\n)</pre><p>Now the vectors and their associated text are stored in the collection.</p>" },
      { id: "p8-chunk-text", title: "Store chunk text", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Embeddings find the chunk, but the LLM needs the original text.</p><p>The embedding is useful for searching, but the LLM generally needs the original text as context.</p><pre>Embedding:\n[0.12, -0.34, 0.56, ...]\n\nOriginal text:\nRAG combines retrieval with generation.</pre><p>When a user asks a question:</p><ol><li>Generate the query embedding.</li><li>Search the vector database.</li><li>Retrieve the matching records.</li><li>Get their original text.</li><li>Send that text to the LLM.</li></ol><div class=\"callout\"><b>Important</b>Do not assume you can reconstruct the original text from its embedding. An embedding is a learned numerical representation, not a lossless encoding of the original document.</div>" },
      { id: "p8-metadata", title: "Store metadata", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Metadata lets you filter results and cite sources.</p><p>Metadata provides additional information about documents and chunks.</p><pre>{\n  \"document_id\": \"doc_001\",\n  \"chunk_id\": \"chunk_001\",\n  \"source\": \"rag_guide.pdf\",\n  \"page\": 5,\n  \"category\": \"technical\",\n  \"author\": \"admin\",\n  \"created_at\": \"2026-11-22\"\n}</pre><h3>Why is metadata useful?</h3><p>Suppose a user asks: \"Find information about embeddings from the RAG documentation.\" You can filter by:</p><pre>category = technical\nsource = rag_guide.pdf\npage &gt;= 5</pre><p>The supported filtering operators depend on the database.</p><h3>Metadata best practices</h3><ul><li>Use stable document IDs.</li><li>Use unique chunk IDs.</li><li>Track source information.</li><li>Store page or section numbers.</li><li>Store tenant or access-control identifiers where applicable.</li><li>Track document versions.</li><li>Avoid unnecessarily large metadata objects.</li></ul>" },
      { id: "p8-relationships", title: "Document-to-chunk relationships", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> One document has many chunks, and keeping that link makes updates and deletes easy.</p><p>One document can contain multiple chunks.</p><pre>Document: doc_001\n       |\n       +---- chunk_001\n       |\n       +---- chunk_002\n       |\n       +---- chunk_003\n       |\n       +---- chunk_004</pre><p>This is a one-to-many relationship.</p><h3>Why preserve this relationship?</h3><p>It allows you to:</p><ul><li>Identify the original source.</li><li>Retrieve the full document.</li><li>Find neighboring chunks.</li><li>Implement parent-child retrieval.</li><li>Track document versions.</li><li>Delete all chunks associated with a document.</li></ul><h3>Example relational structure</h3><h4><b>Documents table</b></h4><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>document_id</th><th>filename</th><th>version</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>doc_001</td><td>rag.pdf</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>doc_002</td><td>ml.pdf</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4><b>Chunks table</b></h4><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>chunk_id</th><th>document_id</th><th>page</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>chunk_001</td><td>doc_001</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>chunk_002</td><td>doc_001</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>chunk_003</td><td>doc_002</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4><b>Vector records</b></h4><p>Each vector record references its chunk ID. This design makes document lifecycle management easier.</p>" },
      { id: "p8-dimensions", title: "Vector dimensions", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Every vector in an index must have the same number of dimensions.</p><p>Every vector in a particular vector index must follow its configured dimensionality. For example, the model all-MiniLM-L6-v2 produces 384 dimensions, so every vector inserted into that collection must contain 384 values. If you attempt to insert a 768-dimensional vector into a 384-dimensional collection, the operation should fail.</p><p>Why? Vector similarity calculations require compatible vector dimensions.</p><h3>Example validation</h3><pre>def validate_vector(vector, expected_dimensions):\n    if len(vector) != expected_dimensions:\n        raise ValueError(\n            f\"Expected {expected_dimensions} dimensions, \"\n            f\"received {len(vector)}\"\n        )\n\n    return True</pre><div class=\"callout\"><b>Important</b>Dimensions alone do not establish compatibility. Two different embedding models may produce vectors with identical dimensions but incompatible representation spaces. Keep model identity and configuration with the index.</div>" },
      { id: "p8-indexing", title: "Vector indexing", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> An index finds likely nearest neighbors without scanning everything.</p><p>A vector index is a data structure designed to help search vectors efficiently. Without indexing, you may need to compare a query against every stored vector. With an index, the database can identify likely nearest neighbors more efficiently.</p><h3>Why indexing?</h3><p>Suppose you have 1,000,000 vectors with 768 dimensions. A full scan requires distance calculations against all one million vectors. An approximate index can search a smaller candidate set.</p><h3>Types of indexing</h3><ul><li>Flat or brute-force indexes.</li><li>HNSW.</li><li>IVF.</li><li>Product Quantization.</li><li>Other approximate nearest-neighbor structures.</li></ul><p>Each offers different trade-offs involving:</p><ul><li>Search latency.</li><li>Recall.</li><li>Memory.</li><li>Index construction time.</li><li>Update behavior.</li></ul>" },
      { id: "p8-exact", title: "Exact nearest neighbor search", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Compare against every vector: exact, simple, and slow at scale.</p><p>Exact nearest-neighbor search calculates the distance or similarity between the query and every candidate vector, then returns the nearest results. This is also called brute-force search or flat search.</p><pre>query = [0.1, 0.2, 0.3]\n\nvectors = [\n    [0.2, 0.3, 0.4],\n    [0.9, 0.8, 0.7],\n    [0.1, 0.2, 0.4]\n]</pre><p>Calculate the distance between the query and each stored vector, sort by distance, and return the closest results.</p><h3>Advantages</h3><ul><li>Exact results for the selected metric.</li><li>Simple implementation.</li><li>Useful for small datasets.</li><li>Good baseline for evaluating approximate indexes.</li></ul><h3>Limitations</h3><ul><li>Search cost increases with dataset size.</li><li>Large vector collections require substantial computation.</li></ul>" },
      { id: "p8-ann", title: "Approximate nearest neighbor search", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> ANN trades a little accuracy for much faster search, and recall tells you how much.</p><p>Approximate Nearest Neighbor (ANN) search finds vectors that are likely to be among the nearest neighbors without necessarily checking every vector. It trades a small amount of retrieval accuracy for improved search performance.</p><p>With one million vectors, exact search compares against all one million. Approximate search explores a smaller candidate set using a vector index.</p><h3>Why use ANN?</h3><ul><li>Lower search latency.</li><li>Better scalability.</li><li>Efficient large-scale retrieval.</li><li>Reduced search computation.</li></ul><h3>Important metric: recall</h3><p>ANN search must be evaluated against exact nearest-neighbor results.</p><pre>Exact search top 10:\n[A, B, C, D, E, F, G, H, I, J]\n\nANN search top 10:\n[A, B, C, D, F, G, K, L, M, N]</pre><p>The ANN result found 6 of the 10 exact neighbors, so Recall@10 = 6/10 = 0.6. This is an illustrative comparison.</p>" },
      { id: "p8-hnsw", title: "HNSW index", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> HNSW searches a layered graph of nearby vectors.</p><p>HNSW stands for Hierarchical Navigable Small World. It is a graph-based approximate nearest-neighbor indexing algorithm.</p><h3>Basic idea</h3><p>Each vector is represented as a node in a graph. Nodes connect to other nearby nodes. The graph has multiple layers:</p><ul><li>Upper layers: fewer nodes and longer-range navigation.</li><li>Lower layers: more nodes and detailed local navigation.</li></ul><h3>Search process</h3><pre>                Upper Layer\n               /           \\\n              v             v\n          Node A -------- Node B\n               |\n               v\n             Lower Layer\n          /      |       \\\n         v       v        v\n       Node C  Node D   Node E\n                   |\n                   v\n              Candidate Results</pre><p>The search starts in a sparse upper layer and moves toward promising regions before exploring more detailed connections.</p><h3>Important parameters</h3><ul><li><b>M</b>: controls the approximate number of graph connections per node.</li><li><b>ef_construction</b>: controls the search breadth during index construction.</li><li><b>ef_search</b>: controls the search breadth during querying.</li></ul><h3>Trade-offs</h3><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Increasing it generally means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>M</td><td>More connections and memory</td></tr><tr><td>ef_construction</td><td>Better index construction quality, slower build</td></tr><tr><td>ef_search</td><td>Better recall, slower search</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Exact behavior depends on the implementation.</p><h3>Where is HNSW used?</h3><p>It is supported by several vector search systems, including FAISS, Qdrant, and others.</p>" },
      { id: "p8-ivf", title: "IVF index", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> IVF groups vectors into clusters and searches only the closest clusters.</p><p>IVF stands for Inverted File Index. It divides vectors into groups called clusters. A query first identifies the nearest clusters and then searches vectors within those clusters.</p><pre>All Vectors\n     |\n     v\nClustering\n     |\n     +---- Cluster 1\n     |\n     +---- Cluster 2\n     |\n     +---- Cluster 3\n     |\n     +---- Cluster 4</pre><p>At query time:</p><ol><li>Find the nearest cluster centroids.</li><li>Select a subset of clusters.</li><li>Search vectors inside those clusters.</li><li>Return the best candidates.</li></ol><h3>Important parameters</h3><ul><li><b>nlist</b>: number of clusters or inverted lists.</li><li><b>nprobe</b>: number of clusters searched during retrieval.</li></ul><p>Increasing <code>nprobe</code> generally increases recall but also increases search work.</p><h3>HNSW vs IVF</h3><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>HNSW</th><th>IVF</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Structure</td><td>Graph</td><td>Clusters</td></tr><tr><td>Search</td><td>Graph traversal</td><td>Cluster selection</td></tr><tr><td>Main parameters</td><td>M, ef_search</td><td>nlist, nprobe</td></tr><tr><td>Memory</td><td>Can be relatively high</td><td>Depends on configuration</td></tr><tr><td>Index construction</td><td>Graph building</td><td>Clustering and assignment</td></tr><tr><td>Use</td><td>Fast ANN retrieval</td><td>Large-scale partitioned search</td></tr></tbody></table></div>" },
      { id: "p8-metrics", title: "Distance metrics", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Use the metric your embedding model recommends.</p><p>Vector databases support different similarity and distance metrics.</p><ul><li><b>Cosine similarity</b>: measures directional similarity. Higher similarity generally indicates closer semantic representations.</li><li><b>Dot product</b>: measures the inner product of vectors. Its score depends on both direction and magnitude unless vectors are normalized.</li><li><b>Euclidean distance</b>: measures straight-line distance between vectors. Smaller distance indicates closer vectors.</li></ul><h3>Comparison</h3><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Metric</th><th>Desired result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Cosine similarity</td><td>Higher</td></tr><tr><td>Inner product</td><td>Depends on model and normalization</td></tr><tr><td>Euclidean distance</td><td>Lower</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Selection rule</h3><p>Follow the embedding model's recommended similarity metric and normalization requirements. Do not select a metric randomly.</p>" },
      { id: "p8-filtering", title: "Metadata filtering", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Filter by source, tenant, date or version, and enforce access control on the server.</p><p>Metadata filtering narrows vector search to records satisfying specified conditions. For example, a user asks \"Find RAG documents from the engineering department\", and the filter is:</p><pre>{\n  \"department\": \"engineering\"\n}</pre><h3>Other filtering examples</h3><ul><li>Document source.</li><li>File type.</li><li>Creation date.</li><li>Category.</li><li>Tenant ID.</li><li>Access permissions.</li><li>Document version.</li></ul><h3>Filtered retrieval</h3><pre>Query\n  |\n  v\nQuery Embedding\n  |\n  v\nMetadata Filter\n  |\n  v\nVector Search\n  |\n  v\nRelevant Results</pre><p>The order of filtering and vector search depends on the database's query execution and indexing strategy.</p><div class=\"callout\"><b>Important</b>If your system supports multiple tenants, enforce tenant and access-control filters on the server side. Never rely on the LLM to decide whether a user is allowed to retrieve a document.</div>" },
      { id: "p8-crud", title: "Insert, update and delete vectors", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Keep the index in step with your documents by replacing a changed document's chunks.</p><p>A vector database must support document lifecycle operations.</p><h3>Insert</h3><pre>collection.add(\n    ids=[\"chunk_004\"],\n    documents=[\"New RAG document content\"],\n    embeddings=[[0.1, 0.2, 0.3]]\n)</pre><p>The vector dimensions above are illustrative. They must match the collection configuration.</p><h3>Update</h3><pre>collection.update(\n    ids=[\"chunk_004\"],\n    documents=[\"Updated document content\"],\n    embeddings=[[0.2, 0.3, 0.4]]\n)</pre><h3>Delete</h3><pre>collection.delete(\n    ids=[\"chunk_004\"]\n)</pre><h3>Document update workflow</h3><p>If a PDF changes:</p><ol><li>Identify the document.</li><li>Detect the new version.</li><li>Extract and clean the updated content.</li><li>Generate new chunks.</li><li>Generate new embeddings.</li><li>Replace the old chunk records.</li><li>Update document metadata.</li><li>Verify retrieval results.</li></ol><p>Use versioning or an atomic index-switching strategy when consistency matters.</p>" },
      { id: "p8-persistence", title: "Index persistence", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Persist the database to disk, and still plan real backups.</p><p>Persistence means retaining stored vectors and indexes after the application stops. Without persistence, you may lose data when a process ends.</p><pre>import chromadb\n\nclient = chromadb.PersistentClient(\n    path=\"./chroma_db\"\n)</pre><p>This uses a persistent local Chroma database.</p><h3>Why persistence matters</h3><ul><li>Application restarts.</li><li>Long-running projects.</li><li>Large document collections.</li><li>Production deployments.</li><li>Incremental ingestion.</li></ul><h3>Persistence strategies</h3><ul><li>Local persistent storage.</li><li>Managed vector database.</li><li>Database backups.</li><li>Snapshot and restore.</li><li>Replication.</li><li>Index rebuilding from source records.</li></ul><div class=\"callout\"><b>Important</b>Persistent local storage is not the same as a production backup strategy. You still need backups, monitoring, and recovery procedures.</div>" },
      { id: "p8-selection", title: "Vector database selection", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Learn NumPy, then FAISS, Chroma, Qdrant and pgvector, in that order.</p><h3>FAISS</h3><p>A library for efficient similarity search and clustering of dense vectors. Best for local experimentation, custom vector search, retrieval prototypes, research and benchmarking. FAISS is primarily a vector search library, not a full managed database with all the application features of a database server.</p><h3>ChromaDB</h3><p>A developer-friendly database for storing embeddings, documents, and metadata. Best for learning RAG, local applications, prototypes and simple persistent vector storage.</p><h3>Qdrant</h3><p>A vector database with dense and sparse retrieval capabilities and metadata filtering. Best for production vector retrieval, filtering, hybrid search workflows, and self-hosted or managed deployments.</p><h3>Pinecone</h3><p>A managed vector database service. Best for managed infrastructure, hosted vector search, and applications that prefer less database operations work.</p><h3>Weaviate</h3><p>A vector database supporting vector search, filtering, and hybrid search. Best for semantic search applications, hybrid retrieval, and structured metadata with vector search.</p><h3>Milvus</h3><p>A vector database designed for large-scale vector search. Best for large vector collections, distributed vector retrieval and high-volume applications.</p><h3>pgvector</h3><p>A PostgreSQL extension for storing and searching vectors. Best for applications already using PostgreSQL, combining relational data and vector search, and SQL-based filtering and data management.</p><h3>Comparison</h3><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Technology</th><th>Type</th><th>Good starting use</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>FAISS</td><td>Vector search library</td><td>Learning ANN and benchmarking</td></tr><tr><td>ChromaDB</td><td>Vector database</td><td>Beginner RAG applications</td></tr><tr><td>Qdrant</td><td>Vector database</td><td>Production retrieval</td></tr><tr><td>Pinecone</td><td>Managed vector database</td><td>Managed cloud search</td></tr><tr><td>Weaviate</td><td>Vector database</td><td>Hybrid search</td></tr><tr><td>Milvus</td><td>Vector database</td><td>Large-scale retrieval</td></tr><tr><td>pgvector</td><td>PostgreSQL extension</td><td>SQL + vector search</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Suggested learning order</h3><ol><li>NumPy – understand similarity manually.</li><li>FAISS – understand indexes and nearest-neighbor search.</li><li>ChromaDB – learn practical vector storage.</li><li>Qdrant – learn filtering and production retrieval.</li><li>pgvector – understand relational and vector database integration.</li></ol>" },
      { id: "p8-project", title: "Practical project: vector storage engine", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> Store chunks, vectors and metadata in a searchable, persistent database.</p><h3>Objective</h3><p>Take the chunks and embeddings from previous phases and store them in a searchable vector database.</p><h3>Features</h3><ul><li>Load chunk JSONL.</li><li>Load or generate embeddings.</li><li>Create a collection.</li><li>Store vectors.</li><li>Store text.</li><li>Store metadata.</li><li>Search using a query vector.</li><li>Return top-K results.</li><li>Apply metadata filters.</li><li>Update records.</li><li>Delete records.</li><li>Persist the database.</li></ul><h3>Suggested structure</h3><pre>vector-storage/\n│\n├── data/\n│   ├── chunks/\n│   └── embeddings/\n│\n├── src/\n│   └── vector_storage/\n│       ├── __init__.py\n│       ├── base.py\n│       ├── chroma_store.py\n│       ├── faiss_store.py\n│       ├── qdrant_store.py\n│       ├── metadata.py\n│       ├── index_manager.py\n│       └── search.py\n│\n├── tests/\n├── pyproject.toml\n└── README.md</pre><h3>Completion criteria</h3><ul><li>Store at least 1,000 chunks.</li><li>Store their embeddings.</li><li>Retrieve the top 5 similar chunks.</li><li>Filter by document ID.</li><li>Update a chunk.</li><li>Delete a document's chunks.</li><li>Restart the application without losing persistent data.</li><li>Compare exact search and approximate search.</li><li>Understand HNSW and IVF.</li></ul>" },
      { id: "p8-final", title: "Final understanding", html: "<p><b>In one line:</b> The vector database is the storage and search layer of RAG.</p><p>A vector database is the storage and search layer of your RAG system. It stores vectors and related information, indexes vectors, and retrieves the nearest neighbors for incoming queries. The next phase is about preparing those incoming queries correctly.</p>" },
      { id: "ph-11", title: "Phase 11 – Context construction & preparation", html: "<p><b>Goal:</b> Prepare retrieved information before sending it to the LLM.</p><h3>Concepts to cover</h3><ol><li>Retrieved Chunk Processing</li><li>Remove Duplicate Chunks</li><li>Context Relevance Filtering</li><li>Context Ranking</li><li>Context Selection</li><li>Context Ordering</li><li>Context Compression</li><li>Context Window Management</li><li>Token Budget Management</li><li>Context Assembly</li><li>Source Attribution</li><li>Citation and Reference Mapping</li></ol><div class=\"callout\"><b>Status</b>Notes coming soon.</div>" },
      { id: "ph-12", title: "Phase 12 – LLM generation & response processing", html: "<p><b>Goal:</b> Generate accurate answers using retrieved context.</p><h3>Concepts to cover</h3><ol><li>LLM Integration</li><li>Model Selection</li><li>Prompt Template Design</li><li>System Prompt Construction</li><li>Context Injection</li><li>Question and Context Formatting</li><li>Grounded Answer Generation</li><li>Hallucination Reduction</li><li>Handling Missing Context</li><li>Handling Out-of-Context Questions</li><li>Structured Output Generation</li><li>Streaming Responses</li><li>Response Parsing</li><li>Source Citation Generation</li></ol><div class=\"callout\"><b>Status</b>Notes coming soon.</div>" },
      { id: "ph-13", title: "Phase 13 – RAG evaluation & testing", html: "<p><b>Goal:</b> Measure retrieval quality and answer quality.</p><h3>Concepts to cover</h3><ol><li>RAG Evaluation Fundamentals</li><li>Create Evaluation Datasets</li><li>Ground Truth Preparation</li><li>Retrieval Evaluation</li><li>Generation Evaluation</li><li>Context Precision</li><li>Context Recall</li><li>Faithfulness</li><li>Answer Relevancy</li><li>Answer Correctness</li><li>Recall@K</li><li>Precision@K</li><li>Mean Reciprocal Rank (MRR)</li><li>NDCG</li><li>Hallucination Testing</li><li>RAGAS</li><li>DeepEval</li><li>Regression Testing</li><li>End-to-End RAG Testing</li></ol><div class=\"callout\"><b>Status</b>Notes coming soon.</div>" },
      { id: "ph-14", title: "Phase 14 – RAG optimization", html: "<p><b>Goal:</b> Improve accuracy, speed and cost efficiency.</p><h3>Concepts to cover</h3><ol><li>Chunk Size Optimization</li><li>Chunk Overlap Optimization</li><li>Embedding Model Optimization</li><li>Retrieval Parameter Optimization</li><li>Top-K Optimization</li><li>Similarity Threshold Tuning</li><li>Reranking Optimization</li><li>Context Compression</li><li>Prompt Optimization</li><li>LLM Model Optimization</li><li>Token Usage Optimization</li><li>Semantic Caching</li><li>Query Caching</li><li>Batch Embedding Generation</li><li>Parallel Processing</li><li>Latency Optimization</li><li>Cost Optimization</li></ol><div class=\"callout\"><b>Status</b>Notes coming soon.</div>" },
      { id: "ph-15", title: "Phase 15 – RAG backend & API development", html: "<p><b>Goal:</b> Convert your RAG pipeline into a usable backend service.</p><h3>Concepts to cover</h3><ol><li>RAG Application Architecture</li><li>FastAPI Integration</li><li>Document Upload API</li><li>Document Processing API</li><li>Query API</li><li>Streaming API Responses</li><li>Background Document Processing</li><li>Async Processing</li><li>Job Queues</li><li>Database Integration</li><li>Redis Integration</li><li>API Validation</li><li>Error Handling</li><li>Rate Limiting</li><li>API Versioning</li></ol><div class=\"callout\"><b>Status</b>Notes coming soon.</div>" },
      { id: "ph-16", title: "Phase 16 – Production data pipeline & management", html: "<p><b>Goal:</b> Manage documents and ingestion reliably at scale.</p><h3>Concepts to cover</h3><ol><li>Automated Document Ingestion</li><li>Incremental Document Processing</li><li>Document Change Detection</li><li>Document Versioning</li><li>Document Update and Re-indexing</li><li>Document Deletion</li><li>Duplicate Document Detection</li><li>Failed Ingestion Recovery</li><li>Retry Mechanisms</li><li>Batch Processing</li><li>Distributed Processing</li><li>Scheduled Data Synchronization</li><li>Data Validation</li><li>Data Retention Policies</li></ol><div class=\"callout\"><b>Status</b>Notes coming soon.</div>" },
      { id: "ph-17", title: "Phase 17 – Security & multi-tenant RAG", html: "<p><b>Goal:</b> Build secure enterprise-level RAG systems.</p><h3>Concepts to cover</h3><ol><li>Authentication</li><li>Authorization</li><li>Role-Based Access Control (RBAC)</li><li>Document-Level Permissions</li><li>Metadata-Based Access Filtering</li><li>Tenant Isolation</li><li>Tenant-Specific Vector Collections</li><li>Data Encryption</li><li>Secure Document Upload</li><li>PII Detection and Masking</li><li>Prompt Injection Protection</li><li>Retrieval Data Leakage Prevention</li><li>Audit Logs</li><li>Data Deletion Compliance</li></ol><div class=\"callout\"><b>Status</b>Notes coming soon.</div>" },
      { id: "ph-18", title: "Phase 18 – Monitoring & observability", html: "<p><b>Goal:</b> Monitor your RAG application after deployment.</p><h3>Concepts to cover</h3><ol><li>Application Logging</li><li>Structured Logging</li><li>Retrieval Monitoring</li><li>LLM Response Monitoring</li><li>Token Usage Tracking</li><li>Cost Monitoring</li><li>Latency Monitoring</li><li>Retrieval Failure Monitoring</li><li>Hallucination Monitoring</li><li>User Feedback Collection</li><li>Distributed Tracing</li><li>RAG Pipeline Observability</li><li>Alerts and Notifications</li><li>Production Performance Dashboards</li></ol><div class=\"callout\"><b>Status</b>Notes coming soon.</div>" },
      { id: "ph-19", title: "Phase 19 – Deployment & infrastructure", html: "<p><b>Goal:</b> Deploy and operate RAG systems in production.</p><h3>Concepts to cover</h3><ol><li>Environment Configuration</li><li>Docker Containerization</li><li>Docker Compose</li><li>CI/CD Pipelines</li><li>AWS S3 / Azure Blob Storage</li><li>Vector Database Deployment</li><li>PostgreSQL Deployment</li><li>Redis Deployment</li><li>Cloud Deployment</li><li>Kubernetes</li><li>Horizontal Scaling</li><li>Load Balancing</li><li>Autoscaling</li><li>Secrets Management</li><li>Backup and Recovery</li><li>Disaster Recovery</li><li>Production Release Management</li></ol><div class=\"callout\"><b>Status</b>Notes coming soon.</div>" },
      { id: "ph-20", title: "Phase 20 – Advanced production RAG architecture", html: "<p><b>Goal:</b> Understand enterprise and large-scale RAG architectures.</p><h3>Concepts to cover</h3><ol><li>Modular RAG Architecture</li><li>Agentic RAG</li><li>Graph RAG</li><li>Multi-Index RAG</li><li>Multi-Modal RAG</li><li>Multi-Tenant RAG</li><li>Hierarchical RAG</li><li>Adaptive RAG</li><li>Corrective RAG</li><li>Self-RAG</li><li>Query Routing</li><li>Multi-Vector Retrieval</li><li>Knowledge Graph Integration</li><li>Distributed RAG Architecture</li><li>RAG with Multiple LLM Providers</li><li>Model Fallback and Provider Routing</li></ol><div class=\"callout\"><b>Status</b>Notes coming soon.</div>" },
      { id: "ph-21", title: "Phase 21 – RAG evaluation harness & testing frameworks", html: "<p><b>Goal:</b> Build a repeatable environment to test and compare RAG systems.</p><h3>Concepts to cover</h3><ol><li>What is an Evaluation Harness?</li><li>RAG Evaluation Architecture</li><li>Offline vs Online Evaluation</li><li>Golden Dataset Creation</li><li>Question-Answer Dataset Preparation</li><li>Ground Truth Generation</li><li>Expected Context Preparation</li><li>Synthetic Test Dataset Generation</li><li>Test Case Management</li><li>Automated RAG Testing</li><li>End-to-End Pipeline Testing</li><li>Batch Evaluation</li><li>Experiment Tracking</li><li>Evaluation Reports</li><li>Model-to-Model Comparison</li><li>Embedding Model Benchmarking</li><li>Chunking Strategy Benchmarking</li><li>Retrieval Strategy Benchmarking</li><li>Prompt Version Comparison</li><li>Regression Testing</li></ol><h3>Tools and frameworks to explore</h3><ol><li>Ragas</li><li>DeepEval</li><li>TruLens</li><li>Phoenix</li><li>LangSmith</li><li>MLflow</li></ol><div class=\"callout\"><b>Status</b>Notes coming soon.</div>" },
      { id: "ph-22", title: "Phase 22 – RAG metrics & performance measurement", html: "<p><b>Goal:</b> Work out whether your RAG system is actually performing well.</p><h3>A. Retrieval metrics</h3><ol><li>Precision@K</li><li>Recall@K</li><li>Hit Rate@K</li><li>MRR</li><li>MAP</li><li>NDCG@K</li><li>Context Precision</li><li>Context Recall</li></ol><h3>B. Generation metrics</h3><ol><li>Faithfulness</li><li>Answer Relevancy</li><li>Answer Correctness</li><li>Groundedness</li><li>Hallucination Rate</li><li>Completeness</li><li>Citation Precision</li><li>Citation Recall</li></ol><h3>C. System performance metrics</h3><ol><li>End-to-End Latency</li><li>Query Processing Latency</li><li>Embedding Generation Latency</li><li>Vector Search Latency</li><li>Reranking Latency</li><li>LLM Generation Latency</li><li>Time to First Token (TTFT)</li><li>Tokens Per Second</li><li>Input Token Consumption</li><li>Output Token Consumption</li><li>Cost Per Query</li><li>Throughput (Queries Per Second)</li><li>Concurrent Request Capacity</li><li>Error Rate</li><li>Retrieval Failure Rate</li><li>Cache Hit Rate</li></ol><h3>D. Ingestion metrics</h3><ol><li>Documents Processed</li><li>Documents Failed</li><li>Average Document Processing Time</li><li>Average Chunks Per Document</li><li>Average Chunk Token Count</li><li>Embedding Generation Time</li><li>Embedding Failure Rate</li><li>Indexing Time</li><li>Duplicate Document Rate</li><li>Re-indexing Frequency</li></ol><div class=\"callout\"><b>Status</b>Notes coming soon.</div>" },
      { id: "ph-23", title: "Phase 23 – RAG benchmarking & benchmark datasets", html: "<p><b>Goal:</b> Compare your RAG architecture against standard datasets and other retrieval systems.</p><h3>Concepts to cover</h3><ol><li>What is RAG Benchmarking?</li><li>Benchmark Dataset Selection</li><li>Open-Domain Question Answering</li><li>Multi-Hop Question Answering</li><li>Domain-Specific Benchmarking</li><li>Long-Context Retrieval Benchmarking</li><li>Multilingual Retrieval Benchmarking</li><li>Noisy Document Benchmarking</li><li>Temporal Question Answering</li><li>Synthetic vs Real-World Evaluation Datasets</li><li>Benchmark Reproducibility</li><li>Statistical Significance Testing</li><li>Confidence Intervals</li><li>Baseline Comparison</li><li>Benchmark Report Generation</li></ol><h3>Datasets and benchmarks to explore</h3><ol><li>BEIR</li><li>MTEB</li><li>MS MARCO</li><li>HotpotQA</li><li>MuSiQue</li><li>RAGBench</li><li>FinanceBench</li></ol><div class=\"callout\"><b>Status</b>Notes coming soon.</div>" },
      { id: "ph-24", title: "Phase 24 – RAG observability & tracing", html: "<p><b>Goal:</b> Understand what happens inside every RAG request.</p><h3>Concepts to cover</h3><ol><li>What is LLM Observability?</li><li>RAG Pipeline Tracing</li><li>Trace and Span Concepts</li><li>Document Ingestion Tracing</li><li>Embedding Generation Tracing</li><li>Retrieval Tracing</li><li>Reranking Tracing</li><li>Prompt Tracing</li><li>LLM Call Tracing</li><li>Context Inspection</li><li>Retrieved Chunk Inspection</li><li>Token Consumption Tracking</li><li>Cost Attribution</li><li>Latency Breakdown</li><li>Error Tracking</li><li>Distributed Tracing</li><li>OpenTelemetry Integration</li><li>User Feedback Tracking</li><li>Production Trace Analysis</li></ol><h3>Tools to explore</h3><ol><li>Arize Phoenix</li><li>LangSmith</li><li>Langfuse</li><li>OpenTelemetry</li><li>MLflow Tracing</li></ol><div class=\"callout\"><b>Status</b>Notes coming soon.</div>" },
      { id: "ph-25", title: "Phase 25 – Advanced RAG techniques & emerging architectures", html: "<p><b>Goal:</b> Understand modern approaches beyond traditional vector-based RAG.</p><h3>Concepts to cover</h3><ol><li>Naive RAG</li><li>Advanced RAG</li><li>Modular RAG</li><li>Hybrid RAG</li><li>GraphRAG</li><li>Agentic RAG</li><li>Adaptive RAG</li><li>Corrective RAG (CRAG)</li><li>Self-RAG</li><li>LightRAG</li><li>RAPTOR (Recursive Abstractive Processing for Tree-Organized Retrieval)</li><li>Multi-Vector RAG</li><li>Hierarchical RAG</li><li>Multi-Hop RAG</li><li>Multi-Modal RAG</li><li>Table-Based RAG</li><li>Knowledge Graph-Based RAG</li><li>Long-Context RAG</li><li>Memory-Augmented RAG</li><li>Temporal RAG</li><li>Structure-Aware RAG</li><li>Reasoning-Driven Retrieval</li><li>Retrieval-Augmented Agents</li><li>Search-R1 and Retrieval with Reinforcement Learning</li></ol><div class=\"callout\"><b>Status</b>Notes coming soon.</div>" },
      { id: "ph-26", title: "Phase 26 – RAG reliability, security & governance", html: "<p><b>Goal:</b> Make RAG trustworthy and safe for enterprise usage.</p><h3>Concepts to cover</h3><ol><li>Retrieval Robustness</li><li>Adversarial Query Testing</li><li>Prompt Injection Attacks</li><li>Document Poisoning</li><li>Data Leakage Testing</li><li>Unauthorized Document Retrieval</li><li>Access Control Evaluation</li><li>Citation Verification</li><li>Source Provenance</li><li>Answer Abstention</li><li>Uncertainty Estimation</li><li>Out-of-Distribution Queries</li><li>Stale Information Detection</li><li>Data Freshness Evaluation</li><li>PII Leakage Prevention</li><li>Compliance and Auditability</li><li>Red Team Testing</li><li>RAG Security Evaluation</li></ol><div class=\"callout\"><b>Status</b>Notes coming soon.</div>" },
      { id: "ph-27", title: "Phase 27 – RAG experimentation & research", html: "<p><b>Goal:</b> Learn how to experiment with new architectures and make evidence-based engineering decisions.</p><h3>Concepts to cover</h3><ol><li>RAG Architecture Ablation Studies</li><li>Chunking Strategy Experiments</li><li>Embedding Model Experiments</li><li>Retriever Comparison</li><li>Reranker Comparison</li><li>Prompt Ablation Testing</li><li>LLM-as-a-Judge Reliability</li><li>Human vs AI Evaluation</li><li>Evaluation Dataset Bias</li><li>Retrieval Failure Analysis</li><li>Error Categorization</li><li>Cost vs Accuracy Trade-offs</li><li>Latency vs Quality Trade-offs</li><li>Reproducible Experiments</li><li>Research Paper Implementation</li></ol><div class=\"callout\"><b>Status</b>Notes coming soon.</div>" },
      { id: "ph-28", title: "Phase 28 – RAG orchestration & workflow engineering", html: "<p><b>Goal:</b> Coordinate all RAG components into reliable workflows.</p><h3>Concepts to cover</h3><ol><li>RAG Pipeline Orchestration</li><li>Workflow-Based RAG</li><li>DAG-Based Execution</li><li>State Management</li><li>Workflow Checkpointing</li><li>Conditional Routing</li><li>Parallel Retrieval</li><li>Sequential Retrieval</li><li>Retry and Recovery</li><li>Workflow Timeouts</li><li>Human-in-the-Loop</li><li>Agent and Tool Integration</li><li>Workflow Versioning</li><li>Long-Running Ingestion Workflows</li></ol><h3>Tools</h3><ul><li>LangGraph</li><li>Temporal</li><li>Prefect</li><li>Celery</li></ul><div class=\"callout\"><b>Status</b>Notes coming soon.</div>" },
      { id: "ph-29", title: "Phase 29 – Advanced search & retrieval engineering", html: "<p><b>Goal:</b> Improve search quality for complex enterprise queries.</p><h3>Concepts to cover</h3><ol><li>Query Intent Classification</li><li>Query Complexity Detection</li><li>Search Strategy Selection</li><li>Field-Aware Retrieval</li><li>Metadata-Aware Retrieval</li><li>Multi-Index Search</li><li>Federated Search</li><li>Structured Data Retrieval</li><li>SQL-Based Retrieval</li><li>Document and Table Retrieval</li><li>Image and Visual Retrieval</li><li>Cross-Lingual Retrieval</li><li>Temporal and Freshness-Aware Retrieval</li><li>Search Result Fusion</li><li>Search Result Diversification</li><li>Retrieval Confidence Estimation</li></ol><div class=\"callout\"><b>Status</b>Notes coming soon.</div>" },
      { id: "ph-30", title: "Phase 30 – RAG infrastructure & distributed systems", html: "<p><b>Goal:</b> Handle large-scale document ingestion and concurrent retrieval.</p><h3>Concepts to cover</h3><ol><li>Distributed Document Processing</li><li>Message Queues</li><li>Event-Driven Ingestion</li><li>Kafka Fundamentals</li><li>Worker Architecture</li><li>Horizontal Scaling</li><li>Sharding and Partitioning</li><li>Vector Database Replication</li><li>Index Optimization</li><li>Connection Pooling</li><li>Caching Architecture</li><li>Database Failover</li><li>High Availability</li><li>Load Testing</li><li>Fault Tolerance</li><li>Disaster Recovery</li></ol><div class=\"callout\"><b>Status</b>Notes coming soon.</div>" },
      { id: "ph-31", title: "Phase 31 – Continuous evaluation & improvement", html: "<p><b>Goal:</b> Improve RAG quality using real-world production feedback.</p><h3>Concepts to cover</h3><ol><li>Online RAG Evaluation</li><li>User Feedback Collection</li><li>Human Evaluation Pipelines</li><li>Production Query Sampling</li><li>Failure Case Collection</li><li>Retrieval Quality Drift</li><li>Embedding Model Drift</li><li>Data Distribution Drift</li><li>Evaluation Dataset Refresh</li><li>Automated Regression Testing</li><li>Continuous Benchmarking</li><li>A/B Testing</li><li>Champion vs Challenger Models</li><li>Automated Evaluation Reports</li><li>Evaluation-Based Model Selection</li></ol><div class=\"callout\"><b>Status</b>Notes coming soon.</div>" },
      { id: "ph-32", title: "Phase 32 – LLM & embedding model infrastructure", html: "<p><b>Goal:</b> Manage models efficiently across RAG environments.</p><h3>Concepts to cover</h3><ol><li>Embedding Model Serving</li><li>LLM Model Serving</li><li>Local vs Cloud Inference</li><li>GPU and CPU Inference</li><li>Model Quantization</li><li>Embedding Batch Processing</li><li>Model Version Management</li><li>Model Registry</li><li>Model Fallback</li><li>Multi-Provider Routing</li><li>Rate Limit Management</li><li>Request Batching</li><li>Inference Optimization</li><li>Model Warm-Up</li><li>Model Deployment Strategies</li></ol><div class=\"callout\"><b>Status</b>Notes coming soon.</div>" },
      { id: "ph-33", title: "Phase 33 – Enterprise RAG governance & cost management", html: "<p><b>Goal:</b> Manage RAG as an enterprise platform.</p><h3>Concepts to cover</h3><ol><li>Multi-Tenant Resource Management</li><li>Tenant-Level Usage Tracking</li><li>Query-Level Cost Attribution</li><li>Embedding Cost Management</li><li>LLM Cost Management</li><li>Budget Limits</li><li>Usage Quotas</li><li>Rate Limits per Tenant</li><li>Data Governance</li><li>Data Lineage</li><li>Document Ownership</li><li>Retention and Archival</li><li>Audit Trails</li><li>SLA and SLO Design</li><li>Chargeback and Showback</li></ol><div class=\"callout\"><b>Status</b>Notes coming soon.</div>" },
      { id: "ph-34", title: "Phase 34 – RAG architecture patterns & design decisions", html: "<p><b>Goal:</b> Independently design RAG systems based on business requirements.</p><h3>Concepts to cover</h3><ol><li>Monolithic vs Microservices RAG</li><li>Modular RAG</li><li>Single-Tenant vs Multi-Tenant RAG</li><li>Centralized vs Distributed Retrieval</li><li>Synchronous vs Asynchronous Ingestion</li><li>Batch vs Real-Time Ingestion</li><li>Managed vs Self-Hosted Vector Databases</li><li>Local vs Remote Embeddings</li><li>Single-Model vs Multi-Model Architecture</li><li>Cost vs Accuracy Trade-Offs</li><li>Latency vs Quality Trade-Offs</li><li>Scalability Trade-Offs</li><li>Architecture Decision Records (ADR)</li><li>Capacity Planning</li><li>Production Architecture Reviews</li></ol><div class=\"callout\"><b>Status</b>Notes coming soon.</div>" },
      { id: "ph-latest", title: "Latest concepts worth prioritizing", html: "<p><b>Goal:</b> Areas to investigate beyond traditional RAG.</p><h3>Concepts to cover</h3><ol><li>Agentic RAG: agents decide when to search, what to retrieve, whether to search again and when to answer</li><li>GraphRAG: entities and relationships for questions that need connections across documents</li><li>Multimodal RAG: text, images, tables, charts, audio and other modalities</li><li>Adaptive RAG: retrieval strategy chosen by the question and its complexity</li><li>Continuous RAG Evaluation: evaluate production queries, detect quality degradation and find retrieval failures</li></ol><div class=\"callout\"><b>Status</b>Notes coming soon.</div>" },
      { id: "ph-roadmap", title: "Final RAG master roadmap: 34 phases", html: "<p>Complete learning structure from fundamentals to enterprise architecture.</p><ul><li><b>Phases 01–04</b>: Data collection, loading, cleaning and storage</li><li><b>Phases 05–08</b>: Chunking, tokenization, embeddings and vector databases</li><li><b>Phases 09–10</b>: Query processing and retrieval</li><li><b>Phases 11–12</b>: Context construction and LLM generation</li><li><b>Phases 13–14</b>: Evaluation and optimization</li><li><b>Phases 15–20</b>: Backend, data management, security, monitoring and deployment</li><li><b>Phases 21–24</b>: Evaluation harnesses, metrics, benchmarking and observability</li><li><b>Phases 25–27</b>: Advanced RAG, security and research</li><li><b>Phases 28–30</b>: Orchestration, search and distributed systems</li><li><b>Phases 31–33</b>: Continuous improvement, model infrastructure and governance</li><li><b>Phases 34</b>: Architecture patterns and design decisions</li></ul>" },
      { id: "ph-skills", title: "Final skills after completing the roadmap", html: "<h3>You should be able to</h3><ul><li>Build RAG systems from scratch without frameworks.</li><li>Design document ingestion and indexing pipelines.</li><li>Select and benchmark embedding models.</li><li>Implement advanced retrieval and reranking.</li><li>Build custom RAG evaluation harnesses.</li><li>Measure retrieval accuracy and generation quality.</li><li>Debug RAG failures using traces and metrics.</li><li>Deploy scalable, secure, multi-tenant RAG applications.</li><li>Optimize latency, token usage and infrastructure costs.</li><li>Design enterprise-level RAG architectures.</li></ul>" },
      { id: "ph-capstone", title: "Final capstone projects", html: "<h3>Five projects to finish with</h3><ol><li><b>Universal Document Intelligence</b>: PDF, DOCX, CSV, Excel, HTML and database ingestion.</li><li><b>Advanced Retrieval Engine</b>: hybrid search, BM25, vector search, reranking and query rewriting.</li><li><b>RAG Evaluation Harness</b>: benchmark multiple embedding models, chunking strategies and retrieval methods.</li><li><b>Enterprise Multi-Tenant RAG</b>: authentication, tenant isolation, permissions, observability and cost tracking.</li><li><b>Agentic Knowledge Intelligence</b>: multi-step retrieval, tool calling, knowledge graphs and adaptive retrieval.</li></ol><div class=\"callout\"><b>Advice</b>Treat these 34 phases as your complete RAG engineering syllabus. You do not need every framework or research architecture in depth. Understand the fundamentals, implement the important techniques, benchmark them, and justify your architecture decisions.</div>" }
      ] },
  ] },
  { label: 'Concepts', all: 'concepts.html', items: [
    { title: "Data source", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Any location or system RAG collects information from.", desc: "Any location or system RAG collects information from.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=data-source',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>A data source is any location or system from which we collect information. In a RAG application, data sources provide the knowledge the AI model uses to answer user questions.</div><h3>Examples</h3><ul><li>PDF files with company policies</li><li>Excel files with employee information</li><li>Markdown files with technical documentation</li><li>A database with customer information</li><li>APIs with product information</li><li>A website with knowledge articles</li></ul><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#data-sources\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Data ingestion", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Bringing data into a RAG system.", desc: "Bringing data into a RAG system.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=data-ingestion',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Data ingestion is bringing data into a RAG system. It covers finding the source, reading it, extracting the content and passing it to the next stage.</div><h3>Typical document ingestion steps</h3><ol><li>Identify and locate the source</li><li>Read the file</li><li>Extract text and metadata</li><li>Clean the content</li><li>Convert to a common document structure</li><li>Pass to chunking</li></ol><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#documents\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Data extraction", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Pulling useful information out of a source.", desc: "Pulling useful information out of a source.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=data-extraction',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Data extraction is extracting useful information from a source. Each type of source needs its own method.</div><h3>Method by source</h3><ul><li>PDF \u2192 PDF parser</li><li>CSV \u2192 Pandas</li><li>JSON \u2192 Python JSON module</li><li>HTML \u2192 BeautifulSoup</li><li>Database \u2192 SQL queries</li></ul><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#structured\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Data loading", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Passing extracted information to the next stage.", desc: "Passing extracted information to the next stage.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=data-loading',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Data loading is loading extracted information into the next processing stage of the pipeline, such as cleaning and chunking.</div><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#data-sources\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Data pipeline", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "A sequence of operations that processes data.", desc: "A sequence of operations that processes data.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=data-pipeline',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>A data pipeline is a sequence of operations that processes data. In RAG, data flows from the source through extraction, a common format, validation and cleaning, and then on to chunking.</div><pre>Data sources \u2192 Extractors \u2192 Common format \u2192 Validation \u2192 Cleaning \u2192 Documents \u2192 Chunking</pre><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#multi-source\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Data connector", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "A component that connects to a data source.", desc: "A component that connects to a data source.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=data-connector',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>A data connector is a component that connects to a data source, such as a PDF folder, a database or an API.</div><p>Keeping each connector as its own module (modular architecture) makes it reusable and easy to replace.</p><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#multi-source\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Data synchronization", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Keeping collected data updated when the source changes.", desc: "Keeping collected data updated when the source changes.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=data-synchronization',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Data synchronization is keeping collected data updated when the original source changes.</div><h3>Related ideas</h3><ul><li>Incremental ingestion</li><li>Data versioning</li><li>Handling document updates</li><li>Source tracking</li></ul><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#multi-source\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Metadata", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Information that describes a document.", desc: "Information that describes a document.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=metadata',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Metadata is additional information describing a document, such as where it came from and which page it is on.</div><pre>{\n  \"source\": \"employee_policy.pdf\",\n  \"file_type\": \"pdf\",\n  \"page\": 3,\n  \"department\": \"HR\",\n  \"document_type\": \"policy\"\n}</pre><h3>Why it matters</h3><ul><li>Identify the original document</li><li>Filter retrieval results</li><li>Track document versions</li><li>Identify page numbers</li><li>Support source citations</li><li>Manage access permissions</li></ul><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#documents\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Structured data", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Data in rows and columns with a fixed schema.", desc: "Data in rows and columns with a fixed schema.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=structured-data',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Structured data is information organized according to a predefined schema, usually in rows and columns.</div><h3>Characteristics</h3><ul><li>Predefined columns and consistent data types</li><li>Easy to filter and query</li><li>Stored in relational databases or spreadsheets</li></ul><p>Examples: SQL databases, CSV files, Excel spreadsheets.</p><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#structured\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Semi-structured data", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Flexible data organized with keys, tags or attributes.", desc: "Flexible data organized with keys, tags or attributes.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=semi-structured-data',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Semi-structured data does not follow a rigid relational schema, but it contains organizational elements such as keys, tags or attributes.</div><p>Examples: JSON, XML, HTML, JSONL. In RAG this usually needs parsing.</p><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#structured\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Unstructured data", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Free-form data with no fixed schema.", desc: "Free-form data with no fixed schema.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=unstructured-data',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Unstructured data does not follow a fixed tabular schema. It often contains natural language and may include images, tables and layouts.</div><p>Examples: PDFs, Word documents, text files, images, audio, video, emails. In RAG this needs text extraction and processing.</p><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#structured\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "OCR (Optical Character Recognition)", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Recognizing text inside images and scanned pages.", desc: "Recognizing text inside images and scanned pages.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=ocr',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>OCR (Optical Character Recognition) recognizes text in images. It is required for scanned PDFs, where the content is visible but not machine-readable.</div><h3>Tools</h3><ul><li>pytesseract for OCR</li><li>pdf2image to turn PDF pages into images</li></ul><p>Tesseract OCR and Poppler may need to be installed on your operating system.</p><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#pdf\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Image preprocessing", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Cleaning images before OCR to improve recognition.", desc: "Cleaning images before OCR to improve recognition.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=image-preprocessing',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Image preprocessing prepares images before OCR so the text is recognized more accurately.</div><h3>Common techniques</h3><ul><li>Grayscale conversion</li><li>Noise removal</li><li>Image resizing</li><li>Thresholding</li><li>Deskewing</li><li>Contrast enhancement</li><li>Rotation correction</li></ul><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#pdf\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Text-based, scanned and mixed PDFs", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Three kinds of PDF that need different handling.", desc: "Three kinds of PDF that need different handling.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=pdf-types',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>A PDF may contain real selectable text, scanned images, or both. The type decides how you extract it.</div><ul><li><b>Text-based</b>: extract directly with a PDF parsing library</li><li><b>Scanned</b>: needs OCR</li><li><b>Mixed</b>: PDF extraction plus OCR</li></ul><h3>Common challenges</h3><ul><li>Incorrect reading order</li><li>Multi-column layouts</li><li>Tables losing structure</li><li>Repeated headers and footers</li><li>Images with important information</li><li>Special characters and equations</li></ul><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#pdf\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Converting rows into text", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Turning spreadsheet or JSON records into readable sentences.", desc: "Turning spreadsheet or JSON records into readable sentences.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=rows-to-text',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>To retrieve information from tabular or JSON data, each row or record is converted into a readable text representation.</div><pre>Product: Laptop. Price: 50000. Category: Electronics.</pre><div class=\"callout\"><b>Important</b>Not every structured dataset should be embedded. Exact-value questions, filtering, calculations and frequently changing records may be better handled with SQL or structured queries.</div><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#csv-excel\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "JSON vs JSONL", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "One JSON value versus one JSON object per line.", desc: "One JSON value versus one JSON object per line.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=json-vs-jsonl',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>JSON usually holds one JSON value that you load completely. JSONL (JSON Lines) holds one independent JSON object per line, so it can be read line by line.</div><ul><li><b>JSON</b>: common for APIs and configuration</li><li><b>JSONL</b>: common for datasets and logs, easier to stream large data</li></ul><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#json\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Web crawling", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Automatically discovering and visiting many web pages.", desc: "Automatically discovering and visiting many web pages.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=web-crawling',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Web crawling is the process of automatically discovering and visiting multiple web pages by following links.</div><h3>Basic crawling logic</h3><ol><li>Start with a seed URL</li><li>Fetch the page and extract its content</li><li>Find and filter links</li><li>Add new URLs to a queue</li><li>Visit discovered pages</li><li>Stop at the crawl limit</li></ol><h3>Key ideas</h3><ul><li>Seed URL</li><li>URL queue and visited URLs</li><li>Domain restriction</li><li>Depth limit</li><li>Rate limiting</li><li>Duplicate detection</li></ul><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#crawling\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Web scraping", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Extracting specific information from a page.", desc: "Extracting specific information from a page.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=web-scraping',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Web scraping extracts specific information such as headings, paragraphs and tables, and processes page content. Crawling finds the pages; scraping pulls the content out of them. They are often used together.</div><h3>Tools</h3><ul><li>Requests + BeautifulSoup</li><li>Trafilatura</li><li>Scrapy</li><li>Crawl4AI</li><li>Playwright and Selenium for dynamic pages</li></ul><p>Respect robots.txt, website terms and access restrictions.</p><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#html\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "API data collection", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Getting data from services through HTTP requests.", desc: "Getting data from services through HTTP requests.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=api-data-collection',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>An API (Application Programming Interface) lets two applications exchange information. For collection, GET requests are most common, and the response is usually JSON.</div><h3>What to handle</h3><ul><li>Authentication: API keys, bearer tokens, OAuth 2.0</li><li>Pagination</li><li>Rate limiting</li><li>Errors: 400, 401, 403, 404, 429, 500</li></ul><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#api\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "SQL vs semantic retrieval", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Choosing a query for exact answers and retrieval for meaning.", desc: "Choosing a query for exact answers and retrieval for meaning.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=sql-vs-semantic',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Not every database record needs to be embedded. Exact questions are better answered with a query, while questions about meaning in text benefit from retrieval.</div><ul><li>\"How many orders were placed yesterday?\" \u2192 a database query</li><li>\"What are the most common complaints about our products?\" \u2192 retrieve and analyze review text</li></ul><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#database\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Common document structure", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "One standard format every source is converted into.", desc: "One standard format every source is converted into.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=common-document-structure',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>A common document structure is a single format that every source is converted into, so chunking, embedding and retrieval work the same way for all data.</div><pre>{\n  \"id\": \"doc_001\",\n  \"content\": \"Introduction to Retrieval-Augmented Generation\",\n  \"metadata\": {\n    \"source\": \"rag_intro.pdf\",\n    \"source_type\": \"pdf\",\n    \"page\": 1\n  }\n}</pre><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#multi-source\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Incremental ingestion", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Processing only new or changed data.", desc: "Processing only new or changed data.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=incremental-ingestion',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Incremental ingestion processes only data that is new or has changed, instead of re-processing everything. It goes together with duplicate detection, data versioning and source tracking.</div><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#multi-source\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Duplicate detection", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Avoiding processing the same content twice.", desc: "Avoiding processing the same content twice.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=duplicate-detection',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Duplicate detection finds repeated documents or pages so the same content is not ingested more than once.</div><p>It applies to files, crawled pages and spreadsheet rows.</p><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#documents\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Common ingestion problems", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "What to check when processing documents.", desc: "What to check when processing documents.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=ingestion-issues',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Real documents are messy. When ingesting, plan for these cases:</div><ul><li>Duplicate documents</li><li>Empty documents</li><li>Corrupted files</li><li>Unsupported formats</li><li>Password-protected files</li><li>Document updates</li><li>File size limitations</li><li>Encoding issues</li></ul><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#documents\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Text cleaning", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Removing unwanted content from text while keeping its meaning.", desc: "Removing unwanted content from text while keeping its meaning.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=text-cleaning',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Text cleaning is the process of removing unwanted or irrelevant content from extracted text while preserving its actual meaning.</div><pre>\"   RAG is a technique !!!   used to improve AI responses.     \"\n→ \"RAG is a technique used to improve AI responses.\"</pre><div class=\"callout\"><b>Important</b>Cleaning must not destroy useful information. \"10% to 25%\" must not become \"10 to 25\".</div><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p3-cleaning\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Control characters and decorative separators", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Characters that are usually noise from extraction.", desc: "Characters that are usually noise from extraction.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=control-characters',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Null characters, other control characters, repeated separators and stray tabs often appear during extraction and can be removed.</div><ul><li>\\x00 null character</li><li>\\t unwanted tab</li><li>Repeated hyphens, underscores or equals as separators</li></ul><p>Only remove separators when you know they are decorative. Hyphens and underscores can be meaningful in identifiers and technical content.</p><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p3-characters\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Headers and footers", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Repeated elements at the top and bottom of pages.", desc: "Repeated elements at the top and bottom of pages.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=headers-footers',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Headers and footers are repeated elements at the top and bottom of document pages, such as \"COMPANY CONFIDENTIAL\" or \"Page 01\".</div><h3>Detecting them</h3><ol><li>Extract text page by page</li><li>Find blocks near the top and bottom</li><li>Normalize the text</li><li>Count repeats across pages</li><li>Remove or store separately</li></ol><p>Some, like the title, version or legal notices, may be useful metadata.</p><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p3-headers\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Exact, normalized and near-duplicates", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Three levels of duplicate detection.", desc: "Three levels of duplicate detection.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=exact-vs-near-duplicates',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Duplicate content means the same or substantially identical information appears more than once. Detection ranges from identical text to very similar text.</div><ul><li><b>Exact</b>: identical text</li><li><b>Normalized</b>: identical after harmless differences in whitespace or case</li><li><b>Near-duplicate</b>: very similar, such as \"retrieval and generation\" vs \"information retrieval and text generation\"</li></ul><p>Use cheap exact hashing before expensive similarity checks, and keep source information.</p><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p3-duplicates\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Hashing", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Creating a fixed-length identifier from content.", desc: "Creating a fixed-length identifier from content.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=hashing',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Hashing creates a fixed-length identifier from content. Identical text always produces the same hash, which makes exact duplicates easy to find.</div><pre>hashlib.sha256(text.encode(\"utf-8\")).hexdigest()</pre><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p3-duplicates\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Jaccard similarity", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "A score for how much two sets overlap.", desc: "A score for how much two sets overlap.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=jaccard-similarity',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Jaccard similarity measures the similarity between two sets: the size of their intersection divided by the size of their union.</div><pre>J(A, B) = |A ∩ B| / |A ∪ B|</pre><p>A = {RAG, retrieval, generation}, B = {RAG, retrieval, embeddings}. Intersection 2, union 4, so J = 0.5.</p><p>Other near-duplicate methods: Levenshtein distance, MinHash, SimHash, embedding cosine similarity.</p><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p3-duplicates\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Empty documents", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Documents with no useful extractable content.", desc: "Documents with no useful extractable content.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=empty-documents',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>An empty document contains no useful extractable content. It may be truly blank, or extraction may have failed.</div><div class=\"callout\"><b>Important</b>An empty result does not prove a page is blank. It may be a scanned image that should be routed to OCR.</div><h3>Possible actions</h3><ul><li>Skip</li><li>Send for OCR</li><li>Mark as failed</li><li>Store an empty-content status</li><li>Request a different source file</li></ul><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p3-empty\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Unicode", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "A standard that gives every character a code point.", desc: "A standard that gives every character a code point.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=unicode',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Unicode assigns code points to characters from different writing systems, including English, Telugu, Hindi, Chinese, Arabic, mathematical symbols and emojis. Python strings support Unicode.</div><p>Special characters are often essential, for example ₹50,000, 37°C, 95% and /v1/users. Do not strip them.</p><p>The replacement character � signals a decoding or extraction failure. Removing it does not recover the original character.</p><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p3-special\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Unicode normalization", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Giving visually identical text one internal form.", desc: "Giving visually identical text one internal form.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=unicode-normalization',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Some visually identical characters have different internal Unicode representations. Python's <code>unicodedata</code> module can normalize them.</div><ul><li><b>NFC</b>: canonical composition, a sensible default</li><li><b>NFD</b>: canonical decomposition</li><li><b>NFKC</b>: compatibility composition, can change characters</li><li><b>NFKD</b>: compatibility decomposition</li></ul><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p3-special\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Text normalization", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Converting text to a consistent representation.", desc: "Converting text to a consistent representation.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=text-normalization',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Text normalization converts text into a consistent representation, reducing unnecessary variation while preserving meaning.</div><h3>Techniques</h3><ul><li>Whitespace</li><li>Unicode</li><li>Case</li><li>Line breaks</li><li>Encoding (UTF-8)</li><li>Punctuation</li></ul><p>Preserve casing for general RAG content. <code>OpenAI</code>, <code>HTTP</code> and <code>US</code> vs <code>us</code> all depend on case.</p><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p3-normalize\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Cleaning vs normalization", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Removing noise versus standardizing representation.", desc: "Removing noise versus standardizing representation.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=cleaning-vs-normalization',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Cleaning removes unwanted content such as artifacts, separators and repeated headers. Normalization standardizes how the remaining text is represented, such as whitespace, Unicode and sometimes case.</div><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p3-normalize\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Preserving document formatting", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Keeping headings, lists, tables and code.", desc: "Keeping headings, lists, tables and code.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=preserving-formatting',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Formatting describes how content is organized: headings, paragraphs, lists, tables, code blocks and equations. Preserving it keeps the relationships between parts of the text.</div><p>Markdown is a good way to keep structure. Do not remove all newlines, since they mark paragraphs, headings, table rows, code and list items.</p><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p3-formatting\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Metadata enrichment", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Adding descriptive fields to documents.", desc: "Adding descriptive fields to documents.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=metadata-enrichment',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Metadata enrichment is adding useful descriptive information to an existing document or its metadata so retrieval can filter and cite it.</div><h3>Types</h3><ul><li>Source: name, file type, URL, table</li><li>Content: title, topic, category, language, keywords</li><li>Business: department, product, business unit</li><li>Technical: ID, version, content hash, timestamp, extraction method</li><li>Access: owner, access level, tenant ID, permissions</li></ul><p>Validate generated metadata, since an LLM can infer the wrong department or category.</p><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p3-enrichment\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Document validation", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Checking a document against pipeline rules.", desc: "Checking a document against pipeline rules.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=document-validation',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Document validation checks whether a processed document satisfies the requirements of the pipeline. A document can be extracted successfully and still be unsuitable.</div><h3>Typical rules</h3><ul><li>Document ID exists</li><li>Content is a non-empty string</li><li>Metadata is a valid dictionary</li><li>Source is available</li><li>File type is supported</li><li>Page number is valid</li></ul><p>Cleaning corrects content. Validation checks the result.</p><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p3-validation\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Schema validation (Pydantic)", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Defining a document model that validates data.", desc: "Defining a document model that validates data.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=schema-validation',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Schema validation defines a document model and checks data against it. In Python, Pydantic is commonly used.</div><pre>class Document(BaseModel):\n    document_id: str = Field(min_length=1)\n    content: str = Field(min_length=1)\n    metadata: dict</pre><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p3-validation\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Dimensions of data quality", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Six ways to judge whether data is good enough.", desc: "Six ways to judge whether data is good enough.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=data-quality-dimensions',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Data quality is how suitable, accurate, complete, consistent and reliable data is for its purpose.</div><ul><li><b>Completeness</b>: is the required content there?</li><li><b>Accuracy</b>: does it match the original?</li><li><b>Consistency</b>: same formats throughout?</li><li><b>Uniqueness</b>: no unnecessary duplicates?</li><li><b>Validity</b>: follows the schema and rules?</li><li><b>Timeliness</b>: is it current?</li></ul><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p3-quality\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Data quality metrics", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Simple rates for measuring ingestion quality.", desc: "Simple rates for measuring ingestion quality.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=data-quality-metrics',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Quality metrics turn the dimensions into numbers, such as completeness rate, duplicate rate, validation pass rate, extraction success rate, OCR accuracy and processing failure rate.</div><pre>Extraction success rate = 90 / 100 × 100 = 90%</pre><p>Metrics can overlap, so define the denominator and counting rules for each one.</p><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p3-quality\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Original document storage", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Keeping the uploaded file untouched.", desc: "Keeping the uploaded file untouched.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=original-document-storage',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Original document storage is storing the actual files collected from data sources without modifying their content, even after text is extracted and cleaned.</div><h3>Why keep them</h3><ul><li>Reprocessing</li><li>Verification and auditing</li><li>OCR improvements</li><li>Version comparison</li><li>Re-extraction with a different parser</li></ul><p>Never overwrite originals accidentally, keep checksums and timestamps, and consider encryption at rest.</p><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p4-original\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Original vs extracted document", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "The uploaded file versus the text taken from it.", desc: "The uploaded file versus the text taken from it.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=original-vs-extracted',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>The original document is the actual uploaded file, such as employee_policy.pdf. The extracted document is the text and information extracted from it. Store them separately.</div><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p4-original\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Local file storage", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Saving documents on the machine where the app runs.", desc: "Saving documents on the machine where the app runs.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=local-file-storage',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Local file storage saves documents directly on the computer or server running your application. It is simple and cheap, and a good start for learning and prototypes.</div><h3>Limits</h3><ul><li>Limited by disk capacity</li><li>Hard to share across servers</li><li>Needs backup management</li><li>Server failure can lose data</li></ul><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p4-local\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "JSON vs JSONL for storage", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "One structure per file versus one record per line.", desc: "One structure per file versus one record per line.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=json-vs-jsonl-storage',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>JSON stores one complete structure, which suits single records, configuration and metadata. JSONL stores one JSON record per line, which suits large collections, streaming and incremental or batch processing.</div><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p4-local\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Object storage", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Storing data as objects with a key and metadata.", desc: "Storing data as objects with a key and metadata.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=object-storage',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Object storage stores data as objects, each with data, a key and metadata. It is designed for large amounts of unstructured data such as PDFs, images, backups and extracted datasets.</div><p>Highly scalable, cost-effective and durable, but not suited to complex relational queries.</p><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p4-sql-nosql\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "AWS S3", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Amazon's object storage service.", desc: "Amazon's object storage service.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=aws-s3',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>AWS S3 (Amazon Simple Storage Service) is an object storage service for files and other unstructured data.</div><ul><li><b>Bucket</b>: a container for objects</li><li><b>Object</b>: an individual stored file</li><li><b>Object key</b>: the unique name or path in a bucket, such as tenant_001/documents/employee_policy.pdf</li><li><b>Version ID</b>: identifies a version when bucket versioning is on</li></ul><p>S3 is a flat store. Folders are only key prefixes.</p><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p4-cloud\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Azure Blob Storage", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Microsoft's cloud object storage service.", desc: "Microsoft's cloud object storage service.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=azure-blob',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Azure Blob Storage stores large amounts of unstructured data using a storage account, containers and blobs.</div><p>It maps to S3 as container ↔ bucket and blob ↔ object. Access is managed with Azure RBAC.</p><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p4-cloud\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Cloud storage concepts", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Security and cost features of cloud object stores.", desc: "Security and cost features of cloud object stores.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=cloud-storage-concepts',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Cloud object storage adds durability, scale and managed policies for your documents.</div><ul><li>IAM and access policies</li><li>Encryption at rest and in transit</li><li>Versioning</li><li>Object lifecycle and storage classes</li><li>Replication and retention</li><li>Signed URLs</li><li>Multipart uploads</li><li>Backup and recovery</li></ul><p>Use IAM roles or a secrets manager instead of hardcoded credentials.</p><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p4-cloud\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Extracted text storage", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Saving parsed text separately from the file.", desc: "Saving parsed text separately from the file.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=extracted-text-storage',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Extracted text storage keeps the content retrieved from original documents after loading, parsing and preprocessing, so you avoid repeated parsing and can resume pipelines.</div><p>Extracted text is the full content of a document or page. Chunked text is smaller pieces made from it. Store them as separate artifacts.</p><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p4-extracted\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Document metadata storage", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Keeping document facts in a queryable database.", desc: "Keeping document facts in a queryable database.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=metadata-storage',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Document metadata storage keeps information that describes documents, such as ID, filename, type, size, uploader, tenant and status, separate from the content.</div><p>It lets you find all PDFs, one tenant's documents or failed documents without opening files. Use PostgreSQL for production and add indexes based on your query patterns.</p><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p4-metadata\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Document ID, version ID and chunk ID", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Three identifiers with different jobs.", desc: "Three identifiers with different jobs.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=document-version-ids',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>A document ID identifies the logical document, a version ID identifies a particular revision, and a chunk ID identifies a particular chunk.</div><p>The document ID stays stable while its version changes. Never reuse document IDs.</p><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p4-versions\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Document versioning", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Keeping multiple revisions of a document.", desc: "Keeping multiple revisions of a document.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=document-versioning',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Versioning maintains multiple revisions of a document so you can track changes, restore older versions, compare and keep an audit history.</div><h3>Strategy</h3><ol><li>Assign a stable document ID</li><li>Generate a checksum</li><li>Check if it exists and compare</li><li>If unchanged, skip processing</li><li>If changed, create a new version</li><li>Keep the old version and mark the new one active</li></ol><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p4-versions\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Checksum-based change detection", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Using a file hash to tell if content changed.", desc: "Using a file hash to tell if content changed.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=checksum-change-detection',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>A checksum such as SHA-256 is calculated from file content. If the new hash matches the stored one the file is unchanged; if it differs the content has changed.</div><pre>sha256 = hashlib.sha256()\nfor block in iter(lambda: file.read(1024 * 1024), b\"\"):\n    sha256.update(block)</pre><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p4-versions\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Updating documents and old embeddings", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Keeping retrieval current after a change.", desc: "Keeping retrieval current after a change.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=updating-and-embeddings',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>When a document changes, you create a new version, re-extract, regenerate chunks and embeddings, mark the new version active and retire the old one from retrieval.</div><p>Tag every chunk and embedding with document ID, version ID, chunk ID and tenant ID so old records can be deactivated cleanly.</p><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p4-update-delete\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Soft delete vs hard delete", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Marking as deleted versus removing the data.", desc: "Marking as deleted versus removing the data.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=soft-vs-hard-delete',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Soft delete marks a document as deleted but keeps its data for recovery, audit or retention. Hard delete physically removes the document and its data from every storage system.</div><p>Hard delete means coordinated cleanup of extracted text, chunks, embeddings, caches and files, not just one database row. Check authorization first and make deletion retryable.</p><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p4-update-delete\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Document lifecycle management", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Managing a document from upload to deletion.", desc: "Managing a document from upload to deletion.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=document-lifecycle',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Document lifecycle management is managing a document from the moment it enters the system until it is updated, archived or deleted.</div><p>Stages: uploaded, stored, processing, extracted, cleaned, validated, active, updated, archived, deleted. Keep processing status and lifecycle status distinct.</p><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p4-lifecycle\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Retention policies", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Rules for how long data is kept.", desc: "Rules for how long data is kept.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=retention-policies',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>A retention policy defines how long documents and related data are stored, for example 30 days for temporary files or 7 years for archived reports.</div><p>Actual periods must follow business, contractual and legal requirements. Cloud lifecycle policies can move or delete objects automatically.</p><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p4-lifecycle\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Storage architecture", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "How different data is stored and connected.", desc: "How different data is stored and connected.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=storage-architecture',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Storage architecture defines how data is stored, organized, connected and accessed. In RAG, use different stores for different jobs.</div><ul><li>Originals: object storage</li><li>Metadata and versions: relational database</li><li>Flexible records: NoSQL</li><li>Extracted content: JSONL, PostgreSQL or object storage</li><li>Embeddings: vector database</li></ul><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p4-architecture\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Multi-tenant storage", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Keeping each tenant's data separate.", desc: "Keeping each tenant's data separate.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=multi-tenant-storage',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>In a multi-tenant application, each tenant uploads documents independently, so storage must keep their data isolated.</div><div class=\"callout\"><b>Important</b>Prefix-based organization is not a security boundary. Enforce isolation with authorization checks, IAM policies, database access controls and vector-query filters.</div><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p4-architecture\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "SQL vs NoSQL vs object storage", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Which storage fits which data.", desc: "Which storage fits which data.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=sql-nosql-object',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>SQL suits metadata and relationships, NoSQL suits flexible nested records, and object storage suits large original files.</div><ul><li><b>SQL</b>: tables, ACID transactions, joins, constraints</li><li><b>NoSQL</b>: document, key-value and wide-column models; flexible schema</li><li><b>Object storage</b>: large files, durable, cheap, key-based access</li></ul><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p4-sql-nosql\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "PostgreSQL as a starting choice", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "A strong default for RAG metadata.", desc: "A strong default for RAG metadata.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=postgres-default',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>For many RAG projects PostgreSQL is a good default for metadata and relational records.</div><p>It supports transactions, indexes, foreign keys, JSONB, full-text search and vector search through the pgvector extension, so you can delay adding a separate vector database.</p><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p4-sql-nosql\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Vector database", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "A store for embeddings and vector search.", desc: "A store for embeddings and vector search.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=vector-database',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>A vector database stores embeddings and searchable vector records. Examples: Qdrant, Milvus, Weaviate, Pinecone.</div><p>It appears here as part of the architecture. Embeddings and vector storage are covered in later phases.</p><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p4-architecture\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Chunking", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Splitting a document into smaller retrievable pieces.", desc: "Splitting a document into smaller retrievable pieces.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=chunking',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Chunking is the process of dividing a large document into smaller pieces of text called chunks. Chunks are later converted into embeddings and stored in a vector database or other retrieval index.</div><h3>Why it is needed</h3><ul><li>Fits the LLM context window</li><li>Retrieves only the relevant section</li><li>Reduces token consumption</li><li>Makes semantic search more precise</li><li>Easier to manage and update</li></ul><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p5-what\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Chunk", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "A smaller unit of information from a document.", desc: "A smaller unit of information from a document.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=chunk',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>A chunk is a smaller unit of information extracted from a larger document. It generally has a chunk ID, document ID, text content, metadata and position information.</div><ul><li><b>Chunk size</b>: how much content a chunk holds</li><li><b>Chunk overlap</b>: content shared between neighbors</li><li><b>Chunk boundary</b>: where one chunk ends and the next begins</li><li><b>Parent document</b>: the document the chunk came from</li></ul><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p5-what\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Context window", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "How much a model can process in one request.", desc: "How much a model can process in one request.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=context-window',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>The context window defines how much input and output content an LLM can process within a request. Very large documents can exceed it or use too many tokens, which is one reason RAG retrieves chunks instead.</div><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p5-why\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Document-level vs chunk-level processing", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Whole documents versus smaller sections.", desc: "Whole documents versus smaller sections.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=document-vs-chunk-level',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Document-level processing treats the complete document as one unit. Chunk-level processing divides it into smaller units managed separately.</div><ul><li><b>Document-level</b>: simpler, keeps full context, suits classification and summaries</li><li><b>Chunk-level</b>: precise retrieval, suits question answering and semantic search</li></ul><p>Many production systems keep both.</p><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p5-levels\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Fixed-size chunking", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Equal-size pieces by characters, tokens or words.", desc: "Equal-size pieces by characters, tokens or words.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=fixed-size-chunking',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Fixed-size chunking divides text into pieces of about the same size. It is simple, fast and predictable, so it makes a good baseline.</div><p>Limitation: it can split words and sentences and does not understand meaning.</p><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p5-fixed\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Character count vs token count", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Two different ways to measure text size.", desc: "Two different ways to measure text size.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=character-vs-token-count',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Character count is the length of the string. Token count depends on the tokenizer, so the same text can give different token counts and different languages tokenize differently.</div><pre>len(\"Hello RAG\")  # 9 characters</pre><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p5-character\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Token", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "The unit of text a language model processes.", desc: "The unit of text a language model processes.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=token',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>A token may be a complete word, part of a word, punctuation or another character sequence. There is no rule that one word equals one token.</div><h3>Tokenizers</h3><ul><li>tiktoken</li><li>Hugging Face Tokenizers</li><li>SentencePiece</li><li>Transformers AutoTokenizer</li></ul><p>Use the tokenizer that matches your target embedding model.</p><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p5-token\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Token-based chunking", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Splitting by a maximum token count.", desc: "Splitting by a maximum token count.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=token-based-chunking',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Token-based chunking divides text by token count instead of characters, giving better control over embedding input limits, context windows and prompt size.</div><p>Boundaries can still split sentences, and limits may include special tokens or reserved context.</p><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p5-token\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Sentence-based chunking", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Keeping complete sentences together.", desc: "Keeping complete sentences together.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=sentence-based-chunking',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Sentence-based chunking splits on sentence boundaries, then groups a few sentences per chunk. It suits articles, blogs and FAQs.</div><p>Tools: NLTK, spaCy, PySBD. Watch for very long sentences and failures on abbreviations and lists.</p><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p5-sentence\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Paragraph-based chunking", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Splitting on paragraph boundaries.", desc: "Splitting on paragraph boundaries.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=paragraph-based-chunking',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Paragraph-based chunking uses paragraph boundaries so related sentences stay together. Oversized paragraphs are split further into sentences, and tiny neighbors can be combined.</div><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p5-paragraph\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Recursive character chunking", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Trying larger separators before smaller ones.", desc: "Trying larger separators before smaller ones.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=recursive-character-chunking',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Recursive character chunking splits using separators in a preferred order, falling back to a smaller one when a piece is still too big.</div><ol><li>Paragraph: <code>\"\\n\\n\"</code></li><li>Line: <code>\"\\n\"</code></li><li>Sentence: <code>\". \"</code></li><li>Word: <code>\" \"</code></li><li>Character: <code>\"\"</code></li></ol><p>LangChain: <code>RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter</code> with <code>chunk_size</code>, <code>chunk_overlap</code>, <code>separators</code> and <code>length_function</code>.</p><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p5-recursive\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Semantic chunking", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Splitting where the meaning changes.", desc: "Splitting where the meaning changes.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=semantic-chunking',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Semantic chunking divides text based on changes in meaning. Sentences are embedded, neighbors are compared, and a boundary is placed where similarity drops.</div><p>It needs an embedding model, is more expensive, needs threshold tuning and can give uneven chunk sizes.</p><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p5-semantic\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Cosine similarity", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "A similarity score between two vectors.", desc: "A similarity score between two vectors.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=cosine-similarity',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Cosine similarity measures the angle-based similarity between two vectors.</div><pre>Cosine Similarity = (A · B) / (||A|| × ||B||)</pre><p>Near 1 means a similar direction, around 0 little similarity, near -1 opposite. Interpretation depends on the embedding model and normalization.</p><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p5-semantic\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Sliding window chunking", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Overlapping fixed-size windows.", desc: "Overlapping fixed-size windows.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=sliding-window-chunking',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Sliding window chunking starts each new chunk before the previous one ends, so neighboring chunks overlap and keep boundary context.</div><pre>Overlap = Window Size - Stride</pre><p>It duplicates content, which raises storage and embedding cost.</p><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p5-sliding\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Chunk overlap", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Text shared between consecutive chunks.", desc: "Text shared between consecutive chunks.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=chunk-overlap',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Chunk overlap is the amount of text shared between consecutive chunks. It keeps context that spans a boundary.</div><pre>Overlap percentage = (Overlap / Chunk Size) × 100</pre><ul><li>Too little: loses context across boundaries</li><li>Too much: duplicate text, higher cost, redundant results</li></ul><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p5-overlap\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Parent-child chunking", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Search small chunks, return larger sections.", desc: "Search small chunks, return larger sections.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=parent-child-chunking',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Parent-child chunking splits a large section (the parent) into smaller child chunks. Children are embedded and searched; the matching parent is returned to the LLM.</div><p>It combines precise retrieval with fuller context, at the cost of managing parent-child relationships and extra storage.</p><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p5-parent-child\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Chunk size selection", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Choosing how big chunks should be.", desc: "Choosing how big chunks should be.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=chunk-size-selection',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Chunk size defines how much content a chunk holds. Small chunks are focused but may lose context; large chunks keep context but can mix topics.</div><div class=\"callout\"><b>Important</b>There is no universally best chunk size. It depends on your documents, queries, embedding model, retrieval strategy and LLM. Test several sizes with the same questions.</div><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p5-size\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Chunk metadata", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Information describing an individual chunk.", desc: "Information describing an individual chunk.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=chunk-metadata',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Chunk metadata says where a chunk came from, which document and version it belongs to, its position, section and tenant or access scope.</div><p>Common fields: chunk_id, document_id, version_id, tenant_id, source, page_number, section, chunk_index, content_hash, created_at.</p><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p5-metadata\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Metadata inheritance", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Chunks copy relevant metadata from their document.", desc: "Chunks copy relevant metadata from their document.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=metadata-inheritance',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Chunks inherit relevant metadata such as tenant ID and source from their parent document, and add chunk-specific fields like page number and section title.</div><p>Tenant IDs and permissions must be assigned and validated by your application, not trusted from the uploaded file.</p><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p5-metadata\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Chunk ID generation", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Giving every chunk a unique identifier.", desc: "Giving every chunk a unique identifier.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=chunk-id-generation',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>A chunk ID uniquely identifies a chunk so you can retrieve, update and delete it and link embeddings to it.</div><ul><li><b>UUID4</b>: random</li><li><b>Sequential</b>: <code>doc_001_chunk_0001</code></li><li><b>Deterministic (UUID5)</b>: same input, same ID</li></ul><p>A robust identity can include document ID, version ID, chunking strategy version and position or content hash. A chunk ID and an embedding ID are conceptually different records.</p><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p5-chunk-id\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Chunk quality evaluation", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Checking whether chunks suit retrieval.", desc: "Checking whether chunks suit retrieval.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=chunk-quality-evaluation',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Chunk quality evaluation checks whether generated chunks are suitable for semantic retrieval and question answering. A successful split does not mean the chunks are good.</div><h3>Dimensions</h3><ul><li>Completeness</li><li>Coherence</li><li>Context preservation</li><li>Boundary quality</li><li>Size consistency</li><li>Redundancy</li><li>Metadata accuracy</li></ul><h3>Automatic checks</h3><ul><li>Empty chunks</li><li>Duplicate chunks</li><li>Extremely short chunks</li><li>Chunks exceeding a token limit</li></ul><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p5-quality\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Tokenization", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Converting text into tokens and token IDs.", desc: "Converting text into tokens and token IDs.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=tokenization',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Tokenization converts text into tokens and then maps them to numerical token IDs before a model processes them.</div><pre>Input text → Normalization → Pre-tokenization → Subword tokenization → Token ID mapping</pre><p>Tokenization turns text into IDs. Embedding turns text or tokens into vectors. They are different operations.</p><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p6-how\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Token ID", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "The number a tokenizer assigns to a token.", desc: "The number a tokenizer assigns to a token.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=token-id',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>A token ID is a numerical identifier assigned to a token in a tokenizer's vocabulary. Models use IDs to look up token embeddings and to turn generated IDs back into text.</div><p>The actual IDs depend on the tokenizer.</p><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p6-ids\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Tokenizer vocabulary", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "The set of tokens a tokenizer supports.", desc: "The set of tokens a tokenizer supports.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=tokenizer-vocabulary',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>A vocabulary is the collection of tokens a tokenizer supports: common words, subword units, punctuation, whitespace-related tokens and special tokens.</div><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p6-ids\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Special tokens", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Tokens that mark structure rather than text.", desc: "Tokens that mark structure rather than text.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=special-tokens',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Special tokens represent structural information such as beginning or end of sequence, unknown, padding, separator, and chat boundary markers. Names and behavior vary by model.</div><p><code>encode()</code> may add them automatically. Use <code>add_special_tokens=False</code> to inspect IDs without them, and count them when checking real model input.</p><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p6-ids\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "BPE (Byte Pair Encoding)", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "A subword method that merges frequent adjacent pairs.", desc: "A subword method that merges frequent adjacent pairs.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=bpe',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>BPE builds a vocabulary by repeatedly merging frequently occurring adjacent units, so common pieces become single tokens.</div><pre>l o w  →  lo w  →  low</pre><p>It handles uncommon words with smaller units, but token boundaries may not match linguistic ones and different vocabularies give different counts.</p><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p6-bpe\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "WordPiece", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "The subword method used by BERT-family models.", desc: "The subword method used by BERT-family models.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=wordpiece',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>WordPiece represents words using reusable subword pieces. A <code>##</code> prefix marks a piece that continues the previous one.</div><pre>playing → [\"play\", \"##ing\"]</pre><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p6-wordpiece\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "SentencePiece", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "A tokenizer framework that works on raw text.", desc: "A tokenizer framework that works on raw text.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=sentencepiece',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>SentencePiece supports BPE and the Unigram Language Model and tokenizes raw text directly, without whitespace-based word splitting. It suits multilingual text.</div><p>The <code>▁</code> marker commonly stands for a whitespace boundary. You need a trained model file to use it.</p><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p6-sentencepiece\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Model-specific tokenizers", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Different models tokenize the same text differently.", desc: "Different models tokenize the same text differently.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=model-specific-tokenizers',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>BERT uses WordPiece-style segmentation, GPT-family models use their own encodings, T5 commonly uses SentencePiece, and Llama-family tokenizers depend on model configuration.</div><div class=\"callout\"><b>Golden rule</b>Always count tokens using the tokenizer associated with the model that will receive the text.</div><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p6-model-specific\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Counting tokens", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Measuring text with the right tokenizer.", desc: "Measuring text with the right tokenizer.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=counting-tokens',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Count tokens with the tokenizer that matches the target model.</div><ul><li><b>tiktoken</b>: OpenAI-compatible encodings such as <code>cl100k_base</code></li><li><b>AutoTokenizer</b>: Hugging Face models</li><li><b>model.tokenizer</b>: Sentence Transformers</li><li><b>Unknown model</b>: the official tokenizer or provider token-count API</li></ul><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p6-counting\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Embedding model token limits", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "The maximum input an embedding model accepts.", desc: "The maximum input an embedding model accepts.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=embedding-token-limits',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Embedding models have a maximum input size in tokens. Input beyond it may be truncated silently or rejected, and silent truncation loses information.</div><h3>Check</h3><ul><li>Maximum input tokens</li><li>Vector dimensions</li><li>Supported languages</li><li>Whether truncation is automatic</li><li>Whether special tokens count</li><li>Batch and request size limits</li></ul><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p6-embedding-limits\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "LLM context budget", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Dividing the context window between inputs and output.", desc: "Dividing the context window between inputs and output.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=llm-context-budget',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>The context window must hold instructions, history, the question, retrieved chunks, tool overhead and the output. Maximum output tokens can be smaller than the window.</div><p>If retrieval returns more than fits, reduce chunk count or size, rerank, remove redundancy, or compress the context.</p><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p6-llm-limits\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Token cost estimation", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Turning token counts into cost.", desc: "Turning token counts into cost.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=token-cost',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Many APIs charge per million tokens, with separate rates for input and output.</div><pre>10,000 / 1,000,000 × $2 = $0.02 (input)\n 2,000 / 1,000,000 × $8 = $0.016 (output)</pre><p>These rates are hypothetical. Track embedding and generation usage separately.</p><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p6-cost\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Boundary-aware token chunking", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Token budgets that respect paragraphs and sentences.", desc: "Token budgets that respect paragraphs and sentences.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=boundary-aware-chunking',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>A production token chunker splits by paragraph, splits oversized paragraphs into sentences, groups sentences within the token budget, splits a sentence only when needed, applies overlap carefully, and recounts every final chunk.</div><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p6-token-chunking\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Handling long documents", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Processing very large documents reliably.", desc: "Processing very large documents reliably.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=long-document-handling',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Long documents are split into chunks, validated, embedded in batches, and tracked with progress, retries and incremental updates.</div><ul><li>Retry with exponential backoff and rate-limit handling</li><li>Track failed chunks and keep processing idempotent</li><li>Use content hashes to avoid reprocessing unchanged text</li></ul><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p6-long-docs\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Retrieval evaluation metrics", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Measuring which chunk size works best.", desc: "Measuring which chunk size works best.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=retrieval-metrics',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Chunk settings are compared with retrieval metrics on the same documents and questions.</div><ul><li>Recall@K</li><li>Precision@K</li><li>Mean Reciprocal Rank (MRR)</li><li>Normalized Discounted Cumulative Gain (NDCG)</li><li>Answer correctness, latency and cost per query</li></ul><p>Start from a baseline like 512 tokens with 75 overlap, then test others. The model's maximum input is a hard limit, not the best size.</p><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p6-chunk-size\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Embedding", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "A numerical representation of data in a vector space.", desc: "A numerical representation of data in a vector space.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=embedding',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>An embedding is a numerical representation of data in a vector space. An embedding model converts text into a list of floating-point numbers.</div><p>Real models produce hundreds or thousands of dimensions. The values are learned features and are not individually interpretable. Meaning is spread across all dimensions.</p><p>Uses: semantic search, RAG retrieval, document similarity, recommendations, duplicate detection, clustering, classification.</p><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p7-1\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Vector", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "An ordered list of numbers.", desc: "An ordered list of numbers.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=vector',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>A vector is an ordered collection of numerical values. A text embedding is a vector with many more dimensions than a simple example like [0.2, 0.5, 0.8].</div><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p7-1\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "How text becomes a vector", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "From text to one fixed-size vector.", desc: "From text to one fixed-size vector.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=embedding-pipeline',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Text is tokenized into token IDs, mapped to token embeddings, processed by transformer layers into contextual representations, then pooled and projected into one embedding.</div><p>A token embedding is not the same as a sentence embedding. Token embeddings are per token, sentence embeddings represent a whole passage.</p><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p7-3\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Pooling", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Combining token vectors into one vector.", desc: "Combining token vectors into one vector.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=pooling',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Pooling converts the token-level representations into one fixed-size vector. Common methods are mean pooling, CLS-token pooling and max pooling.</div><pre>E = (1/N) Σ h_i   (mean pooling, padding excluded by the attention mask)</pre><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p7-4\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "L2 normalization", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Scaling a vector to unit length.", desc: "Scaling a vector to unit length.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=l2-normalization',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>L2 normalization divides a vector by its length so it has length 1. Follow the model's instructions on whether to normalize.</div><pre>Ê = E / ‖E‖₂</pre><p>For normalized vectors, dot product equals cosine similarity.</p><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p7-4\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Embedding dimensions", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "The number of values in each vector.", desc: "The number of values in each vector.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=embedding-dimensions',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Dimensions set how many numbers represent each text, such as 384, 768, 1,024, 1,536 or 3,072. They affect storage, memory, index size, speed and compatibility.</div><p>More dimensions do not always mean better retrieval. Test on your own data. Some models support reduced dimensions, which must be validated.</p><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p7-6\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Vector storage calculation", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Estimating raw vector size.", desc: "Estimating raw vector size.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=vector-storage-calculation',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Raw storage equals the number of vectors × dimensions × bytes per value.</div><pre>1,000,000 × 768 × 4 bytes = 3,072,000,000 bytes ≈ 3.07 GB</pre><p>This excludes metadata, indexes, replicas and overhead.</p><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p7-16\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Dense vs sparse embeddings", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Semantic vectors versus term-based features.", desc: "Semantic vectors versus term-based features.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=dense-vs-sparse',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Dense embeddings are mostly non-zero and capture meaning. Sparse representations have mostly zero values and capture terms and lexical features.</div><ul><li><b>Dense</b>: BGE, E5; strong on synonyms</li><li><b>Sparse</b>: TF-IDF, BM25, SPLADE; strong on exact keywords</li></ul><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p7-7\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Hybrid search", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Combining dense and sparse retrieval.", desc: "Combining dense and sparse retrieval.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=hybrid-search',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Hybrid search runs dense (semantic) and sparse (keyword) search, then fuses the results. It helps when a question has both meaning and exact terms such as product codes or error messages.</div><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p7-7\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Document vs query embeddings", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Chunks and questions are embedded at different times.", desc: "Chunks and questions are embedded at different times.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=document-vs-query-embeddings',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Document embeddings are generated during ingestion and stored. Query embeddings are generated when a user asks, usually per query, and compared with the stored vectors.</div><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p7-8\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Asymmetric retrieval and prefixes", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Models that treat queries and documents differently.", desc: "Models that treat queries and documents differently.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=asymmetric-retrieval',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Some models are trained so queries and documents play different roles and need different prefixes, such as <code>query:</code> and <code>passage:</code> for E5 or <code>search_query:</code> and <code>search_document:</code> for Nomic.</div><p>Never assume every model needs prefixes. Follow the model card.</p><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p7-8\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Dot product", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Multiply matching values and sum them.", desc: "Multiply matching values and sum them.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=dot-product',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>The dot product of two vectors is the sum of their element-wise products. It reflects direction and magnitude.</div><pre>[1,2,3] · [4,5,6] = 4 + 10 + 18 = 32</pre><p>For L2-normalized vectors it equals cosine similarity. Do not assume the same ranking as cosine for unnormalized vectors.</p><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p7-11\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Euclidean distance", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Straight-line distance between vectors.", desc: "Straight-line distance between vectors.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=euclidean-distance',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Euclidean distance is the straight-line distance between two points. Smaller means closer.</div><pre>d(A,B) = √(Σ (A_i − B_i)²)</pre><p>[1,2,3] to [4,5,6] is √27 ≈ 5.196.</p><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p7-12\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Query and document compatibility", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Use one model for both sides.", desc: "Use one model for both sides.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=query-document-compatibility',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Vectors from different embedding models are not comparable, even if they have the same number of dimensions.</div><div class=\"callout\"><b>Golden rule</b>Use the same embedding model and compatible configuration for document and query embeddings.</div><p>If you change models, re-embed all chunks, rebuild the index, update the query pipeline, validate retrieval, and deploy them together.</p><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p7-20\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Selecting an embedding model", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Choose by testing on your own data.", desc: "Choose by testing on your own data.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=embedding-model-selection',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Evaluate retrieval accuracy, dimensions, maximum input length, language support, query/document compatibility, size, latency, hardware, price, licensing and benchmarks.</div><p>Process: define the use case, identify data types, prepare an evaluation dataset, shortlist models, embed, run retrieval evaluation, measure latency and cost, compare, select. Do not choose by popularity, dimensions, size, benchmark rank or price alone.</p><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p7-13\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Open-weight vs API embedding models", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Self-hosted versus provider-hosted.", desc: "Self-hosted versus provider-hosted.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=open-weight-vs-api',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Open-weight models (BGE, E5, Nomic, GTE, Sentence Transformers) can run locally with more control. API models (OpenAI, Cohere) are managed but add cost, latency and provider dependency.</div><p>Open-weight does not mean unrestricted commercial use. Check the license.</p><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p7-14\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Multilingual embedding models", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "One space for several languages.", desc: "One space for several languages.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=multilingual-embeddings',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Multilingual models represent text across languages, so a Telugu query can retrieve an English document. Support is not equally strong for every language, so test with real queries in each language.</div><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p7-18\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Domain-specific embedding models", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Models tuned for specialized text.", desc: "Models tuned for specialized text.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=domain-specific-embeddings',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Healthcare, finance, legal, software and scientific text have terminology general models may not represent best. A domain model is worth using only if it beats general models on your test set.</div><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p7-19\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Precision@K, Recall@K, MRR and NDCG", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Metrics for retrieval quality.", desc: "Metrics for retrieval quality.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=retrieval-accuracy-metrics',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Evaluate embeddings by their retrieval results.</div><pre>Precision@K = relevant retrieved / K\nRecall@K    = relevant retrieved / total relevant\nRR          = 1 / rank of first relevant result</pre><p>NDCG measures ranking quality with graded relevance. Retrieval accuracy is not the same as answer accuracy, so also measure faithfulness, answer relevance and context relevance.</p><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p7-22\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Embedding latency and cost", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Speed and spend of generating embeddings.", desc: "Speed and spend of generating embeddings.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=embedding-latency-cost',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Measure latency (p50, p95), throughput (embeddings per second), memory and cost per 1,000 queries. Local models avoid API fees but use CPU, GPU, RAM and hosting.</div><p>A slower model with better recall may suit a research assistant, while a high-volume real-time app may need a faster one.</p><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p7-23\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Local vs API embedding generation", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Where embeddings are computed.", desc: "Where embeddings are computed.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=local-vs-api-embeddings',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Local generation (for example Sentence Transformers) gives infrastructure control. API generation (for example OpenAI) is simple and managed. A common embedding interface lets you support both.</div><p>Keep API keys in environment variables, never in code.</p><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p7-24\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Popular embedding models", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Families worth studying.", desc: "Families worth studying.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=popular-embedding-models',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>BGE (BAAI), E5, Nomic Embed, GTE, OpenAI text-embedding-3, and Cohere Embed are widely studied. Versions, dimensions, token limits and licenses vary.</div><p>Always read the model card for prefixes, input types and limits.</p><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p7-25\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Sentence Transformers", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "The Python framework for local embeddings.", desc: "The Python framework for local embeddings.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=sentence-transformers',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Sentence Transformers loads pretrained embedding models and provides <code>encode()</code>, <code>normalize_embeddings</code>, similarity helpers, cross-encoder rerankers and evaluation tools.</div><pre>model = SentenceTransformer(\"sentence-transformers/all-MiniLM-L6-v2\")\nembeddings = model.encode(sentences, normalize_embeddings=True)</pre><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p7-29\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Embedding model interface", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "One interface for many providers.", desc: "One interface for many providers.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=embedding-engine-abstraction',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>A base class with <code>embed_documents</code>, <code>embed_query</code> and <code>get_dimensions</code> lets you swap Sentence Transformers, OpenAI, Cohere, BGE or E5 without rewriting the pipeline. Store model name, version, dimensions, similarity metric and whether vectors are normalized.</div><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p7-32\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Vector index", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "A structure for fast nearest-neighbor search.", desc: "A structure for fast nearest-neighbor search.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=vector-index',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>A vector index helps find likely nearest neighbors without comparing the query to every vector. Types include flat, HNSW, IVF and Product Quantization.</div><p>Trade-offs: search latency, recall, memory, build time and update behavior.</p><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p8-indexing\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Vector record", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "What a vector database stores per chunk.", desc: "What a vector database stores per chunk.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=vector-record',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>A record holds a unique ID, the vector, the chunk text and metadata. The vector alone cannot give back the original text, because an embedding is not a lossless encoding.</div><p>The LLM needs the original text, so store it with the vector or look it up by chunk ID.</p><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p8-what\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Vector metadata", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Fields stored beside a vector.", desc: "Fields stored beside a vector.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=vector-metadata',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Metadata such as document ID, chunk ID, source, page, category, author, tenant and version supports filtering and citation. Keep it small, use stable document IDs and unique chunk IDs.</div><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p8-metadata\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Document-to-chunk relationship", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "One document, many chunks.", desc: "One document, many chunks.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=document-chunk-relationship',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Each chunk keeps its document ID. This lets you find the source, neighboring chunks and parent sections, track versions, and delete every chunk of a document.</div><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p8-relationships\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Vector dimension validation", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Rejecting vectors of the wrong size.", desc: "Rejecting vectors of the wrong size.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=vector-dimension-validation',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Every vector in a collection must match its dimension count. Check lengths before insert. Matching dimensions do not prove compatible models, so store model identity with the index.</div><pre>if len(vector) != expected_dimensions:\n    raise ValueError(...)</pre><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p8-dimensions\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Exact nearest neighbor search", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Brute-force comparison with every vector.", desc: "Brute-force comparison with every vector.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=exact-nearest-neighbor',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Exact (flat or brute-force) search compares the query to every vector and returns the true nearest ones. It is simple and a good baseline, but its cost grows with the dataset.</div><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p8-exact\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Approximate nearest neighbor (ANN) search", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Fast search that may miss some neighbors.", desc: "Fast search that may miss some neighbors.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=approximate-nearest-neighbor',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>ANN search explores a smaller candidate set through an index, trading a little accuracy for lower latency and better scale.</div><p>Evaluate it against exact search with recall: if ANN finds 6 of the exact top 10, Recall@10 = 0.6.</p><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p8-ann\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "HNSW (Hierarchical Navigable Small World)", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "A layered graph index.", desc: "A layered graph index.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=hnsw',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>HNSW stores vectors as nodes in a multi-layer graph. Search starts in a sparse upper layer and moves down through more detailed connections.</div><ul><li><b>M</b>: connections per node (more memory)</li><li><b>ef_construction</b>: build quality vs build time</li><li><b>ef_search</b>: recall vs search speed</li></ul><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p8-hnsw\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "IVF (Inverted File Index)", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "A cluster-based index.", desc: "A cluster-based index.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=ivf',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>IVF clusters vectors, finds the nearest cluster centroids for a query, and searches only inside those clusters.</div><ul><li><b>nlist</b>: number of clusters</li><li><b>nprobe</b>: clusters searched (more means higher recall and more work)</li></ul><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p8-ivf\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Choosing a distance metric", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Match the metric to the embedding model.", desc: "Match the metric to the embedding model.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=distance-metric-selection',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Cosine similarity and dot product score higher for closer vectors, and Euclidean distance scores lower. Follow the model's recommended metric and normalization rather than choosing one at random.</div><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p8-metrics\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Metadata filtering", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Narrowing search by record fields.", desc: "Narrowing search by record fields.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=metadata-filtering',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Metadata filters restrict search to records matching conditions such as source, date, category, tenant, permissions or version.</div><div class=\"callout\"><b>Important</b>For multi-tenant systems, enforce tenant and access filters on the server side. Never let the LLM decide who can retrieve a document.</div><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p8-filtering\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Vector insert, update and delete", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Keeping the index current.", desc: "Keeping the index current.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=vector-crud',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Use add, update and delete to keep vectors in step with documents. When a PDF changes, re-extract, re-chunk, re-embed, replace the old chunk records, update metadata and verify retrieval.</div><p>Use versioning or an atomic index-switch when consistency matters.</p><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p8-crud\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Index persistence", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Keeping vectors after the app stops.", desc: "Keeping vectors after the app stops.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=index-persistence',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Persistence retains vectors and indexes across restarts, for example with <code>chromadb.PersistentClient</code>. It is not a backup strategy, so you still need backups, monitoring and recovery.</div><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p8-persistence\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Choosing a vector database", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Options and when to use them.", desc: "Options and when to use them.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=vector-database-selection',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>FAISS is a search library for experiments. ChromaDB suits learning and prototypes. Qdrant suits production retrieval and filtering. Pinecone is a managed service. Weaviate suits hybrid search. Milvus suits large scale. pgvector adds vectors to PostgreSQL.</div><p>Suggested learning order: NumPy, FAISS, ChromaDB, Qdrant, pgvector.</p><p>Learn it in context: <a href=\"topic.html?g=Learnings&amp;t=rag#p8-selection\">RAG topic</a>.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "RAG evaluation harness", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "A repeatable environment for testing and comparing RAG systems.", desc: "A repeatable environment for testing and comparing RAG systems.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=evaluation-harness',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>An evaluation harness is a repeatable setup that runs your RAG system against test cases and compares results across configurations.</div><h3>What it should compare</h3><ul><li>Embedding models</li><li>Chunking strategies</li><li>Vector databases</li><li>Retrieval techniques</li><li>Rerankers</li><li>LLMs</li><li>Prompt versions</li></ul><p>Output: a report covering accuracy, retrieval quality, latency, token usage and cost. Building this is a stronger project than another PDF chatbot.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Golden and synthetic evaluation datasets", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "The test data a harness runs on.", desc: "The test data a harness runs on.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=evaluation-datasets',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>A golden dataset holds questions with ground-truth answers and expected context. Synthetic datasets are generated to scale testing.</div><ul><li>Golden dataset creation</li><li>Question-answer dataset preparation</li><li>Ground truth generation</li><li>Expected context preparation</li><li>Synthetic test dataset generation</li><li>Test case management</li></ul>" } ] },
    { title: "Offline vs online evaluation", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Testing before release versus testing live traffic.", desc: "Testing before release versus testing live traffic.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=offline-vs-online-evaluation',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Offline evaluation runs fixed test datasets, for example in a harness or regression suite. Online evaluation checks real production queries and user feedback, which supports continuous evaluation.</div>" } ] },
    { title: "RAG evaluation and tracing tools", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Frameworks to study.", desc: "Frameworks to study.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=evaluation-tools',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Tools for evaluating and tracing RAG systems.</div><ul><li><b>Ragas and DeepEval</b>: metric implementation: context precision, context recall, faithfulness, answer relevancy</li><li><b>TruLens</b>: evaluation</li><li><b>Phoenix / Arize Phoenix</b>: evaluation and tracing</li><li><b>LangSmith</b>: evaluation and tracing</li><li><b>Langfuse</b>: tracing</li><li><b>MLflow / MLflow Tracing</b>: experiment tracking and tracing</li><li><b>OpenTelemetry</b>: tracing standard</li></ul>" } ] },
    { title: "Hit Rate@K and MAP", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Two more retrieval metrics.", desc: "Two more retrieval metrics.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=hit-rate-and-map',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Hit Rate@K asks whether at least one relevant chunk was retrieved in the top K. MAP (Mean Average Precision) averages precision across ranked results.</div>" } ] },
    { title: "Context precision and context recall", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Quality and coverage of retrieved context.", desc: "Quality and coverage of retrieved context.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=context-precision-recall',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Context precision measures how relevant the retrieved context is. Context recall measures whether it covers the information required to answer.</div>" } ] },
    { title: "Generation metrics", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Measuring the quality of the answer.", desc: "Measuring the quality of the answer.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=generation-metrics',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Generation metrics judge the answer against the context and the expected answer.</div><ul><li><b>Faithfulness</b>: whether the answer is supported by the context</li><li><b>Answer relevancy</b>: whether the answer addresses the question</li><li><b>Answer correctness</b>: whether the answer matches the expected answer</li><li><b>Groundedness</b>: whether claims are supported by evidence</li><li><b>Hallucination rate</b>: frequency of unsupported claims</li><li><b>Completeness</b>: whether the answer covers the required information</li><li><b>Citation precision</b>: whether cited sources support the claims</li><li><b>Citation recall</b>: whether relevant supporting sources are cited</li></ul>" } ] },
    { title: "System performance metrics", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Speed, cost and capacity of a RAG system.", desc: "Speed, cost and capacity of a RAG system.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=system-performance-metrics',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Measure each stage and the whole request.</div><ul><li>End-to-end, query processing, embedding, vector search, reranking and LLM generation latency</li><li>Time to first token (TTFT) and tokens per second</li><li>Input and output token consumption</li><li>Cost per query</li><li>Throughput (queries per second) and concurrent request capacity</li><li>Error rate, retrieval failure rate and cache hit rate</li></ul>" } ] },
    { title: "Ingestion metrics", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Measuring the document pipeline.", desc: "Measuring the document pipeline.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=ingestion-metrics',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Ingestion metrics show whether data enters the system reliably and efficiently.</div><ul><li>Documents processed and failed</li><li>Average processing time per document</li><li>Average chunks per document and average chunk token count</li><li>Embedding generation time and failure rate</li><li>Indexing time</li><li>Duplicate document rate</li><li>Re-indexing frequency</li></ul>" } ] },
    { title: "RAG metrics checklist", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "What to implement first in a harness.", desc: "What to implement first in a harness.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=rag-metrics-checklist',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>A starting set of metrics by category.</div><ul><li><b>Retrieval</b>: Recall@K, Precision@K, MRR, NDCG</li><li><b>Context</b>: Context precision, context recall</li><li><b>Generation</b>: Faithfulness, answer relevancy</li><li><b>Accuracy</b>: Exact match, F1, correctness</li><li><b>Citations</b>: Citation precision, citation recall</li><li><b>Performance</b>: P50, P95, P99 latency, TTFT</li><li><b>Cost</b>: Cost per query, tokens per query</li><li><b>Reliability</b>: Error rate, timeout rate</li><li><b>Security</b>: Leakage rate, unauthorized retrieval rate</li></ul>" } ] },
    { title: "RAG benchmarking and datasets", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Comparing against standard datasets.", desc: "Comparing against standard datasets.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=rag-benchmarking',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Benchmarking compares your RAG architecture against standard datasets and other retrieval systems, with reproducible runs, baselines, confidence intervals and significance tests.</div><h3>Datasets to explore</h3><ul><li>BEIR</li><li>MTEB</li><li>MS MARCO</li><li>HotpotQA</li><li>MuSiQue</li><li>RAGBench</li><li>FinanceBench</li></ul><h3>Benchmark types</h3><ul><li>Open-domain QA</li><li>Multi-hop QA</li><li>Domain-specific</li><li>Long-context retrieval</li><li>Multilingual retrieval</li><li>Noisy documents</li><li>Temporal QA</li></ul>" } ] },
    { title: "RAG observability and tracing", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Seeing what happens inside every request.", desc: "Seeing what happens inside every request.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=rag-observability',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Observability traces a request through ingestion, embedding, retrieval, reranking, prompt and LLM call, using traces and spans.</div><h3>What to inspect</h3><ul><li>Retrieved chunks and the final context</li><li>Token consumption and cost attribution</li><li>Latency breakdown per stage</li><li>Errors and user feedback</li></ul><p>Tools: Arize Phoenix, LangSmith, Langfuse, OpenTelemetry, MLflow Tracing.</p>" } ] },
    { title: "Agentic RAG", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Agents decide when and what to retrieve.", desc: "Agents decide when and what to retrieve.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=agentic-rag',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>In agentic RAG, an agent decides when to search, what to retrieve, whether to search again, and when to answer.</div>" } ] },
    { title: "GraphRAG", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Retrieval over entities and relationships.", desc: "Retrieval over entities and relationships.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=graph-rag',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>GraphRAG uses entities and relationships to answer complex questions that need connections across documents.</div>" } ] },
    { title: "Multimodal RAG", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Retrieval across text, images, tables and more.", desc: "Retrieval across text, images, tables and more.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=multimodal-rag',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Multimodal RAG retrieves information from text, images, tables, charts, audio and other modalities.</div>" } ] },
    { title: "Adaptive RAG", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Choosing a retrieval strategy per question.", desc: "Choosing a retrieval strategy per question.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=adaptive-rag',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Adaptive RAG dynamically selects a retrieval strategy based on the question and its complexity.</div>" } ] },
    { title: "Continuous RAG evaluation", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Evaluating production queries over time.", desc: "Evaluating production queries over time.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=continuous-rag-evaluation',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Continuous evaluation scores real production queries, detects quality degradation and finds retrieval failures. Research directions also include temporal freshness, retrieval fusion, structured memory, security and cost-aware retrieval.</div>" } ] },
    { title: "Advanced RAG architectures", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "A map of modern techniques.", desc: "A map of modern techniques.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=advanced-rag-architectures',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Beyond naive RAG there are many variants to study.</div><ul><li>Naive, advanced, modular and hybrid RAG</li><li>GraphRAG, agentic, adaptive RAG</li><li>Corrective RAG (CRAG) and Self-RAG</li><li>LightRAG and RAPTOR (Recursive Abstractive Processing for Tree-Organized Retrieval)</li><li>Multi-vector, hierarchical, multi-hop and multi-modal RAG</li><li>Table-based and knowledge graph-based RAG</li><li>Long-context, memory-augmented, temporal and structure-aware RAG</li><li>Reasoning-driven retrieval, retrieval-augmented agents, Search-R1</li></ul>" } ] },
    { title: "RAG reliability and security testing", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Making RAG trustworthy for enterprises.", desc: "Making RAG trustworthy for enterprises.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=rag-reliability-security',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Test robustness and safety before and after release.</div><ul><li>Adversarial queries and prompt injection</li><li>Document poisoning</li><li>Data leakage and unauthorized retrieval</li><li>Access control evaluation</li><li>Citation verification and source provenance</li><li>Answer abstention and uncertainty estimation</li><li>Out-of-distribution queries and stale information</li><li>PII leakage prevention, auditability and red team testing</li></ul>" } ] },
    { title: "RAG experimentation", tag: 'RAG', blurb: "Making evidence-based design decisions.", desc: "Making evidence-based design decisions.",
      href: 'topic.html?g=Concepts&t=rag-experimentation',
      sections: [ { id: 'overview', title: 'Overview', html: "<div class=\"callout\"><b>Definition</b>Run controlled experiments instead of guessing.</div><ul><li>Ablation studies for architecture, chunking, embeddings, retrievers, rerankers and prompts</li><li>LLM-as-a-judge reliability and human vs AI evaluation</li><li>Evaluation dataset bias</li><li>Retrieval failure analysis and error categorization</li><li>Cost vs accuracy and latency vs quality trade-offs</li><li>Reproducible experiments</li></ul>" } ] },
  ] },
];
